[Event "Chapter 1: The 3. Nc3 Classical Variation / Study 1: White Plays 6.Bd2, 6. Be2, or 6.Bd3 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/63670ca5-0fcf-4d90-8963-ca234d2f1bee/studies/a877111e-eb4c-43e7-9976-5d63aee35701/lines"]

1.e4 {You have come to the right place if you want to have a solid and unexpected defense against 1.e4! The Scandinavian Defense is a polarizing opening: some are hesitant to play it, but it holds many secrets and ways to get a solid position. We determine the structure and style of play, and we can catch opponents off-guard who have been quick to dismiss it.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White captures. This is the most popular move you'll see. Our queen will recapture and get out early, which seems to break some opening principles, but it's okay, even desired, in the Scandinavian.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nc3 {This move is seen 77% of times, in around 57 million games. It is one of the most popular variations because it allows White to develop with a tempo. However, it may make White overconfident in their opening. In reality, we are in control. Our idea is to keep the queen active: 3...Qa5.} 3...Qa5 4.d4 {d4 and Nf3 are White's most popular ideas, and this is the classical line. It follows traditional development with a pawn in the center. Our plan has gone into effect, and at this point, we'll develop our kingside knight.} 4...Nf6 5.Nf3 {White brings their knight out as well. This occurs 37% of the time, which equals about 745,000 games in the database. Before pushing ...e6, let's get our light-squared bishop out so it's not stuck later.} 5...Bf5 6.Bd2 {6. Bd2 is the most popular variation, being played 102,000 times in the database. White thinks they're threatening some discovered attacks. However, these aren't anything special.} 6...e6 7.Ne5 {You may also face this move. The e5-square is often used by White in the Scandinavian. You should target the weakness left after Ne5: the d4-pawn. But the b2-pawn is also unprotected, so that it will be targeted as well.} 7...Qb6 8.Qf3 {This is the best move for White. It's a little hard to find, but the idea is to pressure the b7-pawn. We should just defend our b7-pawn.} 8...c6 9.O-O-O {White can castle. We can develop our knight, challenging the e5-knight. We cannot take on d4. If 9...Qxd4, 10. Bf4 and our queen is under attack, the rook has an open d-file and g4 would be on the board next.} 9...Nbd7 10.Nc4 {This was White's idea with Ne5 all along: to go to c4 and attack the queen. But we have the d8-square.} 10...Qd8 11.h3 {White prevents the ...Bg4 skewer.} 11...Be7 {And we prepare our standard 0-0 plan. ...Nb6 is a good idea: we propose a trade which would be great for us after Nxb6 and ...axb6, with an open file towards the king. Of course, White won't do that if they're skilled, but ...Nb6 is useful to prepare ...Nfd5. If one knight is taken on d5, the other one takes its place.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 3. Nc3 Classical Variation / Study 1: White Plays 6.Bd2, 6. Be2, or 6.Bd3 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/63670ca5-0fcf-4d90-8963-ca234d2f1bee/studies/a877111e-eb4c-43e7-9976-5d63aee35701/lines"]

1.e4 {You have come to the right place if you want to have a solid and unexpected defense against 1.e4! The Scandinavian Defense is a polarizing opening: some are hesitant to play it, but it holds many secrets and ways to get a solid position. We determine the structure and style of play, and we can catch opponents off-guard who have been quick to dismiss it.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White captures. This is the most popular move you'll see. Our queen will recapture and get out early, which seems to break some opening principles, but it's okay, even desired, in the Scandinavian.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nc3 {This move is seen 77% of times, in around 57 million games. It is one of the most popular variations because it allows White to develop with a tempo. However, it may make White overconfident in their opening. In reality, we are in control. Our idea is to keep the queen active: 3...Qa5.} 3...Qa5 4.d4 {d4 and Nf3 are White's most popular ideas, and this is the classical line. It follows traditional development with a pawn in the center. Our plan has gone into effect, and at this point, we'll develop our kingside knight.} 4...Nf6 5.Nf3 {White brings their knight out as well. This occurs 37% of the time, which equals about 745,000 games in the database. Before pushing ...e6, let's get our light-squared bishop out so it's not stuck later.} 5...Bf5 6.Bd3 {White could be tempted to propose a bishop trade. Their idea is that your bishop on f5 is too strong, so removing it is a good idea. 6. Bd3 has been played in 26% of games in the database, which is equal to 54,000 games.} 6...Bxd3 7.Qxd3 {The queen recaptures. It's now important to control the d5-square while giving our dark-squared bishop some breathing room.} 7...e6 8.O-O {White castles. Before ...Nbd7, 8...Be7 makes sense because our king needs to get to safety quickly.} 8...Be7 9.Bd2 {White may get their bishop on the same diagonal as your queen.} 9...O-O {But there's nothing scary about the bishop here. We're going to go ...Nbd7, c6 and bring the queen back if needed. We have a solid position and we're going to get a rook on the d-file.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 3. Nc3 Classical Variation / Study 1: White Plays 6.Bd2, 6. Be2, or 6.Bd3 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/63670ca5-0fcf-4d90-8963-ca234d2f1bee/studies/a877111e-eb4c-43e7-9976-5d63aee35701/lines"]

1.e4 {You have come to the right place if you want to have a solid and unexpected defense against 1.e4! The Scandinavian Defense is a polarizing opening: some are hesitant to play it, but it holds many secrets and ways to get a solid position. We determine the structure and style of play, and we can catch opponents off-guard who have been quick to dismiss it.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White captures. This is the most popular move you'll see. Our queen will recapture and get out early, which seems to break some opening principles, but it's okay, even desired, in the Scandinavian.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nc3 {This move is seen 77% of times, in around 57 million games. It is one of the most popular variations because it allows White to develop with a tempo. However, it may make White overconfident in their opening. In reality, we are in control. Our idea is to keep the queen active: 3...Qa5.} 3...Qa5 4.d4 {d4 and Nf3 are White's most popular ideas, and this is the classical line. It follows traditional development with a pawn in the center. Our plan has gone into effect, and at this point, we'll develop our kingside knight.} 4...Nf6 5.Nf3 {White brings their knight out as well. This occurs 37% of the time, which equals about 745,000 games in the database. Before pushing ...e6, let's get our light-squared bishop out so it's not stuck later.} 5...Bf5 6.Be2 {This won't be a problem at all. It's too passive because it doesn't challenge our bishop on f5 or aim to do something more aggressive.} 6...e6 7.O-O {White castles. To achieve the most optimal pawn structure, the move ...c6 is by far the best one here. We get to completely control the d5-square, and if we make it to the endgame, we may even be slightly better with some pressure.} 7...c6 8.Bf4 {White develops their bishop to f4.} 8...h6 {And we get ready to develop the rest of our pieces. White's Be2 allowed us to go for this classical structure. If we defend the middlegame properly, we should look forward to a solid endgame without weaknesses.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 3. Nc3 Classical Variation / Study 1: White Plays 6.Bd2, 6. Be2, or 6.Bd3 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/63670ca5-0fcf-4d90-8963-ca234d2f1bee/studies/a877111e-eb4c-43e7-9976-5d63aee35701/lines"]

1.e4 {You have come to the right place if you want to have a solid and unexpected defense against 1.e4! The Scandinavian Defense is a polarizing opening: some are hesitant to play it, but it holds many secrets and ways to get a solid position. We determine the structure and style of play, and we can catch opponents off-guard who have been quick to dismiss it.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White captures. This is the most popular move you'll see. Our queen will recapture and get out early, which seems to break some opening principles, but it's okay, even desired, in the Scandinavian.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nc3 {This move is seen 77% of times, in around 57 million games. It is one of the most popular variations because it allows White to develop with a tempo. However, it may make White overconfident in their opening. In reality, we are in control. Our idea is to keep the queen active: 3...Qa5.} 3...Qa5 4.d4 {d4 and Nf3 are White's most popular ideas, and this is the classical line. It follows traditional development with a pawn in the center. Our plan has gone into effect, and at this point, we'll develop our kingside knight.} 4...Nf6 5.Nf3 {White brings their knight out as well. This occurs 37% of the time, which equals about 745,000 games in the database. Before pushing ...e6, let's get our light-squared bishop out so it's not stuck later.} 5...Bf5 6.Bd2 {6. Bd2 is the most popular variation, being played 102,000 times in the database. White thinks they're threatening some discovered attacks. However, these aren't anything special.} 6...e6 7.Ne4 {For example, if 7. Ne4, there is a discovered attack. We have to save the queen.} 7...Qb6 8.Nxf6+ {White may trade knights here, thinking they're maybe getting some positional advantage by damaging our structure. No such advantage exists: our doubled f-pawns control many squares. They're a solid pawn island. In addition, we get the g-file.} 8...gxf6 9.Bc3 {9. Bc3, going on the same diagonal as our rook, isn't a big deal.} 9...Nc6 {White's center will come under pressure. 10. d5 to attack our f-pawn would be a serious mistake because of 10...0-0-0 and the d-pawn would fall. After we castle, we can go for the ...Bb4 trade, since White's bishop is the only piece with some potential threats.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 3. Nc3 Classical Variation / Study 1: White Plays 6.Bd2, 6. Be2, or 6.Bd3 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/63670ca5-0fcf-4d90-8963-ca234d2f1bee/studies/a877111e-eb4c-43e7-9976-5d63aee35701/lines"]

1.e4 {You have come to the right place if you want to have a solid and unexpected defense against 1.e4! The Scandinavian Defense is a polarizing opening: some are hesitant to play it, but it holds many secrets and ways to get a solid position. We determine the structure and style of play, and we can catch opponents off-guard who have been quick to dismiss it.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White captures. This is the most popular move you'll see. Our queen will recapture and get out early, which seems to break some opening principles, but it's okay, even desired, in the Scandinavian.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nc3 {This move is seen 77% of times, in around 57 million games. It is one of the most popular variations because it allows White to develop with a tempo. However, it may make White overconfident in their opening. In reality, we are in control. Our idea is to keep the queen active: 3...Qa5.} 3...Qa5 4.d4 {d4 and Nf3 are White's most popular ideas, and this is the classical line. It follows traditional development with a pawn in the center. Our plan has gone into effect, and at this point, we'll develop our kingside knight.} 4...Nf6 5.Nf3 {White brings their knight out as well. This occurs 37% of the time, which equals about 745,000 games in the database. Before pushing ...e6, let's get our light-squared bishop out so it's not stuck later.} 5...Bf5 6.Bd2 {6. Bd2 is the most popular variation, being played 102,000 times in the database. White thinks they're threatening some discovered attacks. However, these aren't anything special.} 6...e6 7.Be2 {Setups with Be2 are quite natural, but they're passive. The bishop's most aggressive square is c4.} 7...Be7 {Since Be2 isn't a concern, all you have to do now is complete your development. 0-0, Nbd7, and c6 to bring the queen back if you need to. These are all standard plans in the Scandinavian. Your long-term goal is to get a rook on the d-file and control the d5-square thanks to the c6- and e6-pawns.} *

