[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/63670ca5-0fcf-4d90-8963-ca234d2f1bee/studies/a877111e-eb4c-43e7-9976-5d63aee35701/lines"]

1.e4 {You have come to the right place if you want to have a solid and unexpected defense against 1.e4! The Scandinavian Defense is a polarizing opening: some are hesitant to play it, but it holds many secrets and ways to get a solid position. We determine the structure and style of play, and we can catch opponents off-guard who have been quick to dismiss it.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White captures. This is the most popular move you'll see. Our queen will recapture and get out early, which seems to break some opening principles, but it's okay, even desired, in the Scandinavian.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nc3 {This move is seen 77% of times, in around 57 million games. It is one of the most popular variations because it allows White to develop with a tempo. However, it may make White overconfident in their opening. In reality, we are in control. Our idea is to keep the queen active: 3...Qa5.} 3...Qa5 4.d4 {d4 and Nf3 are White's most popular ideas, and this is the classical line. It follows traditional development with a pawn in the center. Our plan has gone into effect, and at this point, we'll develop our kingside knight.} 4...Nf6 5.Nf3 {White brings their knight out as well. This occurs 37% of the time, which equals about 745,000 games in the database. Before pushing ...e6, let's get our light-squared bishop out so it's not stuck later.} 5...Bf5 6.Bd2 {6. Bd2 is the most popular variation, being played 102,000 times in the database. White thinks they're threatening some discovered attacks. However, these aren't anything special.} 6...e6 7.Bc4 {This is the most aggressive square for the bishop.} 7...Bb4 {This transposes into the 6. Bc4 line in the second study of this chapter.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 3. Nc3 Classical Variation / Study 2: White Plays 6.Bc4 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/63670ca5-0fcf-4d90-8963-ca234d2f1bee/studies/1e34984f-1157-4384-b6db-569a06144247/lines"]

1.e4 {For Study 2, we'll continue our exploration of the Classical Line in the Scandinavian Defense. In particular, after White develops both knights, we'll explore an aggressive sixth move: the bishop coming to c4. This fights for the d5-square and aims at e6 and f7. Let's see how to defend. White begins with 1. e4, and we'll challenge them immediately with 1...d5.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White captures. This is the most popular move you'll see. We recapture.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nc3 {White gains a tempo and develops. With 3...Qa5 we prepare a pin after White plays d4. Our queen is safe, according to theory.} 3...Qa5 4.d4 {d4, Nf3 and Nc3 are the Classical line. Our knight comes out to f6 to control d5.} 4...Nf6 5.Nf3 {White brings their knight out as well, following the principle of knights before bishops. We'll develop our light-squared bishop. f5 is a great square for it since it'll be outside of our pawn chain. We're also controlling the important e4-square.} 5...Bf5 6.Bc4 {This confrontational move is one of White's best. We should cover our weakness right away. White may have ideas of attacking and sacrificing on our f7-e6 diagonal later on, so we must be careful.} 6...e6 7.Bd2 {7. Bd2 is also popular to some degree. To counter the combination of Bc4 and Bd2, we'll play 7...Bb4.} 7...Bb4 8.a3 {White's most popular and natural move is to try and gain the bishop pair by forcing a trade. We'll take.} 8...Bxc3 9.Bxc3 {White recaptures and attacks our queen. And we'll make use of the b6-square where the queen is safe. Even though White has achieved the bishop pair, we'll start applying pressure to the white position.} 9...Qb6 10.O-O {White castles. Let's bring our queen's knight out and put some pressure on d4.} 10...Nc6 11.d5 {White pushes in the center and tries opening up the board. This also opens up an attack on our knight. This may seem dangerous, but we can pin the pawn! d5 can often be refuted in the Scandinavian if we have 0-0-0 available.} 11...O-O-O 12.Bxf6 {White takes on f6, trying to reduce the pressure on d5.} 12...gxf6 {But we simply recapture and we have an open g-file. We're also getting another attacker on the pawn with Be4. d5 and b2 are under attack. There's no way White will defend both. We should emerge up a pawn with a better position.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 3. Nc3 Classical Variation / Study 2: White Plays 6.Bc4 / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/63670ca5-0fcf-4d90-8963-ca234d2f1bee/studies/1e34984f-1157-4384-b6db-569a06144247/lines"]

1.e4 {For Study 2, we'll continue our exploration of the Classical Line in the Scandinavian Defense. In particular, after White develops both knights, we'll explore an aggressive sixth move: the bishop coming to c4. This fights for the d5-square and aims at e6 and f7. Let's see how to defend. White begins with 1. e4, and we'll challenge them immediately with 1...d5.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White captures. This is the most popular move you'll see. We recapture.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nc3 {White gains a tempo and develops. With 3...Qa5 we prepare a pin after White plays d4. Our queen is safe, according to theory.} 3...Qa5 4.d4 {d4, Nf3 and Nc3 are the Classical line. Our knight comes out to f6 to control d5.} 4...Nf6 5.Nf3 {White brings their knight out as well, following the principle of knights before bishops. We'll develop our light-squared bishop. f5 is a great square for it since it'll be outside of our pawn chain. We're also controlling the important e4-square.} 5...Bf5 6.Bc4 {This confrontational move is one of White's best. We should cover our weakness right away. White may have ideas of attacking and sacrificing on our f7-e6 diagonal later on, so we must be careful.} 6...e6 7.Bd2 {7. Bd2 is also popular to some degree. To counter the combination of Bc4 and Bd2, we'll play 7...Bb4.} 7...Bb4 8.a3 {White's most popular and natural move is to try and gain the bishop pair by forcing a trade. We'll take.} 8...Bxc3 9.Bxc3 {White recaptures and attacks our queen. And we'll make use of the b6-square where the queen is safe. Even though White has achieved the bishop pair, we'll start applying pressure to the white position.} 9...Qb6 10.Qe2 {White may get the queen out of the d-file quickly to avoid counterplay. We'll castle to get the king off the e-file.} 10...O-O 11.O-O-O {We have opposite side castling. But we control the light squares in the center. We'll develop our other knight.} 11...Nc6 12.Ne5 {White centralizes their knight. Since they aren't threatening anything yet, we'll increase pressure on d4. Rad8 is the obvious move.} 12...Rad8 13.g4 {This pawn push looks natural, but it's a mistake. We can capture on e5 and win material. If gxf5, obviously ...Nxc4.} 13...Nxe5 14.dxe5 {White takes our knight back.} 14...Nxg4 {We recapture with the knight and are attacking f2. We're up a pawn and should be able to defend this. It's quite hard for White to deal with the pressure on f2 and h2. They'll probably have to play something like Rdf1. If they do, something like ...Qc6 is strong. 0-1 Meunier Pion, J (2175)-Rozman, L (2333) Chess.com INT 2022} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 3. Nc3 Classical Variation / Study 2: White Plays 6.Bc4 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/63670ca5-0fcf-4d90-8963-ca234d2f1bee/studies/1e34984f-1157-4384-b6db-569a06144247/lines"]

1.e4 {For Study 2, we'll continue our exploration of the Classical Line in the Scandinavian Defense. In particular, after White develops both knights, we'll explore an aggressive sixth move: the bishop coming to c4. This fights for the d5-square and aims at e6 and f7. Let's see how to defend. White begins with 1. e4, and we'll challenge them immediately with 1...d5.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White captures. This is the most popular move you'll see. We recapture.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nc3 {White gains a tempo and develops. With 3...Qa5 we prepare a pin after White plays d4. Our queen is safe, according to theory.} 3...Qa5 4.d4 {d4, Nf3 and Nc3 are the Classical line. Our knight comes out to f6 to control d5.} 4...Nf6 5.Nf3 {White brings their knight out as well, following the principle of knights before bishops. We'll develop our light-squared bishop. f5 is a great square for it since it'll be outside of our pawn chain. We're also controlling the important e4-square.} 5...Bf5 6.Bc4 {This confrontational move is one of White's best. We should cover our weakness right away. White may have ideas of attacking and sacrificing on our f7-e6 diagonal later on, so we must be careful.} 6...e6 7.Bd2 {7. Bd2 is also popular to some degree. To counter the combination of Bc4 and Bd2, we'll play 7...Bb4.} 7...Bb4 8.a3 {White's most popular and natural move is to try and gain the bishop pair by forcing a trade. We'll take.} 8...Bxc3 9.Bxc3 {White recaptures and attacks our queen. And we'll make use of the b6-square where the queen is safe. Even though White has achieved the bishop pair, we'll start applying pressure to the white position.} 9...Qb6 10.O-O {White castles. Let's bring our queen's knight out and put some pressure on d4.} 10...Nc6 11.Qe2 {You may see Qe2 here. White wants to develop their queen and connect rooks. This move also defends the bishop and puts some pressure on the e6-pawn. Because of the e-file pressure, we should just castle.} 11...O-O 12.Rad1 {Centralizing rooks is normal in these positions, specially if there won't be any flank attacks. We'll do the same to increase our control of the d5-square.} 12...Rad8 13.h3 {White prevents ...Bg4.} 13...Ne4 {Black has an equal position out of the opening and we'll most likely get to take White's dark-squared bishop. Our plan will be to double up rooks on the d-file. If Nxc3, our queen will also be able to attack weaknesses with ...Qa5.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 3. Nc3 Classical Variation / Study 2: White Plays 6.Bc4 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/63670ca5-0fcf-4d90-8963-ca234d2f1bee/studies/1e34984f-1157-4384-b6db-569a06144247/lines"]

1.e4 {For Study 2, we'll continue our exploration of the Classical Line in the Scandinavian Defense. In particular, after White develops both knights, we'll explore an aggressive sixth move: the bishop coming to c4. This fights for the d5-square and aims at e6 and f7. Let's see how to defend. White begins with 1. e4, and we'll challenge them immediately with 1...d5.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White captures. This is the most popular move you'll see. We recapture.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nc3 {White gains a tempo and develops. With 3...Qa5 we prepare a pin after White plays d4. Our queen is safe, according to theory.} 3...Qa5 4.d4 {d4, Nf3 and Nc3 are the Classical line. Our knight comes out to f6 to control d5.} 4...Nf6 5.Nf3 {White brings their knight out as well, following the principle of knights before bishops. We'll develop our light-squared bishop. f5 is a great square for it since it'll be outside of our pawn chain. We're also controlling the important e4-square.} 5...Bf5 6.Bc4 {This confrontational move is one of White's best. We should cover our weakness right away. White may have ideas of attacking and sacrificing on our f7-e6 diagonal later on, so we must be careful.} 6...e6 7.O-O {The most popular move by far is castling. However, it shouldn't be a problem. We have time to create a blockade on the light squares and castle. We develop our knight.} 7...Nbd7 8.Bd2 {White places their bishop opposite our queen. With ...c6, we clear a path for our queen to retreat.} 8...c6 9.Nd5 {White has this discovered attack. But since we played ...c6, we should be fine. We can simply retreat the queen to d8.} 9...Qd8 10.Nxf6+ {White has to trade.} 10...Nxf6 {We recapture and we'll develop all our pieces in a coherent manner: centralizing our rooks and fighting for control of d5.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 3. Nc3 Classical Variation / Study 2: White Plays 6.Bc4 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/63670ca5-0fcf-4d90-8963-ca234d2f1bee/studies/1e34984f-1157-4384-b6db-569a06144247/lines"]

1.e4 {For Study 2, we'll continue our exploration of the Classical Line in the Scandinavian Defense. In particular, after White develops both knights, we'll explore an aggressive sixth move: the bishop coming to c4. This fights for the d5-square and aims at e6 and f7. Let's see how to defend. White begins with 1. e4, and we'll challenge them immediately with 1...d5.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White captures. This is the most popular move you'll see. We recapture.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nc3 {White gains a tempo and develops. With 3...Qa5 we prepare a pin after White plays d4. Our queen is safe, according to theory.} 3...Qa5 4.d4 {d4, Nf3 and Nc3 are the Classical line. Our knight comes out to f6 to control d5.} 4...Nf6 5.Nf3 {White brings their knight out as well, following the principle of knights before bishops. We'll develop our light-squared bishop. f5 is a great square for it since it'll be outside of our pawn chain. We're also controlling the important e4-square.} 5...Bf5 6.Bc4 {This confrontational move is one of White's best. We should cover our weakness right away. White may have ideas of attacking and sacrificing on our f7-e6 diagonal later on, so we must be careful.} 6...e6 7.Bd2 {7. Bd2 is also popular to some degree. To counter the combination of Bc4 and Bd2, we'll play 7...Bb4.} 7...Bb4 8.a3 {White's most popular and natural move is to try and gain the bishop pair by forcing a trade. We'll take.} 8...Bxc3 9.Bxc3 {White recaptures and attacks our queen. And we'll make use of the b6-square where the queen is safe. Even though White has achieved the bishop pair, we'll start applying pressure to the white position.} 9...Qb6 10.O-O {White castles. Let's bring our queen's knight out and put some pressure on d4.} 10...Nc6 11.a4 {The idea behind this move is to bully our queen around. Whenever you play the Scandinavian, you should expect your opponent to try and attack your queen in some way. That's why you have to have ways to escape prepared. Here, 11...a5 stops White's advance.} 11...a5 12.Bb5 {The bishop pins our knight. But we can castle.} 12...O-O 13.Nd2 {White wants to, once again, attack our queen. We have time to attack c2 because our queen has an escape square on a7.} 13...Nb4 14.Nc4 {White attacks us. We'll retreat to the bolthole on a7.} 14...Qa7 15.Ne3 {And White attacks our attacker and in turn defends c2.} 15...Bg6 {I really like how solid our position is and how vulnerable White's d4-pawn seems. We're blockading the square in front of it, and we can even go ...c6 and ...c5 to attack it.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 3. Nc3 Classical Variation / Study 2: White Plays 6.Bc4 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/63670ca5-0fcf-4d90-8963-ca234d2f1bee/studies/1e34984f-1157-4384-b6db-569a06144247/lines"]

1.e4 {For Study 2, we'll continue our exploration of the Classical Line in the Scandinavian Defense. In particular, after White develops both knights, we'll explore an aggressive sixth move: the bishop coming to c4. This fights for the d5-square and aims at e6 and f7. Let's see how to defend. White begins with 1. e4, and we'll challenge them immediately with 1...d5.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White captures. This is the most popular move you'll see. We recapture.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nc3 {White gains a tempo and develops. With 3...Qa5 we prepare a pin after White plays d4. Our queen is safe, according to theory.} 3...Qa5 4.d4 {d4, Nf3 and Nc3 are the Classical line. Our knight comes out to f6 to control d5.} 4...Nf6 5.Nf3 {White brings their knight out as well, following the principle of knights before bishops. We'll develop our light-squared bishop. f5 is a great square for it since it'll be outside of our pawn chain. We're also controlling the important e4-square.} 5...Bf5 6.Bc4 {This confrontational move is one of White's best. We should cover our weakness right away. White may have ideas of attacking and sacrificing on our f7-e6 diagonal later on, so we must be careful.} 6...e6 7.Bd2 {7. Bd2 is also popular to some degree. To counter the combination of Bc4 and Bd2, we'll play 7...Bb4.} 7...Bb4 8.a3 {White's most popular and natural move is to try and gain the bishop pair by forcing a trade. We'll take.} 8...Bxc3 9.Bxc3 {White recaptures and attacks our queen. And we'll make use of the b6-square where the queen is safe. Even though White has achieved the bishop pair, we'll start applying pressure to the white position.} 9...Qb6 10.O-O {White castles. Let's bring our queen's knight out and put some pressure on d4.} 10...Nc6 11.b4 {The idea behind b4 is to attack our knight. We shouldn't fear this attack, though. We castle into safety.} 11...O-O 12.b5 {White attacks our knight. We can go for a counterattack with Ne4.} 12...Ne4 13.Bb2 {The bishop retreats to b2. We move our knight away to attack the other bishop.} 13...Na5 14.Be2 {The bishop retreats as well. It seems White's b4 and b5 operation wasn't so fruitful.} 14...Rfd8 {We can start moving our pieces into action. ...c5 is good to undermine the center and open our rook up. White will have to find a way to deal with the increasing pressure.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 3. Nc3 Classical Variation / Study 2: White Plays 6.Bc4 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/63670ca5-0fcf-4d90-8963-ca234d2f1bee/studies/1e34984f-1157-4384-b6db-569a06144247/lines"]

1.e4 {For Study 2, we'll continue our exploration of the Classical Line in the Scandinavian Defense. In particular, after White develops both knights, we'll explore an aggressive sixth move: the bishop coming to c4. This fights for the d5-square and aims at e6 and f7. Let's see how to defend. White begins with 1. e4, and we'll challenge them immediately with 1...d5.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White captures. This is the most popular move you'll see. We recapture.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nc3 {White gains a tempo and develops. With 3...Qa5 we prepare a pin after White plays d4. Our queen is safe, according to theory.} 3...Qa5 4.d4 {d4, Nf3 and Nc3 are the Classical line. Our knight comes out to f6 to control d5.} 4...Nf6 5.Nf3 {White brings their knight out as well, following the principle of knights before bishops. We'll develop our light-squared bishop. f5 is a great square for it since it'll be outside of our pawn chain. We're also controlling the important e4-square.} 5...Bf5 6.Bc4 {This confrontational move is one of White's best. We should cover our weakness right away. White may have ideas of attacking and sacrificing on our f7-e6 diagonal later on, so we must be careful.} 6...e6 7.Bd2 {7. Bd2 is also popular to some degree. To counter the combination of Bc4 and Bd2, we'll play 7...Bb4.} 7...Bb4 8.a3 {White's most popular and natural move is to try and gain the bishop pair by forcing a trade. We'll take.} 8...Bxc3 9.Bxc3 {White recaptures and attacks our queen. And we'll make use of the b6-square where the queen is safe. Even though White has achieved the bishop pair, we'll start applying pressure to the white position.} 9...Qb6 10.O-O {White castles. Let's bring our queen's knight out and put some pressure on d4.} 10...Nc6 11.Re1 {White centralizes their rook. However, we can add pressure on d4 before castling.} 11...Rd8 {We'll castle next and bring our other rook to e8. We have the plan of ...Nd5 to attack the bishop on c3. Black has a stable position, which itself puts a pressure on White because they're not playing for a big advantage but are heading into a position where all three results are possible.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 3. Nc3 Classical Variation / Study 2: White Plays 6.Bc4 / Variation 10"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/63670ca5-0fcf-4d90-8963-ca234d2f1bee/studies/1e34984f-1157-4384-b6db-569a06144247/lines"]

1.e4 {For Study 2, we'll continue our exploration of the Classical Line in the Scandinavian Defense. In particular, after White develops both knights, we'll explore an aggressive sixth move: the bishop coming to c4. This fights for the d5-square and aims at e6 and f7. Let's see how to defend. White begins with 1. e4, and we'll challenge them immediately with 1...d5.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White captures. This is the most popular move you'll see. We recapture.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nc3 {White gains a tempo and develops. With 3...Qa5 we prepare a pin after White plays d4. Our queen is safe, according to theory.} 3...Qa5 4.d4 {d4, Nf3 and Nc3 are the Classical line. Our knight comes out to f6 to control d5.} 4...Nf6 5.Nf3 {White brings their knight out as well, following the principle of knights before bishops. We'll develop our light-squared bishop. f5 is a great square for it since it'll be outside of our pawn chain. We're also controlling the important e4-square.} 5...Bf5 6.Bc4 {This confrontational move is one of White's best. We should cover our weakness right away. White may have ideas of attacking and sacrificing on our f7-e6 diagonal later on, so we must be careful.} 6...e6 7.Bd2 {7. Bd2 is also popular to some degree. To counter the combination of Bc4 and Bd2, we'll play 7...Bb4.} 7...Bb4 8.O-O {Castling is a natural move. We'll develop our queen's knight and likely transpose to another line.} 8...Nc6 9.a3 {a3 lands us directly in the 8. a3 line. We'll take on c3.} 9...Bxc3 10.Bxc3 {White recaptures.} 10...Qb6 {And this is the same as the 8. a3 line.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 3. Nc3 Classical Variation / Study 2: White Plays 6.Bc4 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/63670ca5-0fcf-4d90-8963-ca234d2f1bee/studies/1e34984f-1157-4384-b6db-569a06144247/lines"]

1.e4 {For Study 2, we'll continue our exploration of the Classical Line in the Scandinavian Defense. In particular, after White develops both knights, we'll explore an aggressive sixth move: the bishop coming to c4. This fights for the d5-square and aims at e6 and f7. Let's see how to defend. White begins with 1. e4, and we'll challenge them immediately with 1...d5.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White captures. This is the most popular move you'll see. We recapture.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nc3 {White gains a tempo and develops. With 3...Qa5 we prepare a pin after White plays d4. Our queen is safe, according to theory.} 3...Qa5 4.d4 {d4, Nf3 and Nc3 are the Classical line. Our knight comes out to f6 to control d5.} 4...Nf6 5.Nf3 {White brings their knight out as well, following the principle of knights before bishops. We'll develop our light-squared bishop. f5 is a great square for it since it'll be outside of our pawn chain. We're also controlling the important e4-square.} 5...Bf5 6.Bc4 {This confrontational move is one of White's best. We should cover our weakness right away. White may have ideas of attacking and sacrificing on our f7-e6 diagonal later on, so we must be careful.} 6...e6 7.O-O {The most popular move by far is castling. However, it shouldn't be a problem. We have time to create a blockade on the light squares and castle. We develop our knight.} 7...Nbd7 8.Bd2 {White places their bishop opposite our queen. With ...c6, we clear a path for our queen to retreat.} 8...c6 9.Re1 {White may start adding e-file pressure. We get the queen out of any potential discovered attacks.} 9...Qc7 10.h3 {White aims to restrict our bishop on f5 and target it with Nh4.} 10...h6 {We'll go ...Be7 or ...Bd6 and castle kingside. With kings castled on the same side, there will be no tactical race for checkmate. Rather, we're looking to develop all our pieces, control d5, maybe expand on the queenside and centralize our rooks.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 3. Nc3 Classical Variation / Study 2: White Plays 6.Bc4 / Variation 11"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/63670ca5-0fcf-4d90-8963-ca234d2f1bee/studies/1e34984f-1157-4384-b6db-569a06144247/lines"]

1.e4 {For Study 2, we'll continue our exploration of the Classical Line in the Scandinavian Defense. In particular, after White develops both knights, we'll explore an aggressive sixth move: the bishop coming to c4. This fights for the d5-square and aims at e6 and f7. Let's see how to defend. White begins with 1. e4, and we'll challenge them immediately with 1...d5.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White captures. This is the most popular move you'll see. We recapture.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nc3 {White gains a tempo and develops. With 3...Qa5 we prepare a pin after White plays d4. Our queen is safe, according to theory.} 3...Qa5 4.d4 {d4, Nf3 and Nc3 are the Classical line. Our knight comes out to f6 to control d5.} 4...Nf6 5.Nf3 {White brings their knight out as well, following the principle of knights before bishops. We'll develop our light-squared bishop. f5 is a great square for it since it'll be outside of our pawn chain. We're also controlling the important e4-square.} 5...Bf5 6.Bc4 {This confrontational move is one of White's best. We should cover our weakness right away. White may have ideas of attacking and sacrificing on our f7-e6 diagonal later on, so we must be careful.} 6...e6 7.Bd2 {7. Bd2 is also popular to some degree. To counter the combination of Bc4 and Bd2, we'll play 7...Bb4.} 7...Bb4 8.Qe2 {White could continue by putting pressure on the e-file and castling queenside next. We'll castle quickly, getting our king off the e-file.} 8...O-O 9.O-O-O {White's setup is aggressive. We should expect a sharp game where we attack each other's kings. We can develop our knight with a plan: ...Bg4 to attack the d4-pawn.} 9...Nc6 10.a3 {This move doesn't help White. We now have a sacrifice for the attack.} 10...Bxa3 11.bxa3 {White recaptures. We take with check.} 11...Qxa3+ 12.Kb1 {The king goes to the b-file. And now the point is revealed! The king is stuck on the b-file, so 12...b5 is really strong. We have Bxc2, Nb4, and Rb8 in the air.} 12...b5 13.Nxb5 {The knight captures and attacks the queen.} 13...Rfb8 {But we don't have to move the queen away. We can pin the knight and our attack is devastating for White.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 3. Nc3 Classical Variation / Study 2: White Plays 6.Bc4 / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/63670ca5-0fcf-4d90-8963-ca234d2f1bee/studies/1e34984f-1157-4384-b6db-569a06144247/lines"]

1.e4 {For Study 2, we'll continue our exploration of the Classical Line in the Scandinavian Defense. In particular, after White develops both knights, we'll explore an aggressive sixth move: the bishop coming to c4. This fights for the d5-square and aims at e6 and f7. Let's see how to defend. White begins with 1. e4, and we'll challenge them immediately with 1...d5.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White captures. This is the most popular move you'll see. We recapture.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nc3 {White gains a tempo and develops. With 3...Qa5 we prepare a pin after White plays d4. Our queen is safe, according to theory.} 3...Qa5 4.d4 {d4, Nf3 and Nc3 are the Classical line. Our knight comes out to f6 to control d5.} 4...Nf6 5.Nf3 {White brings their knight out as well, following the principle of knights before bishops. We'll develop our light-squared bishop. f5 is a great square for it since it'll be outside of our pawn chain. We're also controlling the important e4-square.} 5...Bf5 6.Bc4 {This confrontational move is one of White's best. We should cover our weakness right away. White may have ideas of attacking and sacrificing on our f7-e6 diagonal later on, so we must be careful.} 6...e6 7.Bd2 {7. Bd2 is also popular to some degree. To counter the combination of Bc4 and Bd2, we'll play 7...Bb4.} 7...Bb4 8.a3 {White's most popular and natural move is to try and gain the bishop pair by forcing a trade. We'll take.} 8...Bxc3 9.Bxc3 {White recaptures and attacks our queen. And we'll make use of the b6-square where the queen is safe. Even though White has achieved the bishop pair, we'll start applying pressure to the white position.} 9...Qb6 10.d5 {This move seems logical: White is trying to open things up since they played aggressively with Bc4. I like taking in the center. We'll be able to shut down White's initiative if we don't panic. Their king is in the center, too.} 10...exd5 11.Bxd5 {White recaptures, with some pressure on b7. Our queen cannot move away. However, we'll just castle. King safety is more important with those bishops roaming about.} 11...O-O 12.Bxf6 {White may damage our pawns here. However, it is of no significant concern. We'll recapture, but despite the doubled pawns, we're solid. All our pieces (except our bishop) are defended.} 12...gxf6 {We'll recapture, but despite the doubled pawns, we're solid. All our pieces (except our bishop) are defended.} 13.O-O {White castles.} 13...Nd7 {Despite our damaged structure, White has lost the bishop pair and our pieces will have good squares. Our knight will be well located on e5, where the f6-pawn provides support. Rad8 will come with d-file pressure, and our queen is attacking b2. The bishop can also go to g6 to defend itself and the kingside.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Critical 6.Ne5 / Study 1: All Lines / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/73dc795b-ea7d-4d58-b550-3beff4d6f006/studies/8d10428c-92f4-4f96-802c-6f20166d089c/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to Chapter 2 of our course in the Scandinavian. In this chapter, we'll analyze White's critical Ne5 jump on move 6, which has become one of the most consistent weapons against the Qa5 Scandinavian. Let's dive into it.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White takes. We'll take back with the queen, of course.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nc3 {White will most likely develop with tempo on the queen. And, as in the last chapter, we are opting for the Qa5 Scandinavian.} 3...Qa5 4.d4 {The most common is for White to place a pawn in the center, and now their knight is pinned. Let's begin developing pieces with Nf6.} 4...Nf6 5.Nf3 {White activates both knights. Our light-squared bishop has a nice place on f5.} 5...Bf5 6.Ne5 {6.Ne5 is only played 3% of the time in the Lichess database under 2000 ELO, but is played 27% in the Masters database! This is the most testing move from White that we could encounter. We do have the ...c6 resource here, so we can retreat our queen if needed.} 6...c6 7.Bc4 {7.Bc4 is the second most popular move, played 25% of the time, but definitely the most sensible one. White is simply attacking the f7-pawn with two pieces. Of course, the most straightforward defense is ...e6, and the best one.} 7...e6 8.g4 {For an opponent that's not prepared, g4 is definitely a big surprise! White attacks our bishop and forces us to retreat. This enables them to start a quick kingside pawn storm with tempo.} 8...Bg6 9.h4 {The most logical move for White is to stay consistent in hunting our bishop. We don't have to stop the threat of h5. Instead, we can challenge the knight on e5.} 9...Nbd7 10.Nxd7 {But what if White trades and then plays h5? We recapture with the knight.} 10...Nxd7 11.h5 {Luckily, h5 only grabs space but doesn't trap the bishop. The e4-square is available, since the c3-knight is pinned.} 11...Be4 12.O-O {Castling is an interesting move recommended by the computer. The threat of capturing our bishop is reintroduced since the knight is no longer pinned. We should retreat the bishop to d5.} 12...Bd5 13.Nxd5 {If White takes, we'll recapture with the c-pawn.} 13...cxd5 14.Bd3 {And White retreats to d3, having traded the knight for our bishop. This is the critical position of this modern variation, as has been tested in many GM games. We have a very sound Caro-esque structure, but White has the bishop pair and space on the kingside. Let's develop the bishop.} 14...Bd6 15.Qf3 {A move worth analyzing is 15.Qf3, played by Alireza Firouzja against Magnus Carlsen. The queen is eyeing the kingside. Black has an interesting move here, which aims to solve the problem of our kingside.} 15...O-O-O 16.Be3 {White could choose not to go into the 16.Qxf7 line and defend the center while developing. Well, we've got another trick for them! First of all, we get our king off the c-file.} 16...Kb8 17.Qxf7 {If White does take in this line, however, that's a losing blunder! White is losing by force after we attack the queen with the h-rook.} 17...Rhf8 18.Qxg7 {If the queen captures on g7, it's only opened up a new line of attack for us.} 18...Rg8 {We can slide to g8 and attack the queen again. Once it moves, the g4-pawn will be hanging with check. This is winning for Black.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Critical 6.Ne5 / Study 1: All Lines / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/73dc795b-ea7d-4d58-b550-3beff4d6f006/studies/8d10428c-92f4-4f96-802c-6f20166d089c/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to Chapter 2 of our course in the Scandinavian. In this chapter, we'll analyze White's critical Ne5 jump on move 6, which has become one of the most consistent weapons against the Qa5 Scandinavian. Let's dive into it.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White takes. We'll take back with the queen, of course.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nc3 {White will most likely develop with tempo on the queen. And, as in the last chapter, we are opting for the Qa5 Scandinavian.} 3...Qa5 4.d4 {The most common is for White to place a pawn in the center, and now their knight is pinned. Let's begin developing pieces with Nf6.} 4...Nf6 5.Nf3 {White activates both knights. Our light-squared bishop has a nice place on f5.} 5...Bf5 6.Ne5 {6.Ne5 is only played 3% of the time in the Lichess database under 2000 ELO, but is played 27% in the Masters database! This is the most testing move from White that we could encounter. We do have the ...c6 resource here, so we can retreat our queen if needed.} 6...c6 7.Bc4 {7.Bc4 is the second most popular move, played 25% of the time, but definitely the most sensible one. White is simply attacking the f7-pawn with two pieces. Of course, the most straightforward defense is ...e6, and the best one.} 7...e6 8.g4 {For an opponent that's not prepared, g4 is definitely a big surprise! White attacks our bishop and forces us to retreat. This enables them to start a quick kingside pawn storm with tempo.} 8...Bg6 9.h4 {The most logical move for White is to stay consistent in hunting our bishop. We don't have to stop the threat of h5. Instead, we can challenge the knight on e5.} 9...Nbd7 10.Nxd7 {But what if White trades and then plays h5? We recapture with the knight.} 10...Nxd7 11.h5 {Luckily, h5 only grabs space but doesn't trap the bishop. The e4-square is available, since the c3-knight is pinned.} 11...Be4 12.O-O {Castling is an interesting move recommended by the computer. The threat of capturing our bishop is reintroduced since the knight is no longer pinned. We should retreat the bishop to d5.} 12...Bd5 13.Nxd5 {If White takes, we'll recapture with the c-pawn.} 13...cxd5 14.Bd3 {And White retreats to d3, having traded the knight for our bishop. This is the critical position of this modern variation, as has been tested in many GM games. We have a very sound Caro-esque structure, but White has the bishop pair and space on the kingside. Let's develop the bishop.} 14...Bd6 15.Qf3 {A move worth analyzing is 15.Qf3, played by Alireza Firouzja against Magnus Carlsen. The queen is eyeing the kingside. Black has an interesting move here, which aims to solve the problem of our kingside.} 15...O-O-O 16.Be3 {White could choose not to go into the 16.Qxf7 line and defend the center while developing. Well, we've got another trick for them! First of all, we get our king off the c-file.} 16...Kb8 17.Qxf7 {If White does take in this line, however, that's a losing blunder! White is losing by force after we attack the queen with the h-rook.} 17...Rhf8 18.Qxe6 {If White captures on e6...} 18...Rf6 {The queen is completely trapped. Oops!} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Critical 6.Ne5 / Study 1: All Lines / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/73dc795b-ea7d-4d58-b550-3beff4d6f006/studies/8d10428c-92f4-4f96-802c-6f20166d089c/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to Chapter 2 of our course in the Scandinavian. In this chapter, we'll analyze White's critical Ne5 jump on move 6, which has become one of the most consistent weapons against the Qa5 Scandinavian. Let's dive into it.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White takes. We'll take back with the queen, of course.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nc3 {White will most likely develop with tempo on the queen. And, as in the last chapter, we are opting for the Qa5 Scandinavian.} 3...Qa5 4.d4 {The most common is for White to place a pawn in the center, and now their knight is pinned. Let's begin developing pieces with Nf6.} 4...Nf6 5.Nf3 {White activates both knights. Our light-squared bishop has a nice place on f5.} 5...Bf5 6.Ne5 {6.Ne5 is only played 3% of the time in the Lichess database under 2000 ELO, but is played 27% in the Masters database! This is the most testing move from White that we could encounter. We do have the ...c6 resource here, so we can retreat our queen if needed.} 6...c6 7.Bc4 {7.Bc4 is the second most popular move, played 25% of the time, but definitely the most sensible one. White is simply attacking the f7-pawn with two pieces. Of course, the most straightforward defense is ...e6, and the best one.} 7...e6 8.g4 {For an opponent that's not prepared, g4 is definitely a big surprise! White attacks our bishop and forces us to retreat. This enables them to start a quick kingside pawn storm with tempo.} 8...Bg6 9.h4 {The most logical move for White is to stay consistent in hunting our bishop. We don't have to stop the threat of h5. Instead, we can challenge the knight on e5.} 9...Nbd7 10.Nxd7 {But what if White trades and then plays h5? We recapture with the knight.} 10...Nxd7 11.h5 {Luckily, h5 only grabs space but doesn't trap the bishop. The e4-square is available, since the c3-knight is pinned.} 11...Be4 12.O-O {Castling is an interesting move recommended by the computer. The threat of capturing our bishop is reintroduced since the knight is no longer pinned. We should retreat the bishop to d5.} 12...Bd5 13.Nxd5 {If White takes, we'll recapture with the c-pawn.} 13...cxd5 14.Bd3 {And White retreats to d3, having traded the knight for our bishop. This is the critical position of this modern variation, as has been tested in many GM games. We have a very sound Caro-esque structure, but White has the bishop pair and space on the kingside. Let's develop the bishop.} 14...Bd6 15.Qf3 {A move worth analyzing is 15.Qf3, played by Alireza Firouzja against Magnus Carlsen. The queen is eyeing the kingside. Black has an interesting move here, which aims to solve the problem of our kingside.} 15...O-O-O 16.Qxf7 {As mentioned, White can take the pawn for free. The queen is also attacking the e6-pawn now. Well, we want to attack the central d4-pawn and actively defend e6!} 16...Qb6 17.Kg2 {Kg2 kills the Bh2+ discovered attack tactic, so now White is threatening to capture on e6 again. Now it's time to defend it for real, with Rde8.} 17...Rde8 18.c3 {White finally defends the d-pawn, and we'll attack the queen, giving up yet another pawn!} 18...Rhf8 19.Qxg7 {The only safe square for White is to capture on g7.} 19...Rg8 {A new line of attack has opened! We attack the queen again, this time on the g-file, and skewer the g4-pawn. White is going to lose that pawn, but the position will remain equal. Confusing and full of poison, but equal.} *

