1.e4 {Welcome to Chapter 2 of our course in the Scandinavian. In this chapter, we'll analyze White's critical Ne5 jump on move 6, which has become one of the most consistent weapons against the Qa5 Scandinavian. Let's dive into it.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White takes. We'll take back with the queen, of course.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nc3 {White will most likely develop with tempo on the queen. And, as in the last chapter, we are opting for the Qa5 Scandinavian.} 3...Qa5 4.d4 {The most common is for White to place a pawn in the center, and now their knight is pinned. Let's begin developing pieces with Nf6.} 4...Nf6 5.Nf3 {White activates both knights. Our light-squared bishop has a nice place on f5.} 5...Bf5 6.Ne5 {6.Ne5 is only played 3% of the time in the Lichess database under 2000 ELO, but is played 27% in the Masters database! This is the most testing move from White that we could encounter. We do have the ...c6 resource here, so we can retreat our queen if needed.} 6...c6 7.Bc4 {7.Bc4 is the second most popular move, played 25% of the time, but definitely the most sensible one. White is simply attacking the f7-pawn with two pieces. Of course, the most straightforward defense is ...e6, and the best one.} 7...e6 8.g4 {For an opponent that's not prepared, g4 is definitely a big surprise! White attacks our bishop and forces us to retreat. This enables them to start a quick kingside pawn storm with tempo.} 8...Bg6 9.h4 {The most logical move for White is to stay consistent in hunting our bishop. We don't have to stop the threat of h5. Instead, we can challenge the knight on e5.} 9...Nbd7 10.Nxd7 {But what if White trades and then plays h5? We recapture with the knight.} 10...Nxd7 11.h5 {Luckily, h5 only grabs space but doesn't trap the bishop. The e4-square is available, since the c3-knight is pinned.} 11...Be4 12.O-O {Castling is an interesting move recommended by the computer. The threat of capturing our bishop is reintroduced since the knight is no longer pinned. We should retreat the bishop to d5.} 12...Bd5 13.Nxd5 {If White takes, we'll recapture with the c-pawn.} 13...cxd5 14.Bd3 {And White retreats to d3, having traded the knight for our bishop. This is the critical position of this modern variation, as has been tested in many GM games. We have a very sound Caro-esque structure, but White has the bishop pair and space on the kingside. Let's develop the bishop.} 14...Bd6 15.c3 {A harmonious move for White is c3, placing pawns on the opposite color of their extra bishop. We should get our king out of the center by castling queenside, so we can challenge White's advanced kingside pawns later.} 15...O-O-O 16.b4 {Playing with tempo, White could try to attack us on the queenside once we castle. No worries, we just retreat to the safest square, c7.} 16...Qc7 17.Be3 {White develops the bishop, perhaps aiming for Rc1.} 17...Kb8 {But nothing to worry about after the prophylactic ...Kb8. Again, important to remember that we've got counterplay in the form of ...g6 and ...f5, pushing the kingside pawns!} *

