[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/73dc795b-ea7d-4d58-b550-3beff4d6f006/studies/8d10428c-92f4-4f96-802c-6f20166d089c/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to Chapter 2 of our course in the Scandinavian. In this chapter, we'll analyze White's critical Ne5 jump on move 6, which has become one of the most consistent weapons against the Qa5 Scandinavian. Let's dive into it.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White takes. We'll take back with the queen, of course.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nc3 {White will most likely develop with tempo on the queen. And, as in the last chapter, we are opting for the Qa5 Scandinavian.} 3...Qa5 4.d4 {The most common is for White to place a pawn in the center, and now their knight is pinned. Let's begin developing pieces with Nf6.} 4...Nf6 5.Nf3 {White activates both knights. Our light-squared bishop has a nice place on f5.} 5...Bf5 6.Ne5 {6.Ne5 is only played 3% of the time in the Lichess database under 2000 ELO, but is played 27% in the Masters database! This is the most testing move from White that we could encounter. We do have the ...c6 resource here, so we can retreat our queen if needed.} 6...c6 7.Bc4 {7.Bc4 is the second most popular move, played 25% of the time, but definitely the most sensible one. White is simply attacking the f7-pawn with two pieces. Of course, the most straightforward defense is ...e6, and the best one.} 7...e6 8.g4 {For an opponent that's not prepared, g4 is definitely a big surprise! White attacks our bishop and forces us to retreat. This enables them to start a quick kingside pawn storm with tempo.} 8...Bg6 9.h4 {The most logical move for White is to stay consistent in hunting our bishop. We don't have to stop the threat of h5. Instead, we can challenge the knight on e5.} 9...Nbd7 10.Nxd7 {But what if White trades and then plays h5? We recapture with the knight.} 10...Nxd7 11.h5 {Luckily, h5 only grabs space but doesn't trap the bishop. The e4-square is available, since the c3-knight is pinned.} 11...Be4 12.O-O {Castling is an interesting move recommended by the computer. The threat of capturing our bishop is reintroduced since the knight is no longer pinned. We should retreat the bishop to d5.} 12...Bd5 13.Nxd5 {If White takes, we'll recapture with the c-pawn.} 13...cxd5 14.Bd3 {And White retreats to d3, having traded the knight for our bishop. This is the critical position of this modern variation, as has been tested in many GM games. We have a very sound Caro-esque structure, but White has the bishop pair and space on the kingside. Let's develop the bishop.} 14...Bd6 15.Bd2 {Another logical idea by White is to develop with tempo on our queen. We'll retreat to b6 and hit some pawns.} 15...Qb6 16.c3 {16.c3 sufficiently connects the pawns and defends the center. Capturing on b2 is too dangerous because of Rb1 ideas. But we have another concrete plan.} 16...Bc7 {We'll retreat to c7, with the idea of placing the queen on d6 and threatening checkmate. Apart from that, kingside ideas to undermine White's structure could be ...g6 and ...f5, as well as ...Nf6. In that case, we'll probably castle long. Frankly, you can castle either way in this line in general, but there's a long game awaiting.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Critical 6.Ne5 / Study 1: All Lines / Variation 10"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/73dc795b-ea7d-4d58-b550-3beff4d6f006/studies/8d10428c-92f4-4f96-802c-6f20166d089c/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to Chapter 2 of our course in the Scandinavian. In this chapter, we'll analyze White's critical Ne5 jump on move 6, which has become one of the most consistent weapons against the Qa5 Scandinavian. Let's dive into it.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White takes. We'll take back with the queen, of course.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nc3 {White will most likely develop with tempo on the queen. And, as in the last chapter, we are opting for the Qa5 Scandinavian.} 3...Qa5 4.d4 {The most common is for White to place a pawn in the center, and now their knight is pinned. Let's begin developing pieces with Nf6.} 4...Nf6 5.Nf3 {White activates both knights. Our light-squared bishop has a nice place on f5.} 5...Bf5 6.Ne5 {6.Ne5 is only played 3% of the time in the Lichess database under 2000 ELO, but is played 27% in the Masters database! This is the most testing move from White that we could encounter. We do have the ...c6 resource here, so we can retreat our queen if needed.} 6...c6 7.Bc4 {7.Bc4 is the second most popular move, played 25% of the time, but definitely the most sensible one. White is simply attacking the f7-pawn with two pieces. Of course, the most straightforward defense is ...e6, and the best one.} 7...e6 8.g4 {For an opponent that's not prepared, g4 is definitely a big surprise! White attacks our bishop and forces us to retreat. This enables them to start a quick kingside pawn storm with tempo.} 8...Bg6 9.Bd2 {The 9.Bd2 line will follow the same moves we've seen in the previous line, but we'll show the variation in more depth. First, we'll offer the knight trade as per usual.} 9...Nbd7 10.Qe2 {It makes more sense for White to defend e5 now, because it enables them to castle long on the next move. Now we can activate the bishop to b4.} 10...Bb4 11.O-O-O {White castles long. This is how you could think: Now we've activated the bishop to pressure the knight, it would be great if we could add even more pressure with ...Nd5. But if we play it right now, White can capture on d7. Therefore, we play Nxe5.} 11...Nxe5 12.dxe5 {The pawn recaptures, and now we have an easy decision on where to jump with the knight.} 12...Nd5 13.Ne4 {White evades the pressure and adds a defender to the d2-bishop. Trades are about to happen.} 13...Bxd2+ 14.Rxd2 {Rook takes. Now, notice that since we've traded the dark-squared bishops, White is threatening to infiltrate with Nd6+. Therefore, we'll exchange minor pieces on e4.} 14...Bxe4 15.Qxe4 {Queen takes.} 15...O-O-O {After all is said and done, we'll castle long with a strong knight in the center. A good idea would be to push on the kingside. Something like ...g5 and ...Nf4 would give the knight a great outpost. The knight could also retreat to b6 to hit the bishop on c4.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Critical 6.Ne5 / Study 1: All Lines / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/73dc795b-ea7d-4d58-b550-3beff4d6f006/studies/8d10428c-92f4-4f96-802c-6f20166d089c/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to Chapter 2 of our course in the Scandinavian. In this chapter, we'll analyze White's critical Ne5 jump on move 6, which has become one of the most consistent weapons against the Qa5 Scandinavian. Let's dive into it.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White takes. We'll take back with the queen, of course.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nc3 {White will most likely develop with tempo on the queen. And, as in the last chapter, we are opting for the Qa5 Scandinavian.} 3...Qa5 4.d4 {The most common is for White to place a pawn in the center, and now their knight is pinned. Let's begin developing pieces with Nf6.} 4...Nf6 5.Nf3 {White activates both knights. Our light-squared bishop has a nice place on f5.} 5...Bf5 6.Ne5 {6.Ne5 is only played 3% of the time in the Lichess database under 2000 ELO, but is played 27% in the Masters database! This is the most testing move from White that we could encounter. We do have the ...c6 resource here, so we can retreat our queen if needed.} 6...c6 7.Bc4 {7.Bc4 is the second most popular move, played 25% of the time, but definitely the most sensible one. White is simply attacking the f7-pawn with two pieces. Of course, the most straightforward defense is ...e6, and the best one.} 7...e6 8.g4 {For an opponent that's not prepared, g4 is definitely a big surprise! White attacks our bishop and forces us to retreat. This enables them to start a quick kingside pawn storm with tempo.} 8...Bg6 9.h4 {The most logical move for White is to stay consistent in hunting our bishop. We don't have to stop the threat of h5. Instead, we can challenge the knight on e5.} 9...Nbd7 10.Nxd7 {But what if White trades and then plays h5? We recapture with the knight.} 10...Nxd7 11.h5 {Luckily, h5 only grabs space but doesn't trap the bishop. The e4-square is available, since the c3-knight is pinned.} 11...Be4 12.Rh3 {12.Rh3 gives up any ever so slight advantage that White might have had. We've got a nice improving move. Nb6 brings the knight into the game, attacks the c4-bishop, and increases our central control.} 12...Nb6 13.Bb3 {Once the bishop retreats, let's get our bishop out of the way and offer a trade.} 13...Bd5 14.Bd2 {They can activate the c1-bishop instead. We have already seen the ...Bb4 concept in response.} 14...Bb4 15.a3 {In this case, our whole idea if White attacks our bishop is to trade.} 15...Bxc3 16.Bxc3 {White can recapture with the bishop and attack our queen.} 16...Qa6 {But we can slide it to a6, where it's completely safe. Next, we'll castle long with an equal position and good light-square control.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Critical 6.Ne5 / Study 1: All Lines / Variation 12"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/73dc795b-ea7d-4d58-b550-3beff4d6f006/studies/8d10428c-92f4-4f96-802c-6f20166d089c/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to Chapter 2 of our course in the Scandinavian. In this chapter, we'll analyze White's critical Ne5 jump on move 6, which has become one of the most consistent weapons against the Qa5 Scandinavian. Let's dive into it.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White takes. We'll take back with the queen, of course.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nc3 {White will most likely develop with tempo on the queen. And, as in the last chapter, we are opting for the Qa5 Scandinavian.} 3...Qa5 4.d4 {The most common is for White to place a pawn in the center, and now their knight is pinned. Let's begin developing pieces with Nf6.} 4...Nf6 5.Nf3 {White activates both knights. Our light-squared bishop has a nice place on f5.} 5...Bf5 6.Ne5 {6.Ne5 is only played 3% of the time in the Lichess database under 2000 ELO, but is played 27% in the Masters database! This is the most testing move from White that we could encounter. We do have the ...c6 resource here, so we can retreat our queen if needed.} 6...c6 7.Bc4 {7.Bc4 is the second most popular move, played 25% of the time, but definitely the most sensible one. White is simply attacking the f7-pawn with two pieces. Of course, the most straightforward defense is ...e6, and the best one.} 7...e6 8.O-O {If White castles, they are basically telling us they want a calm and equal game. Once again, the knight on e5 should be challenged.} 8...Nbd7 9.Qe2 {If they keep the tension, that's fine too. We don't need to capture and let them recapture with tempo.} 9...Rd8 {We can simply get the rook into the game. In some cases, we could even play ...Nb6 to attack both the bishop and the pawn on d4. Development and castling next, either way.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Critical 6.Ne5 / Study 1: All Lines / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/73dc795b-ea7d-4d58-b550-3beff4d6f006/studies/8d10428c-92f4-4f96-802c-6f20166d089c/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to Chapter 2 of our course in the Scandinavian. In this chapter, we'll analyze White's critical Ne5 jump on move 6, which has become one of the most consistent weapons against the Qa5 Scandinavian. Let's dive into it.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White takes. We'll take back with the queen, of course.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nc3 {White will most likely develop with tempo on the queen. And, as in the last chapter, we are opting for the Qa5 Scandinavian.} 3...Qa5 4.d4 {The most common is for White to place a pawn in the center, and now their knight is pinned. Let's begin developing pieces with Nf6.} 4...Nf6 5.Nf3 {White activates both knights. Our light-squared bishop has a nice place on f5.} 5...Bf5 6.Ne5 {6.Ne5 is only played 3% of the time in the Lichess database under 2000 ELO, but is played 27% in the Masters database! This is the most testing move from White that we could encounter. We do have the ...c6 resource here, so we can retreat our queen if needed.} 6...c6 7.Bc4 {7.Bc4 is the second most popular move, played 25% of the time, but definitely the most sensible one. White is simply attacking the f7-pawn with two pieces. Of course, the most straightforward defense is ...e6, and the best one.} 7...e6 8.g4 {For an opponent that's not prepared, g4 is definitely a big surprise! White attacks our bishop and forces us to retreat. This enables them to start a quick kingside pawn storm with tempo.} 8...Bg6 9.h4 {The most logical move for White is to stay consistent in hunting our bishop. We don't have to stop the threat of h5. Instead, we can challenge the knight on e5.} 9...Nbd7 10.Qe2 {If White avoids the trade and defends the knight, that's actually a mistake! We'll take on e5.} 10...Nxe5 11.dxe5 {White takes, and now we can put pressure on a pinned piece. Ne4, moving our attacked knight, is the way forward.} 11...Ne4 12.Rh3 {If Rh3 is played, controlling the third rank, here's a move to remember.} 12...h5 {After ...h5, Black is winning. If they capture, our bishop and our rook will get active. If g5, Bf5 will prove hugely disruptive. Otherwise, we can capture on g4 and if the queen recaptures, there's a skewer on f5!} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Critical 6.Ne5 / Study 1: All Lines / Variation 14"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/73dc795b-ea7d-4d58-b550-3beff4d6f006/studies/8d10428c-92f4-4f96-802c-6f20166d089c/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to Chapter 2 of our course in the Scandinavian. In this chapter, we'll analyze White's critical Ne5 jump on move 6, which has become one of the most consistent weapons against the Qa5 Scandinavian. Let's dive into it.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White takes. We'll take back with the queen, of course.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nc3 {White will most likely develop with tempo on the queen. And, as in the last chapter, we are opting for the Qa5 Scandinavian.} 3...Qa5 4.d4 {The most common is for White to place a pawn in the center, and now their knight is pinned. Let's begin developing pieces with Nf6.} 4...Nf6 5.Nf3 {White activates both knights. Our light-squared bishop has a nice place on f5.} 5...Bf5 6.Ne5 {6.Ne5 is only played 3% of the time in the Lichess database under 2000 ELO, but is played 27% in the Masters database! This is the most testing move from White that we could encounter. We do have the ...c6 resource here, so we can retreat our queen if needed.} 6...c6 7.Nc4 {White retreats the knight 17% of the time. This would have been a problem if hadn't we played ...c6 on the last move. Now, we can just retreat back to d8.} 7...Qd8 8.Be2 {White develops and prepares for castling. Also, because the bishop is on e2, g4 is now in the cards. We should be careful, and prepare a retreating square for our bishop.} 8...h6 9.O-O {White castles...} 9...e6 {And ...e6 is natural to continue development. We'll develop the knight to d7 next, the bishop to either e7 or d6 and castle. A comfortable position for Black.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Critical 6.Ne5 / Study 1: All Lines / Variation 11"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/73dc795b-ea7d-4d58-b550-3beff4d6f006/studies/8d10428c-92f4-4f96-802c-6f20166d089c/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to Chapter 2 of our course in the Scandinavian. In this chapter, we'll analyze White's critical Ne5 jump on move 6, which has become one of the most consistent weapons against the Qa5 Scandinavian. Let's dive into it.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White takes. We'll take back with the queen, of course.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nc3 {White will most likely develop with tempo on the queen. And, as in the last chapter, we are opting for the Qa5 Scandinavian.} 3...Qa5 4.d4 {The most common is for White to place a pawn in the center, and now their knight is pinned. Let's begin developing pieces with Nf6.} 4...Nf6 5.Nf3 {White activates both knights. Our light-squared bishop has a nice place on f5.} 5...Bf5 6.Ne5 {6.Ne5 is only played 3% of the time in the Lichess database under 2000 ELO, but is played 27% in the Masters database! This is the most testing move from White that we could encounter. We do have the ...c6 resource here, so we can retreat our queen if needed.} 6...c6 7.Bc4 {7.Bc4 is the second most popular move, played 25% of the time, but definitely the most sensible one. White is simply attacking the f7-pawn with two pieces. Of course, the most straightforward defense is ...e6, and the best one.} 7...e6 8.g4 {For an opponent that's not prepared, g4 is definitely a big surprise! White attacks our bishop and forces us to retreat. This enables them to start a quick kingside pawn storm with tempo.} 8...Bg6 9.Nxg6 {White capturing now doesn't make too much sense.} 9...hxg6 {They just give us the open h-file for the rook. And we'll gladly take it, and continue by developing the knight and castling long! Now White will have to permanently worry about the h-file.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Critical 6.Ne5 / Study 1: All Lines / Variation 13"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/73dc795b-ea7d-4d58-b550-3beff4d6f006/studies/8d10428c-92f4-4f96-802c-6f20166d089c/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to Chapter 2 of our course in the Scandinavian. In this chapter, we'll analyze White's critical Ne5 jump on move 6, which has become one of the most consistent weapons against the Qa5 Scandinavian. Let's dive into it.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White takes. We'll take back with the queen, of course.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nc3 {White will most likely develop with tempo on the queen. And, as in the last chapter, we are opting for the Qa5 Scandinavian.} 3...Qa5 4.d4 {The most common is for White to place a pawn in the center, and now their knight is pinned. Let's begin developing pieces with Nf6.} 4...Nf6 5.Nf3 {White activates both knights. Our light-squared bishop has a nice place on f5.} 5...Bf5 6.Ne5 {6.Ne5 is only played 3% of the time in the Lichess database under 2000 ELO, but is played 27% in the Masters database! This is the most testing move from White that we could encounter. We do have the ...c6 resource here, so we can retreat our queen if needed.} 6...c6 7.Bc4 {7.Bc4 is the second most popular move, played 25% of the time, but definitely the most sensible one. White is simply attacking the f7-pawn with two pieces. Of course, the most straightforward defense is ...e6, and the best one.} 7...e6 8.Bd2 {White can go here, of course, but it's not scary and the main idea is supposed to be g2-g4. You simply play...} 8...Nbd7 {And if the Nc3 moves, just retreat your queen to c7 or d8.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Other White 5th Moves / Study 1: White Goes 5.Bd2 And Others / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/9888622d-c31d-4e3f-9c96-6e493551cfad/studies/da0adb77-91ac-47b5-b3f2-9ecb1f180fad/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the third chapter. Let's look at a very popular variation: 5. Bd2, placing the bishop opposite the queen right away. Among the 5th move options we'll explore, we'll also look at other less popular ones. Let's get right into it, with 1.e4 d5!} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White captures, as usual. We'll take back with the queen.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nc3 {White usually gains a tempo with Nc3. We slide our queen to a5.} 3...Qa5 4.d4 {White grabs some central squares. Let's start getting our pieces out with Nf6.} 4...Nf6 5.Bd3 {White develops the light-squared bishop. Some players may choose to delay Bd2 by playing another developing move beforehand. Alternatively, White may simply be focused on castling kingside quickly. We'll play the natural developing move Nc6.} 5...Nc6 6.Nf3 {White could protect the d-pawn with Nf3. Our bishop comes into the action now, pinning the knight.} 6...Bg4 7.Be3 {White adds a further protector to the d4-pawn. Let's challenge White's center with the pawn push e5.} 7...e5 8.dxe5 {White's d4-pawn was hanging, so they traded. We recapture with the knight.} 8...Nxe5 9.Be2 {White retreats and defends. Let's grab the open d-file, hitting the white queen!} 9...Rd8 10.Bd2 {White retreats and breaks the pin on the c3-knight. The g4-bishop doesn't have a better use, so let's trade off on f3 and remove the challenge to our strong central knight.} 10...Bxf3 11.Bxf3 {Recapturing.} 11...Bb4 {Black is doing very well here. We are ready to castle, bring the king's rook to the e-file, and try to use our piece activity.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Other White 5th Moves / Study 1: White Goes 5.Bd2 And Others / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/9888622d-c31d-4e3f-9c96-6e493551cfad/studies/da0adb77-91ac-47b5-b3f2-9ecb1f180fad/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the third chapter. Let's look at a very popular variation: 5. Bd2, placing the bishop opposite the queen right away. Among the 5th move options we'll explore, we'll also look at other less popular ones. Let's get right into it, with 1.e4 d5!} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White captures, as usual. We'll take back with the queen.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nc3 {White usually gains a tempo with Nc3. We slide our queen to a5.} 3...Qa5 4.d4 {White grabs some central squares. Let's start getting our pieces out with Nf6.} 4...Nf6 5.Bd3 {White develops the light-squared bishop. Some players may choose to delay Bd2 by playing another developing move beforehand. Alternatively, White may simply be focused on castling kingside quickly. We'll play the natural developing move Nc6.} 5...Nc6 6.Ne2 {White protects the d-pawn with Nge2. White plans to meet ...Bg4 with f3, but that's exactly what we're going for.} 6...Bg4 7.f3 {White attacks our bishop, breaking the pin on the e2-knight.} 7...Bf5 {We'll retreat to f5 and offer a trade. Black aims to castle queenside and add pressure to the d4-pawn.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Other White 5th Moves / Study 1: White Goes 5.Bd2 And Others / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/9888622d-c31d-4e3f-9c96-6e493551cfad/studies/da0adb77-91ac-47b5-b3f2-9ecb1f180fad/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the third chapter. Let's look at a very popular variation: 5. Bd2, placing the bishop opposite the queen right away. Among the 5th move options we'll explore, we'll also look at other less popular ones. Let's get right into it, with 1.e4 d5!} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White captures, as usual. We'll take back with the queen.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nc3 {White usually gains a tempo with Nc3. We slide our queen to a5.} 3...Qa5 4.d4 {White grabs some central squares. Let's start getting our pieces out with Nf6.} 4...Nf6 5.Bd2 {This happens approximately 43% of the time in more than 1.5 million games. White wants to set up a discovered attack on our queen. We can ignore it for now, and develop with tempo instead.} 5...Bg4 6.f3 {White blocks the diagonal and attacks our bishop, which occurs in 28% of the games. We'll just retreat to a safe square, where our bishop won't be targeted. Note that after we retreat, White no longer has f3 for their king's knight.} 6...Bd7 7.Bc4 {White develops the light-squared bishop to c4. Now it's time to get our queen out of the aim of the bishop on d2, while also pressuring an undefended pawn.} 7...Qb6 8.Nge2 {White is developing. Let's open up the dark-squared bishop and control some central squares.} 8...e6 9.Be3 {White once again lines up the bishop with our queen. No worries, the bishop isn't defended, so there isn't any discovery.} 9...Be7 10.Qd3 {White defends the bishop and prepares to castle queenside. Let's focus on development.} 10...Nc6 11.d5 {We shouldn't be worried about d5 here. It looks dangerous, but we can counter-attack the white queen.} 11...Nb4 12.Qd2 {White doesn't want to simplify by taking our queen.} 12...Qxe3 {A cold shower for White! ...Nxc2 will win the queen back and we'll emerge a pawn up!} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Other White 5th Moves / Study 1: White Goes 5.Bd2 And Others / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/9888622d-c31d-4e3f-9c96-6e493551cfad/studies/da0adb77-91ac-47b5-b3f2-9ecb1f180fad/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the third chapter. Let's look at a very popular variation: 5. Bd2, placing the bishop opposite the queen right away. Among the 5th move options we'll explore, we'll also look at other less popular ones. Let's get right into it, with 1.e4 d5!} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White captures, as usual. We'll take back with the queen.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nc3 {White usually gains a tempo with Nc3. We slide our queen to a5.} 3...Qa5 4.d4 {White grabs some central squares. Let's start getting our pieces out with Nf6.} 4...Nf6 5.Bd2 {This happens approximately 43% of the time in more than 1.5 million games. White wants to set up a discovered attack on our queen. We can ignore it for now, and develop with tempo instead.} 5...Bg4 6.f3 {White blocks the diagonal and attacks our bishop, which occurs in 28% of the games. We'll just retreat to a safe square, where our bishop won't be targeted. Note that after we retreat, White no longer has f3 for their king's knight.} 6...Bd7 7.Bc4 {White develops the light-squared bishop to c4. Now it's time to get our queen out of the aim of the bishop on d2, while also pressuring an undefended pawn.} 7...Qb6 8.Nge2 {White is developing. Let's open up the dark-squared bishop and control some central squares.} 8...e6 9.Be3 {White once again lines up the bishop with our queen. No worries, the bishop isn't defended, so there isn't any discovery.} 9...Be7 10.Qd3 {White defends the bishop and prepares to castle queenside. Let's focus on development.} 10...Nc6 11.O-O-O {White gets their king out of the center. We have a great square for our knight now.} 11...Nb4 12.Qd2 {The queen retreats. And now we get out of the e3-bishop's line of fire and attack the c4-bishop.} 12...Qc6 13.Bb3 {White retreats.} 13...a5 {And we expand on the queenside with ...a5. We are ready to castle short, expand further with a4 and we have good control over the d5-square.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Other White 5th Moves / Study 1: White Goes 5.Bd2 And Others / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/9888622d-c31d-4e3f-9c96-6e493551cfad/studies/da0adb77-91ac-47b5-b3f2-9ecb1f180fad/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the third chapter. Let's look at a very popular variation: 5. Bd2, placing the bishop opposite the queen right away. Among the 5th move options we'll explore, we'll also look at other less popular ones. Let's get right into it, with 1.e4 d5!} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White captures, as usual. We'll take back with the queen.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nc3 {White usually gains a tempo with Nc3. We slide our queen to a5.} 3...Qa5 4.d4 {White grabs some central squares. Let's start getting our pieces out with Nf6.} 4...Nf6 5.Bd2 {This happens approximately 43% of the time in more than 1.5 million games. White wants to set up a discovered attack on our queen. We can ignore it for now, and develop with tempo instead.} 5...Bg4 6.Be2 {White offers to trade the light-squared bishops, which is the most common move here (36%). We'll accept.} 6...Bxe2 7.Qxe2 {White recaptures with the queen, so that Nf3 will be possible. Obviously both knight captures are also fine, but we can simply finish our development and get your king to safety. Also don't blunder the queen after Ncxe2! Here we'll play Nc6 and attack the d-pawn.} 7...Nc6 8.Nf3 {White defends it. Now we play the standard move e6 to control the center and open up the bishop.} 8...e6 9.O-O {White castles and connects the rooks.} 9...Qf5 {We bring our queen to a more active square. We are ready to castle queenside and put more pressure on the d4-pawn.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Other White 5th Moves / Study 1: White Goes 5.Bd2 And Others / Variation 3"]
