1.e4 {Welcome to the third chapter. Let's look at a very popular variation: 5. Bd2, placing the bishop opposite the queen right away. Among the 5th move options we'll explore, we'll also look at other less popular ones. Let's get right into it, with 1.e4 d5!} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White captures, as usual. We'll take back with the queen.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nc3 {White usually gains a tempo with Nc3. We slide our queen to a5.} 3...Qa5 4.d4 {White grabs some central squares. Let's start getting our pieces out with Nf6.} 4...Nf6 5.Bd2 {This happens approximately 43% of the time in more than 1.5 million games. White wants to set up a discovered attack on our queen. We can ignore it for now, and develop with tempo instead.} 5...Bg4 6.f3 {White blocks the diagonal and attacks our bishop, which occurs in 28% of the games. We'll just retreat to a safe square, where our bishop won't be targeted. Note that after we retreat, White no longer has f3 for their king's knight.} 6...Bd7 7.Bc4 {White develops the light-squared bishop to c4. Now it's time to get our queen out of the aim of the bishop on d2, while also pressuring an undefended pawn.} 7...Qb6 8.Nge2 {White is developing. Let's open up the dark-squared bishop and control some central squares.} 8...e6 9.Be3 {White once again lines up the bishop with our queen. No worries, the bishop isn't defended, so there isn't any discovery.} 9...Be7 10.Qd2 {White defends the bishop with Qd2 and aims to play d5 in the future. Let's fearlessly grab the b2-pawn!} 10...Qxb2 11.Rb1 {White attacks our queen. Our next move is forced.} 11...Qa3 12.Rxb7 {White regains the b-pawn. We can simply castle and get our king into safety.} 12...O-O 13.O-O {White castles also. Grabbing on c7 wouldn't end well after Ne8 14.Rb7 Nd6 and the rook finds itself roaming in the wilderness.} 13...Nc6 {White's bishops aren't doing much, and the d5-square is under our control.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Other White 5th Moves / Study 1: White Goes 5.Bd2 And Others / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/9888622d-c31d-4e3f-9c96-6e493551cfad/studies/da0adb77-91ac-47b5-b3f2-9ecb1f180fad/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the third chapter. Let's look at a very popular variation: 5. Bd2, placing the bishop opposite the queen right away. Among the 5th move options we'll explore, we'll also look at other less popular ones. Let's get right into it, with 1.e4 d5!} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White captures, as usual. We'll take back with the queen.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nc3 {White usually gains a tempo with Nc3. We slide our queen to a5.} 3...Qa5 4.d4 {White grabs some central squares. Let's start getting our pieces out with Nf6.} 4...Nf6 5.Bd2 {This happens approximately 43% of the time in more than 1.5 million games. White wants to set up a discovered attack on our queen. We can ignore it for now, and develop with tempo instead.} 5...Bg4 6.Nf3 {White blocks the attack by developing the knight, which happens 28% of the time. We'll put further pressure on the knight by bringing our queen to f5.} 6...Qf5 7.Bd3 {White attacks our queen, which is the second most popular move (26%). We can go ahead and remove the knight on f3, counter-attacking White's queen.} 7...Bxf3 8.Bxf5 {White captures our queen. Naturally, we will capture theirs in return.} 8...Bxd1 9.Rxd1 {White captures the bishop. Now the common idea of ...e6 comes with tempo on the f5-bishop!} 9...e6 10.Bd3 {The bishop retreats.} 10...Nc6 {And c6 is once again a good square for the knight. Here it is eyeing both the d4-pawn and the b4-square.} *

