1.e4 {Welcome to the third chapter. Let's look at a very popular variation: 5. Bd2, placing the bishop opposite the queen right away. Among the 5th move options we'll explore, we'll also look at other less popular ones. Let's get right into it, with 1.e4 d5!} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White captures, as usual. We'll take back with the queen.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nc3 {White usually gains a tempo with Nc3. We slide our queen to a5.} 3...Qa5 4.d4 {White grabs some central squares. Let's start getting our pieces out with Nf6.} 4...Nf6 5.Bd2 {This happens approximately 43% of the time in more than 1.5 million games. White wants to set up a discovered attack on our queen. We can ignore it for now, and develop with tempo instead.} 5...Bg4 6.Nf3 {White blocks the attack by developing the knight, which happens 28% of the time. We'll put further pressure on the knight by bringing our queen to f5.} 6...Qf5 7.Bd3 {White attacks our queen, which is the second most popular move (26%). We can go ahead and remove the knight on f3, counter-attacking White's queen.} 7...Bxf3 8.gxf3 {White grabs the bishop instead.} 8...Qd7 {But ...Qd7 with ...e6 and ...Nc6 will put pressure on the d4-pawn. White's kingside pawns are badly fractured.} *

