1.e4 {Welcome to the third chapter. Let's look at a very popular variation: 5. Bd2, placing the bishop opposite the queen right away. Among the 5th move options we'll explore, we'll also look at other less popular ones. Let's get right into it, with 1.e4 d5!} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White captures, as usual. We'll take back with the queen.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nc3 {White usually gains a tempo with Nc3. We slide our queen to a5.} 3...Qa5 4.d4 {White grabs some central squares. Let's start getting our pieces out with Nf6.} 4...Nf6 5.Bd2 {This happens approximately 43% of the time in more than 1.5 million games. White wants to set up a discovered attack on our queen. We can ignore it for now, and develop with tempo instead.} 5...Bg4 6.Nf3 {White blocks the attack by developing the knight, which happens 28% of the time. We'll put further pressure on the knight by bringing our queen to f5.} 6...Qf5 7.Be2 {This is the most common move here with a 79% occurrence — supporting the f3-knight.} 7...Nc6 {We'll simply develop and go for ... O-O-O with ...e5 to come.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Other White 5th Moves / Study 1: White Goes 5.Bd2 And Others / Variation 10"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/9888622d-c31d-4e3f-9c96-6e493551cfad/studies/da0adb77-91ac-47b5-b3f2-9ecb1f180fad/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the third chapter. Let's look at a very popular variation: 5. Bd2, placing the bishop opposite the queen right away. Among the 5th move options we'll explore, we'll also look at other less popular ones. Let's get right into it, with 1.e4 d5!} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White captures, as usual. We'll take back with the queen.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nc3 {White usually gains a tempo with Nc3. We slide our queen to a5.} 3...Qa5 4.d4 {White grabs some central squares. Let's start getting our pieces out with Nf6.} 4...Nf6 5.Bf4 {White goes for a move which doesn't happen often, but is still a natural developing move. We'll occupy the usual square and attack the queen with our bishop.} 5...Bg4 6.Be2 {White offers a trade of bishops. We'll accept. Retreating wouldn't give us any benefits.} 6...Bxe2 7.Nxe2 {White recaptures.} 7...Nc6 {And as usual, we'll bring the knight to c6, castle queenside and put pressure on the d4-pawn.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: White Doesn't Play Nf3 / Study 1: White Plays 6.f3 or 6.Nf3 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/2845d388-da50-45a7-9635-3f74f5d4aca2/studies/8d88ccd1-2069-4425-a782-4b9154197c24/lines"]

1.e4 {It's time to analyze positions after 5. Bc4 by White in the Qa5 Scandinavian. We'll attack the queen right away with 5... Bg4. In this study, we'll see what to do if White plays f3 or Nf3 on their sixth move. These two are the most popular ways to block our attack on the queen, and I've prepared creative ways to counter both of them. Let's get started with the opening moves 1.e4 d5.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White takes. We'll recapture with the queen.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nc3 {White activates the knight with tempo... We'll slide the queen to a5.} 3...Qa5 4.d4 {Expanding in the center is natural. We'll bring out our king's knight.} 4...Nf6 5.Bc4 {A popular move instead of 5. Nf3 is Bc4, developing the bishop to the most active square. This has been played around 8% of times in more than 300,000 games. If we play in the most natural way, White will develop the knight to f3 and then the bishop to d2. However, here's where we spice things up. We bring our bishop out, attacking the white queen!} 5...Bg4 6.Nf3 {Around 25% of games go like this. White is blocking the attack but also developing. Let's continue to develop.} 6...Nc6 7.h3 {White may attempt to get rid of the pin quickly. This is one of the best approaches, but we have a strong response, since White hasn't castled yet. Qh5 pins the h-pawn to the king's rook.} 7...Qh5 8.Be2 {White may try to break the pin on the knight. We can now get our king into safety and pressure the d4-pawn.} 8...O-O-O 9.O-O {White may castle here. This is a logical move because it removes the h-file pin and revives the threat on the bishop. But we don't have to respond because we have the clever move of taking on d4.} 9...Nxd4 10.Nxd4 {White takes on d4. We'll go for the trade of bishops here. White's coordination is damaged.} 10...Bxe2 11.Qxe2 {If the queen takes, the knight on d4 lacks defense.} 11...Rxd4 12.Qxh5 {White trades.} 12...Nxh5 {And we recapture. We have a slight edge thanks to the central pawn that we're up.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: White Doesn't Play Nf3 / Study 1: White Plays 6.f3 or 6.Nf3 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/2845d388-da50-45a7-9635-3f74f5d4aca2/studies/8d88ccd1-2069-4425-a782-4b9154197c24/lines"]

1.e4 {It's time to analyze positions after 5. Bc4 by White in the Qa5 Scandinavian. We'll attack the queen right away with 5... Bg4. In this study, we'll see what to do if White plays f3 or Nf3 on their sixth move. These two are the most popular ways to block our attack on the queen, and I've prepared creative ways to counter both of them. Let's get started with the opening moves 1.e4 d5.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White takes. We'll recapture with the queen.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nc3 {White activates the knight with tempo... We'll slide the queen to a5.} 3...Qa5 4.d4 {Expanding in the center is natural. We'll bring out our king's knight.} 4...Nf6 5.Bc4 {A popular move instead of 5. Nf3 is Bc4, developing the bishop to the most active square. This has been played around 8% of times in more than 300,000 games. If we play in the most natural way, White will develop the knight to f3 and then the bishop to d2. However, here's where we spice things up. We bring our bishop out, attacking the white queen!} 5...Bg4 6.Nf3 {Around 25% of games go like this. White is blocking the attack but also developing. Let's continue to develop.} 6...Nc6 7.h3 {White may attempt to get rid of the pin quickly. This is one of the best approaches, but we have a strong response, since White hasn't castled yet. Qh5 pins the h-pawn to the king's rook.} 7...Qh5 8.Be2 {White may try to break the pin on the knight. We can now get our king into safety and pressure the d4-pawn.} 8...O-O-O 9.Be3 {White could try to overprotect d4. This strategy often fails. Our rook is nicely placed on the d-file, so we are ready to strike in the center with e5.} 9...e5 10.d5 {White has to push. But they are not even threatening to take our knight! Let's get our last piece out into the game and pin the c3-knight.} 10...Bb4 11.O-O {White castles. This puts us on the spot because the g4-bishop is hanging and our queen may not be in the safest of squares. Also, the c3-knight is no longer pinned. We have the enterprising sacrifice Bxh3, and a chaotic position results.} 11...Bxh3 12.gxh3 {White doesn't have any useful discoveries, so they take the piece. Their king will be exposed in this middlegame, and we'll attack it. We'll now capture on c3, removing d5's defender.} 12...Bxc3 13.bxc3 {White recaptures. We don't want to blunder with Qxh3 because of ...Ng5 tactics. Instead, we'll activate our rook and capture on d5.} 13...Rxd5 14.Qe1 {The queen goes to e1. We can gain a tempo on the knight with e4. Our aim is to remove some defenders of the king.} 14...e4 15.Nh2 {White's best move is to keep the knight on the kingside. Taking on h3 is now safe and it's the best square for our queen.} 15...Qxh3 16.Kh1 {This line is for illustration. 16. Kh1 is tricky to find. We'll play h5. The idea is to support the coming attack with Ng4.} 16...h5 17.Rg1 {The rook goes to g1. White wants to defend nearer the king and maybe propose a trade or play Bf1 to eject the queen. We'll attack the pinned knight with Ng4.} 17...Ng4 18.Bf4 {This is the best defense of the knight. Instead, Rg2 allows ...Nce5 with much more pressure.} 18...Nce5 {We're constantly posing challenges and bringing more pieces to the kingside. If White ever takes on g4, they'll open up our rook on h8. The position is equal because the engine finds incredible resources to defend, but your opponents are either lucky or extremely well-prepared if they manage to last this long!} *

[Event "Chapter 4: White Doesn't Play Nf3 / Study 1: White Plays 6.f3 or 6.Nf3 / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/2845d388-da50-45a7-9635-3f74f5d4aca2/studies/8d88ccd1-2069-4425-a782-4b9154197c24/lines"]

1.e4 {It's time to analyze positions after 5. Bc4 by White in the Qa5 Scandinavian. We'll attack the queen right away with 5... Bg4. In this study, we'll see what to do if White plays f3 or Nf3 on their sixth move. These two are the most popular ways to block our attack on the queen, and I've prepared creative ways to counter both of them. Let's get started with the opening moves 1.e4 d5.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White takes. We'll recapture with the queen.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nc3 {White activates the knight with tempo... We'll slide the queen to a5.} 3...Qa5 4.d4 {Expanding in the center is natural. We'll bring out our king's knight.} 4...Nf6 5.Bc4 {A popular move instead of 5. Nf3 is Bc4, developing the bishop to the most active square. This has been played around 8% of times in more than 300,000 games. If we play in the most natural way, White will develop the knight to f3 and then the bishop to d2. However, here's where we spice things up. We bring our bishop out, attacking the white queen!} 5...Bg4 6.Nf3 {Around 25% of games go like this. White is blocking the attack but also developing. Let's continue to develop.} 6...Nc6 7.h3 {White may attempt to get rid of the pin quickly. This is one of the best approaches, but we have a strong response, since White hasn't castled yet. Qh5 pins the h-pawn to the king's rook.} 7...Qh5 8.Be3 {8. Be3 is a popular attempt to overprotect the d4-pawn. However, we can nevertheless castle: ...e5 is coming. White may all of a sudden start feeling discomfort in this position. A pin on the knight, on the h-file and on the d-file!} 8...O-O-O 9.Qe2 {Many people may resort to Qe2, taking the queen off the d-file. But a natural-looking move like Qe2 loses. We'll play e5. They might have escaped the d-file, but they didn't escape the pin on the knight.} 9...e5 10.O-O-O {If White castles, thinking they revive a threat on our bishop on g4, they will lose material.} 10...Bxf3 {We first remove a defender of d4. We're ready to play a fork on d4 and go up material. We are completely winning!} *

[Event "Chapter 4: White Doesn't Play Nf3 / Study 1: White Plays 6.f3 or 6.Nf3 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/2845d388-da50-45a7-9635-3f74f5d4aca2/studies/8d88ccd1-2069-4425-a782-4b9154197c24/lines"]

1.e4 {It's time to analyze positions after 5. Bc4 by White in the Qa5 Scandinavian. We'll attack the queen right away with 5... Bg4. In this study, we'll see what to do if White plays f3 or Nf3 on their sixth move. These two are the most popular ways to block our attack on the queen, and I've prepared creative ways to counter both of them. Let's get started with the opening moves 1.e4 d5.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White takes. We'll recapture with the queen.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nc3 {White activates the knight with tempo... We'll slide the queen to a5.} 3...Qa5 4.d4 {Expanding in the center is natural. We'll bring out our king's knight.} 4...Nf6 5.Bc4 {A popular move instead of 5. Nf3 is Bc4, developing the bishop to the most active square. This has been played around 8% of times in more than 300,000 games. If we play in the most natural way, White will develop the knight to f3 and then the bishop to d2. However, here's where we spice things up. We bring our bishop out, attacking the white queen!} 5...Bg4 6.Nf3 {Around 25% of games go like this. White is blocking the attack but also developing. Let's continue to develop.} 6...Nc6 7.h3 {White may attempt to get rid of the pin quickly. This is one of the best approaches, but we have a strong response, since White hasn't castled yet. Qh5 pins the h-pawn to the king's rook.} 7...Qh5 8.Be2 {White may try to break the pin on the knight. We can now get our king into safety and pressure the d4-pawn.} 8...O-O-O 9.O-O {White may castle here. This is a logical move because it removes the h-file pin and revives the threat on the bishop. But we don't have to respond because we have the clever move of taking on d4.} 9...Nxd4 10.Nxd4 {White takes on d4. We'll go for the trade of bishops here. White's coordination is damaged.} 10...Bxe2 11.Ncxe2 {The knight captures back.} 11...e5 {But we have a pin. If 12. Be3 we've got 12... Bc5. This is sound for us. White will likely regain their pawn, but we've successfully developed all our pieces.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: White Doesn't Play Nf3 / Study 1: White Plays 6.f3 or 6.Nf3 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/2845d388-da50-45a7-9635-3f74f5d4aca2/studies/8d88ccd1-2069-4425-a782-4b9154197c24/lines"]

1.e4 {It's time to analyze positions after 5. Bc4 by White in the Qa5 Scandinavian. We'll attack the queen right away with 5... Bg4. In this study, we'll see what to do if White plays f3 or Nf3 on their sixth move. These two are the most popular ways to block our attack on the queen, and I've prepared creative ways to counter both of them. Let's get started with the opening moves 1.e4 d5.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White takes. We'll recapture with the queen.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nc3 {White activates the knight with tempo... We'll slide the queen to a5.} 3...Qa5 4.d4 {Expanding in the center is natural. We'll bring out our king's knight.} 4...Nf6 5.Bc4 {A popular move instead of 5. Nf3 is Bc4, developing the bishop to the most active square. This has been played around 8% of times in more than 300,000 games. If we play in the most natural way, White will develop the knight to f3 and then the bishop to d2. However, here's where we spice things up. We bring our bishop out, attacking the white queen!} 5...Bg4 6.Nf3 {Around 25% of games go like this. White is blocking the attack but also developing. Let's continue to develop.} 6...Nc6 7.O-O {You'll most likely see White castle here. This is already bad for them because in the long term White will not be able to defend the d4-pawn. We'll castle and increase the pressure on the pawn.} 7...O-O-O 8.d5 {Black has a pleasant advantage.} 8...e6 {There's no way White can keep their pawn. You should go on to convert this.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: White Doesn't Play Nf3 / Study 1: White Plays 6.f3 or 6.Nf3 / Variation 10"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/2845d388-da50-45a7-9635-3f74f5d4aca2/studies/8d88ccd1-2069-4425-a782-4b9154197c24/lines"]

1.e4 {It's time to analyze positions after 5. Bc4 by White in the Qa5 Scandinavian. We'll attack the queen right away with 5... Bg4. In this study, we'll see what to do if White plays f3 or Nf3 on their sixth move. These two are the most popular ways to block our attack on the queen, and I've prepared creative ways to counter both of them. Let's get started with the opening moves 1.e4 d5.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White takes. We'll recapture with the queen.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nc3 {White activates the knight with tempo... We'll slide the queen to a5.} 3...Qa5 4.d4 {Expanding in the center is natural. We'll bring out our king's knight.} 4...Nf6 5.Bc4 {A popular move instead of 5. Nf3 is Bc4, developing the bishop to the most active square. This has been played around 8% of times in more than 300,000 games. If we play in the most natural way, White will develop the knight to f3 and then the bishop to d2. However, here's where we spice things up. We bring our bishop out, attacking the white queen!} 5...Bg4 6.Nf3 {Around 25% of games go like this. White is blocking the attack but also developing. Let's continue to develop.} 6...Nc6 7.Be3 {White may have the idea of overprotecting d4. However, this passive approach does not work. We have a very active resource here, and we push e5.} 7...e5 8.dxe5 {8. dxe5 allows our c6-knight to get right into the action. Our rook also gets a clear d-file. We'll recapture, with pressure on the knight and bishop.} 8...Nxe5 9.Be2 {If White breaks the pin and protects the knight more, we can continue improving our pieces with tempo.} 9...Rd8 {Our powerful rook enters the d-file. The queen has to move away and no squares are desirable. If White blocks with 10.Bd2 we can simply keep developing our pieces.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: White Doesn't Play Nf3 / Study 1: White Plays 6.f3 or 6.Nf3 / Variation 11"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/2845d388-da50-45a7-9635-3f74f5d4aca2/studies/8d88ccd1-2069-4425-a782-4b9154197c24/lines"]

1.e4 {It's time to analyze positions after 5. Bc4 by White in the Qa5 Scandinavian. We'll attack the queen right away with 5... Bg4. In this study, we'll see what to do if White plays f3 or Nf3 on their sixth move. These two are the most popular ways to block our attack on the queen, and I've prepared creative ways to counter both of them. Let's get started with the opening moves 1.e4 d5.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White takes. We'll recapture with the queen.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nc3 {White activates the knight with tempo... We'll slide the queen to a5.} 3...Qa5 4.d4 {Expanding in the center is natural. We'll bring out our king's knight.} 4...Nf6 5.Bc4 {A popular move instead of 5. Nf3 is Bc4, developing the bishop to the most active square. This has been played around 8% of times in more than 300,000 games. If we play in the most natural way, White will develop the knight to f3 and then the bishop to d2. However, here's where we spice things up. We bring our bishop out, attacking the white queen!} 5...Bg4 6.Nf3 {Around 25% of games go like this. White is blocking the attack but also developing. Let's continue to develop.} 6...Nc6 7.Be3 {White may have the idea of overprotecting d4. However, this passive approach does not work. We have a very active resource here, and we push e5.} 7...e5 8.d5 {White may be tempted to push the d-pawn.} 8...O-O-O {But they simply do not have the firepower to defend the pawn. We have ideas of ...e4 and if Qc1 we can easily win d5.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: White Doesn't Play Nf3 / Study 1: White Plays 6.f3 or 6.Nf3 / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/2845d388-da50-45a7-9635-3f74f5d4aca2/studies/8d88ccd1-2069-4425-a782-4b9154197c24/lines"]

1.e4 {It's time to analyze positions after 5. Bc4 by White in the Qa5 Scandinavian. We'll attack the queen right away with 5... Bg4. In this study, we'll see what to do if White plays f3 or Nf3 on their sixth move. These two are the most popular ways to block our attack on the queen, and I've prepared creative ways to counter both of them. Let's get started with the opening moves 1.e4 d5.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White takes. We'll recapture with the queen.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nc3 {White activates the knight with tempo... We'll slide the queen to a5.} 3...Qa5 4.d4 {Expanding in the center is natural. We'll bring out our king's knight.} 4...Nf6 5.Bc4 {A popular move instead of 5. Nf3 is Bc4, developing the bishop to the most active square. This has been played around 8% of times in more than 300,000 games. If we play in the most natural way, White will develop the knight to f3 and then the bishop to d2. However, here's where we spice things up. We bring our bishop out, attacking the white queen!} 5...Bg4 6.Nf3 {Around 25% of games go like this. White is blocking the attack but also developing. Let's continue to develop.} 6...Nc6 7.Bd2 {White may decide to place the bishop on d2 at this point. But there are still many squares our queen can go to, so there are no issues.} 7...O-O-O {Hence, we should simply castle. Our rook is placed on the d-file, with pressure on d4. White may get into trouble quickly. Our queen is always safe - White can move the c3-knight to target it with a discovery, but our queen won't get trapped.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: White Doesn't Play Nf3 / Study 1: White Plays 6.f3 or 6.Nf3 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/2845d388-da50-45a7-9635-3f74f5d4aca2/studies/8d88ccd1-2069-4425-a782-4b9154197c24/lines"]

1.e4 {It's time to analyze positions after 5. Bc4 by White in the Qa5 Scandinavian. We'll attack the queen right away with 5... Bg4. In this study, we'll see what to do if White plays f3 or Nf3 on their sixth move. These two are the most popular ways to block our attack on the queen, and I've prepared creative ways to counter both of them. Let's get started with the opening moves 1.e4 d5.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White takes. We'll recapture with the queen.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nc3 {White activates the knight with tempo... We'll slide the queen to a5.} 3...Qa5 4.d4 {Expanding in the center is natural. We'll bring out our king's knight.} 4...Nf6 5.Bc4 {A popular move instead of 5. Nf3 is Bc4, developing the bishop to the most active square. This has been played around 8% of times in more than 300,000 games. If we play in the most natural way, White will develop the knight to f3 and then the bishop to d2. However, here's where we spice things up. We bring our bishop out, attacking the white queen!} 5...Bg4 6.f3 {f3 is the most common move, just like in Chapter 3. It has been played around more than 50% of time. Our bishop will be solid on d7.} 6...Bd7 7.Ne2 {This is played nearly 48% of the time. If White instead plays 7.Bd2, it transposes to the next study. I recommend challenging the center with ...c5.} 7...c5 8.d5 {The reason we want to see d5 on the board is that the pawn is overextended. Our pawns will now overrun White on the queenside.} 8...b5 9.Bd3 {The bishop avoids its demise by going to d3. Our plan is to remove all of the defenders of the d-pawn to undermine it with greater effect. We start by attacking the bishop once more.} 9...c4 10.Be4 {The bishop goes to e4. Let's continue the queenside expansion with b4!} 10...b4 11.Nb1 {White's knight is forced to a bad square. Now it's time to get rid of White's best-placed piece.} 11...Nxe4 12.fxe4 {Even though White adds a defender to the d-pawn, this doesn't solve White's overextended pawn placement.} 12...e6 {The undermining process begins. We attack d5 and get ready to bring our dark-squared bishop into the game.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: White Doesn't Play Nf3 / Study 1: White Plays 6.f3 or 6.Nf3 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/2845d388-da50-45a7-9635-3f74f5d4aca2/studies/8d88ccd1-2069-4425-a782-4b9154197c24/lines"]

1.e4 {It's time to analyze positions after 5. Bc4 by White in the Qa5 Scandinavian. We'll attack the queen right away with 5... Bg4. In this study, we'll see what to do if White plays f3 or Nf3 on their sixth move. These two are the most popular ways to block our attack on the queen, and I've prepared creative ways to counter both of them. Let's get started with the opening moves 1.e4 d5.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White takes. We'll recapture with the queen.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nc3 {White activates the knight with tempo... We'll slide the queen to a5.} 3...Qa5 4.d4 {Expanding in the center is natural. We'll bring out our king's knight.} 4...Nf6 5.Bc4 {A popular move instead of 5. Nf3 is Bc4, developing the bishop to the most active square. This has been played around 8% of times in more than 300,000 games. If we play in the most natural way, White will develop the knight to f3 and then the bishop to d2. However, here's where we spice things up. We bring our bishop out, attacking the white queen!} 5...Bg4 6.f3 {f3 is the most common move, just like in Chapter 3. It has been played around more than 50% of time. Our bishop will be solid on d7.} 6...Bd7 7.Ne2 {This is played nearly 48% of the time. If White instead plays 7.Bd2, it transposes to the next study. I recommend challenging the center with ...c5.} 7...c5 8.Be3 {White could add another piece into the mix and further defend d4. But as long as we can remove White's central pawn, we're satisfied with our position.} 8...cxd4 9.Qxd4 {The queen recaptures. White's pieces are all out and only their rooks need to be developed. We'll play e6 and prepare to activate our f8-bishop.} 9...e6 10.O-O-O {White castles, with some pressure on the d-file.} 10...Be7 {That's why we'll prepare castling. We'll of course delay Nc6 until we can make sure it's safe. If we can get a queen trade after something like Nc6 we'll take it without hesitation.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: White Doesn't Play Nf3 / Study 1: White Plays 6.f3 or 6.Nf3 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/2845d388-da50-45a7-9635-3f74f5d4aca2/studies/8d88ccd1-2069-4425-a782-4b9154197c24/lines"]

1.e4 {It's time to analyze positions after 5. Bc4 by White in the Qa5 Scandinavian. We'll attack the queen right away with 5... Bg4. In this study, we'll see what to do if White plays f3 or Nf3 on their sixth move. These two are the most popular ways to block our attack on the queen, and I've prepared creative ways to counter both of them. Let's get started with the opening moves 1.e4 d5.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White takes. We'll recapture with the queen.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nc3 {White activates the knight with tempo... We'll slide the queen to a5.} 3...Qa5 4.d4 {Expanding in the center is natural. We'll bring out our king's knight.} 4...Nf6 5.Bc4 {A popular move instead of 5. Nf3 is Bc4, developing the bishop to the most active square. This has been played around 8% of times in more than 300,000 games. If we play in the most natural way, White will develop the knight to f3 and then the bishop to d2. However, here's where we spice things up. We bring our bishop out, attacking the white queen!} 5...Bg4 6.f3 {f3 is the most common move, just like in Chapter 3. It has been played around more than 50% of time. Our bishop will be solid on d7.} 6...Bd7 7.Ne2 {This is played nearly 48% of the time. If White instead plays 7.Bd2, it transposes to the next study. I recommend challenging the center with ...c5.} 7...c5 8.O-O {It's possible that White may ignore the central tension. This is reasonable because the pawn has sufficient defenders, but we should take nevertheless.} 8...cxd4 9.Nxd4 {White recaptures.} 9...e6 {And we block the bishop's view of f7, while opening a diagonal for our other bishop. We are about to complete development and we're the only side left with a central pawn, the e-pawn. We've equalized with the black pieces in the Scandinavian!} *

[Event "Chapter 4: White Doesn't Play Nf3 / Study 2: White Plays 6.Ne2 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/2845d388-da50-45a7-9635-3f74f5d4aca2/studies/95c39777-a496-4986-a68f-c377694b6ee8/lines"]

1.e4 {This study introduces our subject with White blocking the attack with 6. Ne2. Our bishop isn't forced back right away: we'll develop our queenside knight with 6... Nc6, but this alters things a bit. We've got a more dynamic position after that. We'll start with 1.e4 d5.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White takes. We'll recapture with the queen.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nc3 {White activates the knight with tempo... And we'll move the queen to a5.} 3...Qa5 4.d4 {Expanding in the center comes naturally. Let's develop the g8-knight.} 4...Nf6 5.Bc4 {A popular move instead of 5. Nf3 is Bc4, developing the bishop to the most active square. This has been played around 8% of times in more than 300,000 games. If we play in the most natural way, White will develop the knight to f3 and the bishop to d2 next. However, here's where we spice things up with rapid development. Bg4 hits the white queen.} 5...Bg4 6.Ne2 {This is a weird maneuver, but it's not bad. It has happened in about 16% of games. Yet again, the plan of ...Nc6 followed by queenside castling is strong.} 6...Nc6 7.f3 {This is quite disruptive for our coordination because we don't want to play Bd7 here. But we don't mind retreating to e6. The doubled e-pawns will prove to be an asset.} 7...Be6 8.Bxe6 {The most challenging and critical move is White capturing on e6. We'll recapture with the idea of ...e5 later.} 8...fxe6 9.Nf4 {White could try go for our e6-pawn instantly, thinking it's a weakness they could easily win. But we're prepared for that, of course. Castling queenside will likely be a surprise to the white player!} 9...O-O-O 10.Nxe6 {If White captures...} 10...Rd6 {We've got a rook lift with tempo, attacking the knight. If White takes our bishop, we recapture and double our rooks, ready to smash the center. White is in trouble here. If we need, we'll also add Qf5.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: White Doesn't Play Nf3 / Study 2: White Plays 6.Ne2 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/2845d388-da50-45a7-9635-3f74f5d4aca2/studies/95c39777-a496-4986-a68f-c377694b6ee8/lines"]

1.e4 {This study introduces our subject with White blocking the attack with 6. Ne2. Our bishop isn't forced back right away: we'll develop our queenside knight with 6... Nc6, but this alters things a bit. We've got a more dynamic position after that. We'll start with 1.e4 d5.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White takes. We'll recapture with the queen.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nc3 {White activates the knight with tempo... And we'll move the queen to a5.} 3...Qa5 4.d4 {Expanding in the center comes naturally. Let's develop the g8-knight.} 4...Nf6 5.Bc4 {A popular move instead of 5. Nf3 is Bc4, developing the bishop to the most active square. This has been played around 8% of times in more than 300,000 games. If we play in the most natural way, White will develop the knight to f3 and the bishop to d2 next. However, here's where we spice things up with rapid development. Bg4 hits the white queen.} 5...Bg4 6.Ne2 {This is a weird maneuver, but it's not bad. It has happened in about 16% of games. Yet again, the plan of ...Nc6 followed by queenside castling is strong.} 6...Nc6 7.f3 {This is quite disruptive for our coordination because we don't want to play Bd7 here. But we don't mind retreating to e6. The doubled e-pawns will prove to be an asset.} 7...Be6 8.Bxe6 {The most challenging and critical move is White capturing on e6. We'll recapture with the idea of ...e5 later.} 8...fxe6 9.O-O {White castling gives us the green light to move forward with our plans. We'll castle queenside, and now e5 is in the air.} 9...O-O-O 10.f4 {White can stop us from playing e5, in which case we will play...} 10...g6 {This. We can fianchetto the dark-squared bishop, which will serve as another attacker of the d4-pawn. All in all, an unclear position. The good thing is that if f4 is occupied by a pawn, it cannot be occupied by a piece. White cannot play Nf4 to attack the e6-pawn.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: White Doesn't Play Nf3 / Study 2: White Plays 6.Ne2 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/2845d388-da50-45a7-9635-3f74f5d4aca2/studies/95c39777-a496-4986-a68f-c377694b6ee8/lines"]

1.e4 {This study introduces our subject with White blocking the attack with 6. Ne2. Our bishop isn't forced back right away: we'll develop our queenside knight with 6... Nc6, but this alters things a bit. We've got a more dynamic position after that. We'll start with 1.e4 d5.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White takes. We'll recapture with the queen.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nc3 {White activates the knight with tempo... And we'll move the queen to a5.} 3...Qa5 4.d4 {Expanding in the center comes naturally. Let's develop the g8-knight.} 4...Nf6 5.Bc4 {A popular move instead of 5. Nf3 is Bc4, developing the bishop to the most active square. This has been played around 8% of times in more than 300,000 games. If we play in the most natural way, White will develop the knight to f3 and the bishop to d2 next. However, here's where we spice things up with rapid development. Bg4 hits the white queen.} 5...Bg4 6.Ne2 {This is a weird maneuver, but it's not bad. It has happened in about 16% of games. Yet again, the plan of ...Nc6 followed by queenside castling is strong.} 6...Nc6 7.f3 {This is quite disruptive for our coordination because we don't want to play Bd7 here. But we don't mind retreating to e6. The doubled e-pawns will prove to be an asset.} 7...Be6 8.Bxe6 {The most challenging and critical move is White capturing on e6. We'll recapture with the idea of ...e5 later.} 8...fxe6 9.O-O {White castling gives us the green light to move forward with our plans. We'll castle queenside, and now e5 is in the air.} 9...O-O-O 10.Be3 {10. Be3 does nothing to prevent our pawn advance, yet is the most common move in this position in the amateur database.} 10...e5 {And we'll go for it right away. We've achieved all our goals. We can capture on d4 and play e7-e5 immediately once again!} *

[Event "Chapter 4: White Doesn't Play Nf3 / Study 2: White Plays 6.Ne2 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/2845d388-da50-45a7-9635-3f74f5d4aca2/studies/95c39777-a496-4986-a68f-c377694b6ee8/lines"]

1.e4 {This study introduces our subject with White blocking the attack with 6. Ne2. Our bishop isn't forced back right away: we'll develop our queenside knight with 6... Nc6, but this alters things a bit. We've got a more dynamic position after that. We'll start with 1.e4 d5.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White takes. We'll recapture with the queen.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nc3 {White activates the knight with tempo... And we'll move the queen to a5.} 3...Qa5 4.d4 {Expanding in the center comes naturally. Let's develop the g8-knight.} 4...Nf6 5.Bc4 {A popular move instead of 5. Nf3 is Bc4, developing the bishop to the most active square. This has been played around 8% of times in more than 300,000 games. If we play in the most natural way, White will develop the knight to f3 and the bishop to d2 next. However, here's where we spice things up with rapid development. Bg4 hits the white queen.} 5...Bg4 6.Ne2 {This is a weird maneuver, but it's not bad. It has happened in about 16% of games. Yet again, the plan of ...Nc6 followed by queenside castling is strong.} 6...Nc6 7.Bd2 {7. Bd2 is played only 6% of the time but has a concrete idea behind it. White simply lines up the bishop with our queen. However, we should still long castle here.} 7...O-O-O 8.f3 {First, White attacks the bishop. We'll retreat to the natural f5-square.} 8...Bf5 9.Nd5 {An interesting idea by White is 9.Nd5, and it's important to be prepared against it. There's a discovered attack on our queen. It may feel suffocated, but it will survive on a4!} 9...Qa4 10.b3 {White going to b3 doesn't manage to trap our queen, after all. They've just opened up the a3-square for us and given us the diagonal to retreat along.} 10...Qa3 11.Nxf6 {Taking on f6 is the most logical.} 11...gxf6 {And we'll recapture with the g-pawn for a reason! We want to leave the option of ...e5 open, which is our main pawn break in most of the positions here. Our queen is fine and White's d4-pawn is vulnerable.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: White Doesn't Play Nf3 / Study 2: White Plays 6.Ne2 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/2845d388-da50-45a7-9635-3f74f5d4aca2/studies/95c39777-a496-4986-a68f-c377694b6ee8/lines"]

1.e4 {This study introduces our subject with White blocking the attack with 6. Ne2. Our bishop isn't forced back right away: we'll develop our queenside knight with 6... Nc6, but this alters things a bit. We've got a more dynamic position after that. We'll start with 1.e4 d5.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White takes. We'll recapture with the queen.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nc3 {White activates the knight with tempo... And we'll move the queen to a5.} 3...Qa5 4.d4 {Expanding in the center comes naturally. Let's develop the g8-knight.} 4...Nf6 5.Bc4 {A popular move instead of 5. Nf3 is Bc4, developing the bishop to the most active square. This has been played around 8% of times in more than 300,000 games. If we play in the most natural way, White will develop the knight to f3 and the bishop to d2 next. However, here's where we spice things up with rapid development. Bg4 hits the white queen.} 5...Bg4 6.Ne2 {This is a weird maneuver, but it's not bad. It has happened in about 16% of games. Yet again, the plan of ...Nc6 followed by queenside castling is strong.} 6...Nc6 7.Bd2 {7. Bd2 is played only 6% of the time but has a concrete idea behind it. White simply lines up the bishop with our queen. However, we should still long castle here.} 7...O-O-O 8.f3 {First, White attacks the bishop. We'll retreat to the natural f5-square.} 8...Bf5 9.Nd5 {An interesting idea by White is 9.Nd5, and it's important to be prepared against it. There's a discovered attack on our queen. It may feel suffocated, but it will survive on a4!} 9...Qa4 10.Bb3 {Apart from b3, White could attack our queen with Bb3. But that also opens up a new square to escape to.} 10...Qa6 {Which is a6. What will White do about the knight on d5 now?} *

