[Event "Chapter 4: White Doesn't Play Nf3 / Study 2: White Plays 6.Ne2 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/2845d388-da50-45a7-9635-3f74f5d4aca2/studies/95c39777-a496-4986-a68f-c377694b6ee8/lines"]

1.e4 {This study introduces our subject with White blocking the attack with 6. Ne2. Our bishop isn't forced back right away: we'll develop our queenside knight with 6... Nc6, but this alters things a bit. We've got a more dynamic position after that. We'll start with 1.e4 d5.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White takes. We'll recapture with the queen.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nc3 {White activates the knight with tempo... And we'll move the queen to a5.} 3...Qa5 4.d4 {Expanding in the center comes naturally. Let's develop the g8-knight.} 4...Nf6 5.Bc4 {A popular move instead of 5. Nf3 is Bc4, developing the bishop to the most active square. This has been played around 8% of times in more than 300,000 games. If we play in the most natural way, White will develop the knight to f3 and the bishop to d2 next. However, here's where we spice things up with rapid development. Bg4 hits the white queen.} 5...Bg4 6.Ne2 {This is a weird maneuver, but it's not bad. It has happened in about 16% of games. Yet again, the plan of ...Nc6 followed by queenside castling is strong.} 6...Nc6 7.Be3 {7.Be3 is also played 6% of the time, and it's an inaccuracy for the same reason as the previous moves. It's really easy for White to make a mistake after 5...Nc6 by us. That's why I love it!} 7...O-O-O {Of course, we'll castle long and immediately attack the d4-pawn. We can play e7-e5 next, even if White plays 8.f3, due to tactics in the center.} *

