[Event "Chapter 5: Move 4 Sidelines After 3.Nc3 / Study 2: Other 4th Pawn Moves and 4.Rb1 / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/93365b30-794b-4383-b9c8-38689d8ce3ca/studies/cbbcad5e-222e-4ae3-a575-49cf868ba9ad/lines"]

1.e4 {On move 4, White could go for unconventional pawn moves. This is what this study is all about: 4. g3, 4. d3 and odd ones like 4. b4 and 4. a3. All of these have their ideas, which we will promptly deal with. We begin with 1.e4 d5.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White takes on d5. We'll recapture.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nc3 {White attacks the queen. We'll move to a5.} 3...Qa5 4.g3 {This move is quite strange since the light-squared bishop does have a useful square to go to, like c4. I suggest we continue developing and see what White intends beyond Bg2 and piece development.} 4...Nf6 5.Bg2 {White fianchettoes. Now Nc6 is good for quick development.} 5...Nc6 6.Nf3 {This move isn't played that often. It blocks the bishop on g2. Let's target the fianchetto setup with the aggressive h5.} 6...h5 7.h4 {White could overreact to us wanting to play ...h4 and they could play it themselves to block our potential advance. This is yet another pawn pushed in front of the king's castling spot.} 7...Bg4 {Since h3 to eject our bishop isn't possible, this pin is quite uncomfortable. We'll castle queenside and add pressure to the pinned knight with ...Qf5. We can even consider ...Nb4 tricks to overload the queen and attack c2.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Move 4 Sidelines After 3.Nc3 / Study 2: Other 4th Pawn Moves and 4.Rb1 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/93365b30-794b-4383-b9c8-38689d8ce3ca/studies/cbbcad5e-222e-4ae3-a575-49cf868ba9ad/lines"]

1.e4 {On move 4, White could go for unconventional pawn moves. This is what this study is all about: 4. g3, 4. d3 and odd ones like 4. b4 and 4. a3. All of these have their ideas, which we will promptly deal with. We begin with 1.e4 d5.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White takes on d5. We'll recapture.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nc3 {White attacks the queen. We'll move to a5.} 3...Qa5 4.g3 {This move is quite strange since the light-squared bishop does have a useful square to go to, like c4. I suggest we continue developing and see what White intends beyond Bg2 and piece development.} 4...Nf6 5.Bg2 {White fianchettoes. Now Nc6 is good for quick development.} 5...Nc6 6.Nf3 {This move isn't played that often. It blocks the bishop on g2. Let's target the fianchetto setup with the aggressive h5.} 6...h5 7.O-O {It's possible that White simply ignores our advance on the kingside and castles. So let's keep pushing! ...h4 opens up the kingside.} 7...h4 8.Nxh4 {White takes the pawn. This is exactly what we want. We're not winning or anything immediate, but our attack is making progress.} 8...Bg4 9.Bf3 {White's best choice is to propose a bishop trade, since f3 creates a weakness. The light-squared bishop is a strong defender, so let's accept the trade.} 9...Bxf3 10.Nxf3 {The knight recaptures, avoiding 10...g5.} 10...O-O-O {But we can castle queenside and still have good attacking prospects in the position. We'll be going Qf5 and Qh3 almost no matter what, and e5 to control the center.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Move 4 Sidelines After 3.Nc3 / Study 2: Other 4th Pawn Moves and 4.Rb1 / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/93365b30-794b-4383-b9c8-38689d8ce3ca/studies/cbbcad5e-222e-4ae3-a575-49cf868ba9ad/lines"]

1.e4 {On move 4, White could go for unconventional pawn moves. This is what this study is all about: 4. g3, 4. d3 and odd ones like 4. b4 and 4. a3. All of these have their ideas, which we will promptly deal with. We begin with 1.e4 d5.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White takes on d5. We'll recapture.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nc3 {White attacks the queen. We'll move to a5.} 3...Qa5 4.g3 {This move is quite strange since the light-squared bishop does have a useful square to go to, like c4. I suggest we continue developing and see what White intends beyond Bg2 and piece development.} 4...Nf6 5.Bg2 {White fianchettoes. Now Nc6 is good for quick development.} 5...Nc6 6.Nf3 {This move isn't played that often. It blocks the bishop on g2. Let's target the fianchetto setup with the aggressive h5.} 6...h5 7.d3 {White could delay castling and play d3 to develop their bishop. We'll push forward with h4.} 7...h4 8.Nxh4 {White takes. We won't continue with ...Rxh4. Instead, we'll attack the queen.} 8...Bg4 {If we provoke f3, White weakens themselves and all we have to do is retreat to d7. If 9. Bf3, we play ...Bh3, temporarily preventing castling. Our idea is to be annoying with the bishop and induce weaknesses.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Move 4 Sidelines After 3.Nc3 / Study 2: Other 4th Pawn Moves and 4.Rb1 / Variation 10"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/93365b30-794b-4383-b9c8-38689d8ce3ca/studies/cbbcad5e-222e-4ae3-a575-49cf868ba9ad/lines"]

1.e4 {On move 4, White could go for unconventional pawn moves. This is what this study is all about: 4. g3, 4. d3 and odd ones like 4. b4 and 4. a3. All of these have their ideas, which we will promptly deal with. We begin with 1.e4 d5.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White takes on d5. We'll recapture.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nc3 {White attacks the queen. We'll move to a5.} 3...Qa5 4.g3 {This move is quite strange since the light-squared bishop does have a useful square to go to, like c4. I suggest we continue developing and see what White intends beyond Bg2 and piece development.} 4...Nf6 5.Bg2 {White fianchettoes. Now Nc6 is good for quick development.} 5...Nc6 6.Nf3 {This move isn't played that often. It blocks the bishop on g2. Let's target the fianchetto setup with the aggressive h5.} 6...h5 7.h3 {7. h3 is another idea. White wants us to play ...h4 so they can play g4 and lock things down.} 7...g5 {But since our queen on a5 defends g5, we can aim for g4. White is under some pressure and the engine is already starting to give Black the advantage. Since g5 opens up the g7 square, we'll develop with ...Bg7 and Be6.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Move 4 Sidelines After 3.Nc3 / Study 2: Other 4th Pawn Moves and 4.Rb1 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/93365b30-794b-4383-b9c8-38689d8ce3ca/studies/cbbcad5e-222e-4ae3-a575-49cf868ba9ad/lines"]

1.e4 {On move 4, White could go for unconventional pawn moves. This is what this study is all about: 4. g3, 4. d3 and odd ones like 4. b4 and 4. a3. All of these have their ideas, which we will promptly deal with. We begin with 1.e4 d5.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White takes on d5. We'll recapture.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nc3 {White attacks the queen. We'll move to a5.} 3...Qa5 4.g3 {This move is quite strange since the light-squared bishop does have a useful square to go to, like c4. I suggest we continue developing and see what White intends beyond Bg2 and piece development.} 4...Nf6 5.Bg2 {White fianchettoes. Now Nc6 is good for quick development.} 5...Nc6 6.Nge2 {Since White prides themselves in having a bishop on g2, they may develop the knight to e2 so that they don't block the bishop. Let's surprise White with the most aggressive continuation, the unexpected h5!} 6...h5 7.h3 {The best move for White to defend is h3. If we push h4, they'll play g4.} 7...Bd7 {We certainly don't want them to lock the structure down on the kingside, so we'll continue with development by castling queenside and playing e5. We will aim to take advantage of the g3 and h3 pawn moves.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Move 4 Sidelines After 3.Nc3 / Study 2: Other 4th Pawn Moves and 4.Rb1 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/93365b30-794b-4383-b9c8-38689d8ce3ca/studies/cbbcad5e-222e-4ae3-a575-49cf868ba9ad/lines"]

1.e4 {On move 4, White could go for unconventional pawn moves. This is what this study is all about: 4. g3, 4. d3 and odd ones like 4. b4 and 4. a3. All of these have their ideas, which we will promptly deal with. We begin with 1.e4 d5.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White takes on d5. We'll recapture.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nc3 {White attacks the queen. We'll move to a5.} 3...Qa5 4.d3 {4. d3 is the most popular pawn move sideline. 10% of games in the database on the Scandi go like this. I suggest we simply develop with the normal Nf6.} 4...Nf6 5.Bd2 {85% of games continue like this. White places the bishop on d2 and eyes the queen. We can develop both knights since the bishop does not pose a threat yet.} 5...Nc6 6.Ne4 {The most popular variation goes like this. White makes use of the one-mover available to them to attack our queen. b6 is a safe square for our queen.} 6...Qb6 7.Nxf6+ {White takes on f6 with check. This was likely to happen from even before Ne4 was played. The knight did not have a solid outpost on e4, nor any prospects.} 7...exf6 {In essence, we have a great Caro-Tartakower variation, where White's pieces are bad. Material is equal, but we're already -1.2. Moves like ...Bc5 and 0-0 are desirable. White will have a tough time handling the pressure on f2 and b2.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Move 4 Sidelines After 3.Nc3 / Study 2: Other 4th Pawn Moves and 4.Rb1 / Variation 11"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/93365b30-794b-4383-b9c8-38689d8ce3ca/studies/cbbcad5e-222e-4ae3-a575-49cf868ba9ad/lines"]

1.e4 {On move 4, White could go for unconventional pawn moves. This is what this study is all about: 4. g3, 4. d3 and odd ones like 4. b4 and 4. a3. All of these have their ideas, which we will promptly deal with. We begin with 1.e4 d5.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White takes on d5. We'll recapture.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nc3 {White attacks the queen. We'll move to a5.} 3...Qa5 4.Rb1 {The last move I want us to explore is 4. Rb1. The standard 4...c6 works here. But I like 4...c5 to fight for the center.} 4...c5 5.a3 {White may insist on b4. We should just develop since we cannot stop it anyway.} 5...Nf6 6.b4 {White pushes with b4. We'll go backwards to c7, a great square for the queen.} 6...Qc7 7.Nf3 {White develops. Let's get our queen's knight out.} 7...Nc6 8.bxc5 {If they take on c5, we'll have harmonious development after ...e5.} 8...e5 {We're going to take on c5 and castle. If Nb5, we can go Qa5 or even Qd8. We should have virtually no problems in this position.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Move 4 Sidelines After 3.Nc3 / Study 2: Other 4th Pawn Moves and 4.Rb1 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/93365b30-794b-4383-b9c8-38689d8ce3ca/studies/cbbcad5e-222e-4ae3-a575-49cf868ba9ad/lines"]

1.e4 {On move 4, White could go for unconventional pawn moves. This is what this study is all about: 4. g3, 4. d3 and odd ones like 4. b4 and 4. a3. All of these have their ideas, which we will promptly deal with. We begin with 1.e4 d5.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White takes on d5. We'll recapture.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nc3 {White attacks the queen. We'll move to a5.} 3...Qa5 4.b4 {This gambit has been played 3% of the time. It's an attempt to distract us and activate the rook. We can accept the gambit and challenge White to prove their initiative with Rb1.} 4...Qxb4 5.Nb5 {White's idea is to prevent Qd6 and to attack c7. We should move the queen and defend.} 5...Qa5 6.Qh5 {A trappy move. White wants to play a discovered attack with Nd6, so we simply defend our queen with ...Nc6.} 6...Nc6 7.Bc4 {7. Bc4 threatens f7. With 7...e6 we block the attack and get ready to continue our development.} 7...e6 8.Bb2 {White has great diagonals for their bishops. However, we're catching up by playing really natural moves that won't be hard for you to remember. Let's push back the troublesome knight on b5.} 8...a6 9.Bc3 {This is a one-move attack. We can move out of the way.} 9...Qb6 10.Na3 {This is White's only move. We cannot take on a3 because we would lose g7.} 10...Nf6 {But we can block the bishop's diagonal with tempo. Black is completely winning.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Move 4 Sidelines After 3.Nc3 / Study 2: Other 4th Pawn Moves and 4.Rb1 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/93365b30-794b-4383-b9c8-38689d8ce3ca/studies/cbbcad5e-222e-4ae3-a575-49cf868ba9ad/lines"]

1.e4 {On move 4, White could go for unconventional pawn moves. This is what this study is all about: 4. g3, 4. d3 and odd ones like 4. b4 and 4. a3. All of these have their ideas, which we will promptly deal with. We begin with 1.e4 d5.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White takes on d5. We'll recapture.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nc3 {White attacks the queen. We'll move to a5.} 3...Qa5 4.a3 {The idea behind 4. a3 is to push b4. 4...c6 at this point will allow us to bring the queen back to c7 if b4 is played.} 4...c6 5.b4 {This move is to be expected. Our queen drops back.} 5...Qc7 6.Nf3 {White brings their other knight out. We'll continue with the classic Scandi developing moves.} 6...Nf6 7.Bc4 {White develops their bishop and aims at f7. There aren't any tactics on f7, so we can pin with ...Bg4.} 7...Bg4 8.O-O {White castles.} 8...e6 {And now e6 to open up our bishop. We'll want to go for ...Bd6 for pressure on h2. ...Nbd7 is also necessary to get our last piece developed. We want to castle kingside to make sure White's queenside expansion lacks relevance in the middlegame.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Move 4 Sidelines After 3.Nc3 / Study 2: Other 4th Pawn Moves and 4.Rb1 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/93365b30-794b-4383-b9c8-38689d8ce3ca/studies/cbbcad5e-222e-4ae3-a575-49cf868ba9ad/lines"]

1.e4 {On move 4, White could go for unconventional pawn moves. This is what this study is all about: 4. g3, 4. d3 and odd ones like 4. b4 and 4. a3. All of these have their ideas, which we will promptly deal with. We begin with 1.e4 d5.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White takes on d5. We'll recapture.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nc3 {White attacks the queen. We'll move to a5.} 3...Qa5 4.b4 {This gambit has been played 3% of the time. It's an attempt to distract us and activate the rook. We can accept the gambit and challenge White to prove their initiative with Rb1.} 4...Qxb4 5.Rb1 {The rook goes to b1. d6 is a safe square for the time being.} 5...Qd6 6.Nf3 {White develops the other knight. We should develop our king's knight as well.} 6...Nf6 7.d4 {They have to compensate for their pawn down position now. Nb5 and Bf4 ideas could be annoying later, so let's prevent them with a6.} 7...a6 8.Bd3 {The bishop goes to d3 and controls e4.} 8...g6 {I like this fianchetto because our dark-squared bishop will aim at d4 and be relevant in the middlegame. White has a bit more space, but we don't have any weaknesses. In my opinion, the b-file is not enough long-term compensation.} *

