1.e4 {On move 4, White could go for unconventional pawn moves. This is what this study is all about: 4. g3, 4. d3 and odd ones like 4. b4 and 4. a3. All of these have their ideas, which we will promptly deal with. We begin with 1.e4 d5.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White takes on d5. We'll recapture.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nc3 {White attacks the queen. We'll move to a5.} 3...Qa5 4.b4 {This gambit has been played 3% of the time. It's an attempt to distract us and activate the rook. We can accept the gambit and challenge White to prove their initiative with Rb1.} 4...Qxb4 5.Nb5 {White's idea is to prevent Qd6 and to attack c7. We should move the queen and defend.} 5...Qa5 6.Bc4 {White usually develops the bishop to c4. 83% of games go like this.} 6...Nf6 {We can go 6...Nf6 and enjoy the position, up a pawn. This move prevents any Qh5 ideas.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Move 3 Sidelines / Study 1: Lines After 3.Nf3 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/ab6b9c7c-f20c-455f-9511-458c625164d7/studies/a84088d3-1bda-46d1-a165-99dc1b8e434a/lines"]

1.e4 {In chapter 6, we'll take a look at the move 3 deviations by White. A lot of White players assume Black is only prepared for 3.Nc3, so they want to catch them off guard with 3.Nf3, for example, which is the second most popular move, and we'll cover it in this study. Let's get started with 1. e4 d5.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White takes on d5. We recapture.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nf3 {And the most popular alternative is 3.Nf3, played 12% of the time, which is still almost 16 million games in the database! We aim to develop the queenside quickly, so starting with ...Bg4 is logical here.} 3...Bg4 4.Be2 {4.Be2 is by far the most common move in this position, played 76% of the time. White continues with their plan, unpins the knight and has already developed the kingside pieces. It doesn't pose us any direct problems, though. We'll continue to develop naturally and play Nc6.} 4...Nc6 5.O-O {The most popular move is 5.O-O, played 43% of the time here. White aimed for a quick kingside development and we aim for a quick queenside development. We go for opposite-side castling.} 5...O-O-O 6.h3 {What should we do if White attacks our bishop now? We can offer it in order to open up the h-file for the rook!} 6...h5 7.Nc3 {Before potentially capturing on g4, White might attack our queen with Nc3. Retreating would be passive, so let's keep the pressure on with Qf5.} 7...Qf5 8.d3 {The best move is not to capture the bishop but rather to play d3 and try to develop as many pieces as possible first. We'll do the same and continue offering the bishop.} 8...Nf6 9.Be3 {White activates the dark-squared bishop instead. Let's gain some central space with e5. Again, the sacrifice still works.} 9...e5 10.Ng5 {White wants to trade with Ng5! This move opens up the two light-squared bishops, and White attacks our f7-pawn at the same time. So, we have to react.} 10...Bxe2 11.Qxe2 {Once the queen recaptures, there is an active way to defend f7.} 11...Nd5 {We can centralize the knight on d5 and offer a trade. Our queen is covering the f7-pawn now, and if White doesn't trade on d5 we'll get our knights to d4 and f4. Eventually we will push ...f6 and possibly ...g5 and ...g4 as well. We have better control of the center and a potentially powerful pawn storm.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Move 3 Sidelines / Study 1: Lines After 3.Nf3 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/ab6b9c7c-f20c-455f-9511-458c625164d7/studies/a84088d3-1bda-46d1-a165-99dc1b8e434a/lines"]

1.e4 {In chapter 6, we'll take a look at the move 3 deviations by White. A lot of White players assume Black is only prepared for 3.Nc3, so they want to catch them off guard with 3.Nf3, for example, which is the second most popular move, and we'll cover it in this study. Let's get started with 1. e4 d5.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White takes on d5. We recapture.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nf3 {And the most popular alternative is 3.Nf3, played 12% of the time, which is still almost 16 million games in the database! We aim to develop the queenside quickly, so starting with ...Bg4 is logical here.} 3...Bg4 4.Be2 {4.Be2 is by far the most common move in this position, played 76% of the time. White continues with their plan, unpins the knight and has already developed the kingside pieces. It doesn't pose us any direct problems, though. We'll continue to develop naturally and play Nc6.} 4...Nc6 5.O-O {The most popular move is 5.O-O, played 43% of the time here. White aimed for a quick kingside development and we aim for a quick queenside development. We go for opposite-side castling.} 5...O-O-O 6.h3 {What should we do if White attacks our bishop now? We can offer it in order to open up the h-file for the rook!} 6...h5 7.hxg4 {If they take, that's in fact a big blunder. We'll capture on g4 and open the h-file.} 7...hxg4 8.Nh2 {White's best retreating square is h2, attacking the g4-pawn with multiple pieces. We can't let them take it.} 8...f5 {So we'll defend with ...f5, and now our queen has a different route of joining the kingside attack. Despite having an extra piece White is actually losing because of our invasion on the h-file!} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Move 3 Sidelines / Study 1: Lines After 3.Nf3 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/ab6b9c7c-f20c-455f-9511-458c625164d7/studies/a84088d3-1bda-46d1-a165-99dc1b8e434a/lines"]

1.e4 {In chapter 6, we'll take a look at the move 3 deviations by White. A lot of White players assume Black is only prepared for 3.Nc3, so they want to catch them off guard with 3.Nf3, for example, which is the second most popular move, and we'll cover it in this study. Let's get started with 1. e4 d5.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White takes on d5. We recapture.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nf3 {And the most popular alternative is 3.Nf3, played 12% of the time, which is still almost 16 million games in the database! We aim to develop the queenside quickly, so starting with ...Bg4 is logical here.} 3...Bg4 4.Be2 {4.Be2 is by far the most common move in this position, played 76% of the time. White continues with their plan, unpins the knight and has already developed the kingside pieces. It doesn't pose us any direct problems, though. We'll continue to develop naturally and play Nc6.} 4...Nc6 5.O-O {The most popular move is 5.O-O, played 43% of the time here. White aimed for a quick kingside development and we aim for a quick queenside development. We go for opposite-side castling.} 5...O-O-O 6.h3 {What should we do if White attacks our bishop now? We can offer it in order to open up the h-file for the rook!} 6...h5 7.Nc3 {Before potentially capturing on g4, White might attack our queen with Nc3. Retreating would be passive, so let's keep the pressure on with Qf5.} 7...Qf5 8.hxg4 {Again, White capturing on g4 is a mistake. We'll take and open up the h-file, with a strong attack.} 8...hxg4 9.Nh2 {But if White retreats to h2 now, there is no ...f5 to protect our g4-pawn, nor ...Qh5. But we can sacrifice the pawn with e6, preparing to bring the bishop into the game. It will be very difficult for White to deal with our h-file attack.} 9...e6 10.Bxg4 {White doesn't shy away from grabbing the pawn. Our queen is attacked, but we don't have to retreat it to a passive square.} 10...Qg6 {But we have a big attack and a -1 advantage. After ...Nf6 and ...Bd6 or Bc5 it won't be easy for White to handle the pressure on their kingside. For more analysis, refer back to 6.h3.} 11.Kh1 {White wants to play Bh3 to block the h-file without allowing ...Rxh3. And if we're slow and let them do that, they'll gain the advantage. Our c6-knight comes into the action now, and attacks the c2-pawn.} 11...Nd4 12.d3 {White thinks they're out of the woods by blocking our queen and protecting c2. Spoiler: they aren't! We'll take on c2 anyway. The queen is overloaded.} 12...Nxc2 13.Qxc2 {If they capture, their queen isn't defending the bishop anymore. So we capture on g4.} 13...Qxg4 14.Nb5 {They can threaten checkmate with Nb5. There is a straightforward defense, however.} 14...Bd6 15.Nxd6+ {White takes...} 15...Rxd6 {And once we recapture with the rook, Black is winning here. Our queen is still coming to the h-file, the knight is going to activate on f6, and now we can also play ...Rd5 and ...Rdh5 for a three-piece stack on the h-file! Too much for White to deal with.} *

