[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/ab6b9c7c-f20c-455f-9511-458c625164d7/studies/a84088d3-1bda-46d1-a165-99dc1b8e434a/lines"]

1.e4 {In chapter 6, we'll take a look at the move 3 deviations by White. A lot of White players assume Black is only prepared for 3.Nc3, so they want to catch them off guard with 3.Nf3, for example, which is the second most popular move, and we'll cover it in this study. Let's get started with 1. e4 d5.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White takes on d5. We recapture.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nf3 {And the most popular alternative is 3.Nf3, played 12% of the time, which is still almost 16 million games in the database! We aim to develop the queenside quickly, so starting with ...Bg4 is logical here.} 3...Bg4 4.Nc3 {4.Nc3 could be played here, for example, and it is 16% of the time. Our queen is attacked. A queen trade would definitely be beneficial for us. We'll slide the queen to e6 and give a check.} 4...Qe6+ 5.Be2 {If White blocks with the bishop, it's not actually defending the knight on f3 as it's pinned. Which means we can mess up White's structure by capturing on f3.} 5...Bxf3 6.gxf3 {The g-pawn recaptures. Let's continue our development with Nc6.} 6...Nc6 7.d4 {White advances in the center, and we've seen something similar before. We have a great way of pressuring the d4-pawn. We castle queenside.} 7...O-O-O 8.Be3 {The bishop defends it...} 8...Nf6 {And we've got ...Nf6 to continue. Since our queen is on e6, we'll fianchetto the dark-squared bishop next. The pawn on d4 is the main target.} *

