1.e4 {In chapter 6, we'll take a look at the move 3 deviations by White. A lot of White players assume Black is only prepared for 3.Nc3, so they want to catch them off guard with 3.Nf3, for example, which is the second most popular move, and we'll cover it in this study. Let's get started with 1. e4 d5.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White takes on d5. We recapture.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nf3 {And the most popular alternative is 3.Nf3, played 12% of the time, which is still almost 16 million games in the database! We aim to develop the queenside quickly, so starting with ...Bg4 is logical here.} 3...Bg4 4.Be2 {4.Be2 is by far the most common move in this position, played 76% of the time. White continues with their plan, unpins the knight and has already developed the kingside pieces. It doesn't pose us any direct problems, though. We'll continue to develop naturally and play Nc6.} 4...Nc6 5.Nc3 {What if Nc3 now? It's played 25% of the time. I suggest retreating our queen to d7, which is a safe square as we prepare development.} 5...Qd7 6.O-O {For example, 6.O-O does nothing to prevent our plans. We'll castle queenside.} 6...O-O-O 7.d3 {7.d3 is already somewhat passive, but does open up the bishop. We can respond actively.} 7...e5 8.Be3 {White just developing is solid enough, but they are going to find themselves somewhat passive.} 8...f5 {After ...f5, Black is already better. We've got a strong center, a safe king and our knight is coming to f6.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Move 3 Sidelines / Study 1: Lines After 3.Nf3 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/ab6b9c7c-f20c-455f-9511-458c625164d7/studies/a84088d3-1bda-46d1-a165-99dc1b8e434a/lines"]

1.e4 {In chapter 6, we'll take a look at the move 3 deviations by White. A lot of White players assume Black is only prepared for 3.Nc3, so they want to catch them off guard with 3.Nf3, for example, which is the second most popular move, and we'll cover it in this study. Let's get started with 1. e4 d5.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White takes on d5. We recapture.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nf3 {And the most popular alternative is 3.Nf3, played 12% of the time, which is still almost 16 million games in the database! We aim to develop the queenside quickly, so starting with ...Bg4 is logical here.} 3...Bg4 4.Be2 {4.Be2 is by far the most common move in this position, played 76% of the time. White continues with their plan, unpins the knight and has already developed the kingside pieces. It doesn't pose us any direct problems, though. We'll continue to develop naturally and play Nc6.} 4...Nc6 5.Nc3 {What if Nc3 now? It's played 25% of the time. I suggest retreating our queen to d7, which is a safe square as we prepare development.} 5...Qd7 6.h3 {A more confrontational move here is h3, attacking the bishop. In this line, we should not retreat but instead trade and play to control the center more.} 6...Bxf3 7.Bxf3 {Bishop takes, and we can safely castle now.} 7...O-O-O 8.d3 {White advances with d3, since they can't play d4. Nothing stops us from pushing ...e5!} 8...e5 9.Be3 {Once the bishop gets out...} 9...f5 {There's the f-pawn as well. The position is equal, but I would argue that Black has more space and clear prospects of what to do next. That is to activate the knight to f6, the bishop to b4 and shove the c-knight into the central d4-square. If needed, we can slide the king to b8 for safety.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Move 3 Sidelines / Study 1: Lines After 3.Nf3 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/ab6b9c7c-f20c-455f-9511-458c625164d7/studies/a84088d3-1bda-46d1-a165-99dc1b8e434a/lines"]

1.e4 {In chapter 6, we'll take a look at the move 3 deviations by White. A lot of White players assume Black is only prepared for 3.Nc3, so they want to catch them off guard with 3.Nf3, for example, which is the second most popular move, and we'll cover it in this study. Let's get started with 1. e4 d5.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White takes on d5. We recapture.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nf3 {And the most popular alternative is 3.Nf3, played 12% of the time, which is still almost 16 million games in the database! We aim to develop the queenside quickly, so starting with ...Bg4 is logical here.} 3...Bg4 4.Be2 {4.Be2 is by far the most common move in this position, played 76% of the time. White continues with their plan, unpins the knight and has already developed the kingside pieces. It doesn't pose us any direct problems, though. We'll continue to develop naturally and play Nc6.} 4...Nc6 5.h3 {The h3-push on move 5 is played 8% of the time. But we've got a solution to this as well.} 5...Bxf3 6.Bxf3 {Where will we place the queen when White recaptures? In many of these positions, it's too slow if we don't play with tempo like this.} 6...Qe6+ 7.Qe2 {Once White blocks, we don't want to take right away. Their light-squared bishop might be too strong in the future if we do that, and in any case we have a handy way of improving our position by playing Nd4.} 7...Nd4 8.Qxe6 {But once they exchange our idea wasn't to recapture the queen right away. We can take on f3 and split their kingside pawns.} 8...Nxf3+ 9.gxf3 {White takes back.} 9...fxe6 {And we'll take the queen now, with a fighting endgame as both sides have a damaged pawn structure. We'll get the knight to f5 next and fianchetto the dark-squared bishop, while aiming to castle long.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Move 3 Sidelines / Study 1: Lines After 3.Nf3 / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/ab6b9c7c-f20c-455f-9511-458c625164d7/studies/a84088d3-1bda-46d1-a165-99dc1b8e434a/lines"]

1.e4 {In chapter 6, we'll take a look at the move 3 deviations by White. A lot of White players assume Black is only prepared for 3.Nc3, so they want to catch them off guard with 3.Nf3, for example, which is the second most popular move, and we'll cover it in this study. Let's get started with 1. e4 d5.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White takes on d5. We recapture.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nf3 {And the most popular alternative is 3.Nf3, played 12% of the time, which is still almost 16 million games in the database! We aim to develop the queenside quickly, so starting with ...Bg4 is logical here.} 3...Bg4 4.Nc3 {4.Nc3 could be played here, for example, and it is 16% of the time. Our queen is attacked. A queen trade would definitely be beneficial for us. We'll slide the queen to e6 and give a check.} 4...Qe6+ 5.Qe2 {If White blocks with the queen, we want to trade here. We'll be left with no problems in the position whatsoever.} 5...Qxe2+ 6.Bxe2 {Bishop takes, and now we know what side of the board we want to develop first.} 6...Nc6 7.h3 {They might attack our bishop here. We'll simply move it and keep it relevant.} 7...Bh5 8.g4 {It's logical to chase us away with g4. We'll move to g6.} 8...Bg6 9.Bb5 {A good and active move is Bb5, pinning our knight on c6. We cannot push ...e5 now as it would be hanging, although we need to complete our development.} 9...e6 10.Ne5 {White could capture and ruin our structure, but Ne5 is more active, now threatening to capture on c6 while attacking our bishop on g6. We've got a defense, though.} 10...Ne7 {Let's support the c6-knight with a knight on e7 (which is why ...e6 is crucial). If White takes on g6, we'll just recapture with the h-pawn. If not, we'll castle long, play ...f6 to kick the knight and at some point undermine White's extended structure on the kingside with ...h5.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Move 3 Sidelines / Study 2: White Plays 4.Be2 and 5.d4 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/ab6b9c7c-f20c-455f-9511-458c625164d7/studies/752f4bca-51e5-4c49-a707-91e3cf0cdc37/lines"]

1.e4 {I want us to look at more 3.Nf3 lines. There's a less common variation, and that includes White playing 4.Be2 like in the previous study but also 5.d4, the direct central pawn break. We have to know how to fight such a simple and straightforward assault. Let's get things underway with 1.e4 d5.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White takes on d5. We'll recapture with the queen.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nf3 {White wants to develop the kingside first. I suggest we pin the knight right away.} 3...Bg4 4.Be2 {White continues with their plan, unpins the knight and has already developed their kingside pieces. It doesn't pose us any direct problems, though. We'll continue to develop our queenside.} 4...Nc6 5.d4 {5.d4 is played only 10% of the time here, but it might be the most instructive move. White simply puts a pawn in the center and wants to develop further. We'll go for our usual idea here and castle queenside.} 5...O-O-O 6.c4 {A natural move is to take advantage of our queen still being on d5 and attack it with c4. Instead of the usual a5, I suggest we slide our queen to f5.} 6...Qf5 7.O-O {7.O-O is possible for White. If you see this move, you might be playing against an experienced or a prepared opponent who is aware of the problems in the 7.Be3 line. White offers the d4-pawn, and we will grab it.} 7...Nxd4 8.Nxd4 {The white knight takes, and there's tension between the pieces. We don't mind a few trades.} 8...Bxe2 9.Qxe2 {The queen takes, and now we get our piece back on d4.} 9...Rxd4 10.Be3 {And White will do anything to attack our pieces with tempo. Also, the a7-pawn will be under attack once the rook moves back.} 10...Rd8 11.Nc3 {Meanwhile, White is getting a lead in development, which was their point when sacrificing a pawn. As we are behind, let's do something about it and open up our kingside pieces. We need to prioritize development over trying to hold onto the a7-pawn. For example, Kb8 leaves us in dire trouble after either white rook comes to d1.} 11...e6 12.Rad1 {White wants to take control of the d-file. We don't have a better option than to accept the trade.} 12...Rxd1 13.Rxd1 {White is dominating the d-file. Our main goal is still to develop as quickly as we can. ...a6 14.Qd2 is one disaster which might befall the unwary.} 13...Nf6 14.Bxa7 {White can grab the pawn here and shift the material back to equality. Keep in mind that ...b6 to trap the bishop here doesn't work because of c5 and White is winning.} 14...Be7 {We can develop to e7. The material is equal and both sides have activated almost all the pieces now. Our rook is coming to d8, with an equal position but a lot still to happen.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Move 3 Sidelines / Study 2: White Plays 4.Be2 and 5.d4 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/ab6b9c7c-f20c-455f-9511-458c625164d7/studies/752f4bca-51e5-4c49-a707-91e3cf0cdc37/lines"]

1.e4 {I want us to look at more 3.Nf3 lines. There's a less common variation, and that includes White playing 4.Be2 like in the previous study but also 5.d4, the direct central pawn break. We have to know how to fight such a simple and straightforward assault. Let's get things underway with 1.e4 d5.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White takes on d5. We'll recapture with the queen.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nf3 {White wants to develop the kingside first. I suggest we pin the knight right away.} 3...Bg4 4.Be2 {White continues with their plan, unpins the knight and has already developed their kingside pieces. It doesn't pose us any direct problems, though. We'll continue to develop our queenside.} 4...Nc6 5.d4 {5.d4 is played only 10% of the time here, but it might be the most instructive move. White simply puts a pawn in the center and wants to develop further. We'll go for our usual idea here and castle queenside.} 5...O-O-O 6.c4 {A natural move is to take advantage of our queen still being on d5 and attack it with c4. Instead of the usual a5, I suggest we slide our queen to f5.} 6...Qf5 7.Be3 {7.Be3 is somewhat more natural than 7.O-O, and it protects the d4-pawn in the center. However, we've got a tactic! We'll start by taking on f3.} 7...Bxf3 8.Bxf3 {White takes. The d4-pawn seems to be covered, but in reality it isn't. We can capture with the knight!} 8...Nxd4 9.Bxd4 {White accepts the piece sacrifice. Now there is a forcing sequence which regains the piece, starting with a queen check.} 9...Qe6+ 10.Be2 {White blocks with the bishop and covers c4. But the bishop on d4 is pinned and vulnerable.} 10...Qe4 11.O-O {The best White can do is to castle. Let's grab the bishop.} 11...Qxd4 12.Qa4 {Much better than exchanging queens is to slide to a4. This way, White claims they've got compensation for the pawn in the form of activity. Their bishop is coming to f3 and their knight is coming to c3. We have to find a way to develop as well, and we'll start with ...e6.} 12...e6 13.Nc3 {White's best move is Nc3 here, and the position can become poisonous for us in a split second if we're not precise. For example, White is threatening Nb5 here already, as well as to put a rook on d1. We have to stop the bigger of those threats, which is Nb5.} 13...a6 14.Bf3 {Another common improving move is 14.Bf3. It's a good idea to preemptively get our queen off the d-file and possibly offer a queen trade with ...Qb4.} 14...Qc5 15.Rad1 {White still needs a rook on the d-file. They'd love to take on d8, so we should trade first.} 15...Rxd1 16.Rxd1 {The f-rook recaptures, and now there's the threat of Qe8 checkmate!} 16...Nf6 {But we've got ...Nf6 here to start developing the kingside and cover the e8-square. Next, we'll get the bishop out to e7 and contest the d-file with the rook. Black is slightly better.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Move 3 Sidelines / Study 2: White Plays 4.Be2 and 5.d4 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/ab6b9c7c-f20c-455f-9511-458c625164d7/studies/752f4bca-51e5-4c49-a707-91e3cf0cdc37/lines"]

1.e4 {I want us to look at more 3.Nf3 lines. There's a less common variation, and that includes White playing 4.Be2 like in the previous study but also 5.d4, the direct central pawn break. We have to know how to fight such a simple and straightforward assault. Let's get things underway with 1.e4 d5.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White takes on d5. We'll recapture with the queen.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nf3 {White wants to develop the kingside first. I suggest we pin the knight right away.} 3...Bg4 4.Be2 {White continues with their plan, unpins the knight and has already developed their kingside pieces. It doesn't pose us any direct problems, though. We'll continue to develop our queenside.} 4...Nc6 5.d4 {5.d4 is played only 10% of the time here, but it might be the most instructive move. White simply puts a pawn in the center and wants to develop further. We'll go for our usual idea here and castle queenside.} 5...O-O-O 6.c4 {A natural move is to take advantage of our queen still being on d5 and attack it with c4. Instead of the usual a5, I suggest we slide our queen to f5.} 6...Qf5 7.Be3 {7.Be3 is somewhat more natural than 7.O-O, and it protects the d4-pawn in the center. However, we've got a tactic! We'll start by taking on f3.} 7...Bxf3 8.Bxf3 {White takes. The d4-pawn seems to be covered, but in reality it isn't. We can capture with the knight!} 8...Nxd4 9.Bxd4 {White accepts the piece sacrifice. Now there is a forcing sequence which regains the piece, starting with a queen check.} 9...Qe6+ 10.Be2 {White blocks with the bishop and covers c4. But the bishop on d4 is pinned and vulnerable.} 10...Qe4 11.O-O {The best White can do is to castle. Let's grab the bishop.} 11...Qxd4 12.Qa4 {Much better than exchanging queens is to slide to a4. This way, White claims they've got compensation for the pawn in the form of activity. Their bishop is coming to f3 and their knight is coming to c3. We have to find a way to develop as well, and we'll start with ...e6.} 12...e6 13.Nc3 {White's best move is Nc3 here, and the position can become poisonous for us in a split second if we're not precise. For example, White is threatening Nb5 here already, as well as to put a rook on d1. We have to stop the bigger of those threats, which is Nb5.} 13...a6 14.Rad1 {We can't stop the other idea, however! White establishes control of the d-file and attacks our queen. Definitely we have to deal with a lot for the pawn. Let's retreat to e5, with the clear idea of activating our pieces to f6 and d6 next.} 14...Qe5 15.Rxd8+ {It makes sense for White to trade. Our rooks aren't connected yet. We have to recapture with the king, removing it from the c8-square, where it defends b7.} 15...Kxd8 16.Bf3 {Now Bf3 is even stronger, as it attacks the b7-pawn. Watch out not to hang mate in one here with ...Kc8! We need to move up a gear. Instead of trying to protect the king on the queenside, we'll develop and give up the b7-pawn!} 16...Nf6 17.Bxb7 {The idea is, after White captures...} 17...Bc5 {We can also get the bishop out and suddenly our kingside is developed! Our king will find shelter in the center and we suddenly have our own attack against White's kingside based on our dark square control.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Move 3 Sidelines / Study 2: White Plays 4.Be2 and 5.d4 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/ab6b9c7c-f20c-455f-9511-458c625164d7/studies/752f4bca-51e5-4c49-a707-91e3cf0cdc37/lines"]

1.e4 {I want us to look at more 3.Nf3 lines. There's a less common variation, and that includes White playing 4.Be2 like in the previous study but also 5.d4, the direct central pawn break. We have to know how to fight such a simple and straightforward assault. Let's get things underway with 1.e4 d5.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White takes on d5. We'll recapture with the queen.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nf3 {White wants to develop the kingside first. I suggest we pin the knight right away.} 3...Bg4 4.Be2 {White continues with their plan, unpins the knight and has already developed their kingside pieces. It doesn't pose us any direct problems, though. We'll continue to develop our queenside.} 4...Nc6 5.d4 {5.d4 is played only 10% of the time here, but it might be the most instructive move. White simply puts a pawn in the center and wants to develop further. We'll go for our usual idea here and castle queenside.} 5...O-O-O 6.c4 {A natural move is to take advantage of our queen still being on d5 and attack it with c4. Instead of the usual a5, I suggest we slide our queen to f5.} 6...Qf5 7.Be3 {7.Be3 is somewhat more natural than 7.O-O, and it protects the d4-pawn in the center. However, we've got a tactic! We'll start by taking on f3.} 7...Bxf3 8.Bxf3 {White takes. The d4-pawn seems to be covered, but in reality it isn't. We can capture with the knight!} 8...Nxd4 9.Bxd4 {White accepts the piece sacrifice. Now there is a forcing sequence which regains the piece, starting with a queen check.} 9...Qe6+ 10.Be2 {White blocks with the bishop and covers c4. But the bishop on d4 is pinned and vulnerable.} 10...Qe4 11.O-O {The best White can do is to castle. Let's grab the bishop.} 11...Qxd4 12.Qa4 {Much better than exchanging queens is to slide to a4. This way, White claims they've got compensation for the pawn in the form of activity. Their bishop is coming to f3 and their knight is coming to c3. We have to find a way to develop as well, and we'll start with ...e6.} 12...e6 13.Nc3 {White's best move is Nc3 here, and the position can become poisonous for us in a split second if we're not precise. For example, White is threatening Nb5 here already, as well as to put a rook on d1. We have to stop the bigger of those threats, which is Nb5.} 13...a6 14.Rad1 {We can't stop the other idea, however! White establishes control of the d-file and attacks our queen. Definitely we have to deal with a lot for the pawn. Let's retreat to e5, with the clear idea of activating our pieces to f6 and d6 next.} 14...Qe5 15.Rxd8+ {It makes sense for White to trade. Our rooks aren't connected yet. We have to recapture with the king, removing it from the c8-square, where it defends b7.} 15...Kxd8 16.Rd1+ {If White gives a check, which definitely seems natural... We can get the bishop out to d6 and block, which we wanted to do anyway. Now even their h2-pawn is under attack.} 16...Bd6 17.g3 {17.g3 defends the position, but puts us back in the driver's seat. White has no clear targets right now.} 17...Nf6 {And we'll go ...Nf6 to finish development. Next, we'll completely consolidate with ...Ke7 and even look to push on the kingside with ...h5, using the g3-pawn as a hook.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Move 3 Sidelines / Study 2: White Plays 4.Be2 and 5.d4 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/ab6b9c7c-f20c-455f-9511-458c625164d7/studies/752f4bca-51e5-4c49-a707-91e3cf0cdc37/lines"]

1.e4 {I want us to look at more 3.Nf3 lines. There's a less common variation, and that includes White playing 4.Be2 like in the previous study but also 5.d4, the direct central pawn break. We have to know how to fight such a simple and straightforward assault. Let's get things underway with 1.e4 d5.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White takes on d5. We'll recapture with the queen.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nf3 {White wants to develop the kingside first. I suggest we pin the knight right away.} 3...Bg4 4.Be2 {White continues with their plan, unpins the knight and has already developed their kingside pieces. It doesn't pose us any direct problems, though. We'll continue to develop our queenside.} 4...Nc6 5.d4 {5.d4 is played only 10% of the time here, but it might be the most instructive move. White simply puts a pawn in the center and wants to develop further. We'll go for our usual idea here and castle queenside.} 5...O-O-O 6.c4 {A natural move is to take advantage of our queen still being on d5 and attack it with c4. Instead of the usual a5, I suggest we slide our queen to f5.} 6...Qf5 7.Be3 {7.Be3 is somewhat more natural than 7.O-O, and it protects the d4-pawn in the center. However, we've got a tactic! We'll start by taking on f3.} 7...Bxf3 8.Bxf3 {White takes. The d4-pawn seems to be covered, but in reality it isn't. We can capture with the knight!} 8...Nxd4 9.Bg4 {Instead of taking the free piece in the center, White could try to go for the pin with Bg4. However, we won't lose our queen, since we have a check with the knight which also discovers an attack on the white queen!} 9...Nc2+ 10.Qxc2 {If White takes our knight, they lose the guard of the bishop.} 10...Qxg4 11.O-O {From here on, their main idea will be to push on the queenside and try to create an attack. Let's try to solidify our queenside.} 11...a6 12.b4 {Be ready to face b4. Ideally, we want to place our knight on f5.} 12...Nh6 13.b5 {White is persistent with b5, though. And here's a good defensive move to remember.} 13...Qg6 {After ...Qg6, we've got nothing to be afraid of. We offer a queen trade, which would neutralize White's attack immediately, and we cover the a6-square. Our knight is still coming to f5 and Black is slightly better.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Move 3 Sidelines / Study 2: White Plays 4.Be2 and 5.d4 / Variation 12"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/ab6b9c7c-f20c-455f-9511-458c625164d7/studies/752f4bca-51e5-4c49-a707-91e3cf0cdc37/lines"]

1.e4 {I want us to look at more 3.Nf3 lines. There's a less common variation, and that includes White playing 4.Be2 like in the previous study but also 5.d4, the direct central pawn break. We have to know how to fight such a simple and straightforward assault. Let's get things underway with 1.e4 d5.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White takes on d5. We'll recapture with the queen.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nf3 {White wants to develop the kingside first. I suggest we pin the knight right away.} 3...Bg4 4.Be2 {White continues with their plan, unpins the knight and has already developed their kingside pieces. It doesn't pose us any direct problems, though. We'll continue to develop our queenside.} 4...Nc6 5.d4 {5.d4 is played only 10% of the time here, but it might be the most instructive move. White simply puts a pawn in the center and wants to develop further. We'll go for our usual idea here and castle queenside.} 5...O-O-O 6.Nc3 {White could try and attack our queen with Nc3. But the knight blocks the c-pawn, and thus making it harder for White to defend the d4-pawn! We can get into a familiar position with ...Qa5.} 6...Qa5 7.Be3 {7.Be3 defends the center. Natural development follows.} 7...Nf6 8.O-O {For example, if White doesn't think and castles here...} 8...e5 {Black is much better after ...e5. The d4-pawn is under a lot of pressure and White will most likely lose it.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Move 3 Sidelines / Study 2: White Plays 4.Be2 and 5.d4 / Variation 10"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/ab6b9c7c-f20c-455f-9511-458c625164d7/studies/752f4bca-51e5-4c49-a707-91e3cf0cdc37/lines"]

1.e4 {I want us to look at more 3.Nf3 lines. There's a less common variation, and that includes White playing 4.Be2 like in the previous study but also 5.d4, the direct central pawn break. We have to know how to fight such a simple and straightforward assault. Let's get things underway with 1.e4 d5.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White takes on d5. We'll recapture with the queen.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nf3 {White wants to develop the kingside first. I suggest we pin the knight right away.} 3...Bg4 4.Be2 {White continues with their plan, unpins the knight and has already developed their kingside pieces. It doesn't pose us any direct problems, though. We'll continue to develop our queenside.} 4...Nc6 5.d4 {5.d4 is played only 10% of the time here, but it might be the most instructive move. White simply puts a pawn in the center and wants to develop further. We'll go for our usual idea here and castle queenside.} 5...O-O-O 6.Be3 {6.Be3 defends the center but allows our main idea which we've seen in the previous chapters of the course.} 6...e5 7.c4 {In this variation, White can throw in the move c4 to attack our queen while it's on d5. The queen will go to a familiar square with a tempo.} 7...Qa5+ 8.Bd2 {Once the bishop blocks, I want to show you something revolutionary. We can go for ...Bb4, but instead we have a move which will undermine White's hanging central pawns. Qa6 leaves White with questions regarding the pressure on both central pawns.} 8...Qa6 9.d5 {If d5 is pushed, with the idea of kicking our knight away, we should take on f3 first.} 9...Bxf3 10.Bxf3 {White recaptures on f3. ...Nd4 is extremly powerful now.} 10...Nd4 11.b3 {Let's demonstrate the prospects of our knight if White defends with b3. It would be great if we could threaten a fork on c2. We swing the queen over to the kingside.} 11...Qg6 12.O-O {If White castles, ...Nc2 isn't as potent anymore because it isn't check, and White has Bh5. We can prevent the troublesome bishop move by bringing our knight to f6.} 12...Nf6 13.Nc3 {White develops and defends. As a response, we've got a move that gains space in the center.} 13...e4 14.Be2 {The bishop was under attack and will retreat to e2...} 14...Bd6 {After which we can bring the final minor piece into the game. The main goal has become to attack on the kingside and play against the weak h2-pawn! We'll do that with moves like ...h5, ...Ng4 and we can slide the h-rook to the center for activity. An equal position, but I'd rather have Black here!} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Move 3 Sidelines / Study 2: White Plays 4.Be2 and 5.d4 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/ab6b9c7c-f20c-455f-9511-458c625164d7/studies/752f4bca-51e5-4c49-a707-91e3cf0cdc37/lines"]

1.e4 {I want us to look at more 3.Nf3 lines. There's a less common variation, and that includes White playing 4.Be2 like in the previous study but also 5.d4, the direct central pawn break. We have to know how to fight such a simple and straightforward assault. Let's get things underway with 1.e4 d5.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White takes on d5. We'll recapture with the queen.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nf3 {White wants to develop the kingside first. I suggest we pin the knight right away.} 3...Bg4 4.Be2 {White continues with their plan, unpins the knight and has already developed their kingside pieces. It doesn't pose us any direct problems, though. We'll continue to develop our queenside.} 4...Nc6 5.d4 {5.d4 is played only 10% of the time here, but it might be the most instructive move. White simply puts a pawn in the center and wants to develop further. We'll go for our usual idea here and castle queenside.} 5...O-O-O 6.Be3 {6.Be3 defends the center but allows our main idea which we've seen in the previous chapters of the course.} 6...e5 7.c4 {In this variation, White can throw in the move c4 to attack our queen while it's on d5. The queen will go to a familiar square with a tempo.} 7...Qa5+ 8.Bd2 {Once the bishop blocks, I want to show you something revolutionary. We can go for ...Bb4, but instead we have a move which will undermine White's hanging central pawns. Qa6 leaves White with questions regarding the pressure on both central pawns.} 8...Qa6 9.c5 {The most logical move for White is probably c5, attacking the queen. We don't have to move the queen right now, since we can capture on f3, and if White goes for Bxa6, we can capture their queen.} 9...Bxf3 10.gxf3 {They'll capture on f3 instead and preserve the attack on our queen. Our queen seems to be trapped, but we have a strong defensive move with b5!} 10...b5 11.cxb6 {White capturing en passant doesn't pose us any problems whatsoever. We play the obvious recapture.} 11...Qxb6 12.dxe5 {The d4-pawn was under serious pressure, but now our rook is open and pinning the bishop on d2. Bb4 adds more pressure.} 12...Bb4 13.Nc3 {Once White blocks...} 13...Nge7 {We can develop the g-knight to e7. We'll take on e5 next. Again, an unclear position, but White's center is gone.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Move 3 Sidelines / Study 2: White Plays 4.Be2 and 5.d4 / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/ab6b9c7c-f20c-455f-9511-458c625164d7/studies/752f4bca-51e5-4c49-a707-91e3cf0cdc37/lines"]

1.e4 {I want us to look at more 3.Nf3 lines. There's a less common variation, and that includes White playing 4.Be2 like in the previous study but also 5.d4, the direct central pawn break. We have to know how to fight such a simple and straightforward assault. Let's get things underway with 1.e4 d5.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White takes on d5. We'll recapture with the queen.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nf3 {White wants to develop the kingside first. I suggest we pin the knight right away.} 3...Bg4 4.Be2 {White continues with their plan, unpins the knight and has already developed their kingside pieces. It doesn't pose us any direct problems, though. We'll continue to develop our queenside.} 4...Nc6 5.d4 {5.d4 is played only 10% of the time here, but it might be the most instructive move. White simply puts a pawn in the center and wants to develop further. We'll go for our usual idea here and castle queenside.} 5...O-O-O 6.Be3 {6.Be3 defends the center but allows our main idea which we've seen in the previous chapters of the course.} 6...e5 7.c4 {In this variation, White can throw in the move c4 to attack our queen while it's on d5. The queen will go to a familiar square with a tempo.} 7...Qa5+ 8.Bd2 {Once the bishop blocks, I want to show you something revolutionary. We can go for ...Bb4, but instead we have a move which will undermine White's hanging central pawns. Qa6 leaves White with questions regarding the pressure on both central pawns.} 8...Qa6 9.c5 {The most logical move for White is probably c5, attacking the queen. We don't have to move the queen right now, since we can capture on f3, and if White goes for Bxa6, we can capture their queen.} 9...Bxf3 10.gxf3 {They'll capture on f3 instead and preserve the attack on our queen. Our queen seems to be trapped, but we have a strong defensive move with b5!} 10...b5 11.dxe5 {We love to see 11.dxe5. White simply splits their pawns. We capture and bring a piece out at the same time.} 11...Bxc5 12.Nc3 {A natural-looking move here is Nc3, attacking the b5-pawn. We don't need to defend it. There is an aggressive method of counterplay open to us, starting with Nxe5.} 12...Nxe5 13.Bxb5 {If White takes on b5, that's a losing blunder.} 13...Qe6 {We'll slide to e6 and White is busted. They will probably have to play Ne4 to evade discoveries for the moment, in which case we've got ...Nf6. If White castles here, we've got ...Rxd2 followed by ...Nxf3+ to win the queen!} *

