1.e4 {I want us to look at more 3.Nf3 lines. There's a less common variation, and that includes White playing 4.Be2 like in the previous study but also 5.d4, the direct central pawn break. We have to know how to fight such a simple and straightforward assault. Let's get things underway with 1.e4 d5.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White takes on d5. We'll recapture with the queen.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nf3 {White wants to develop the kingside first. I suggest we pin the knight right away.} 3...Bg4 4.Be2 {White continues with their plan, unpins the knight and has already developed their kingside pieces. It doesn't pose us any direct problems, though. We'll continue to develop our queenside.} 4...Nc6 5.d4 {5.d4 is played only 10% of the time here, but it might be the most instructive move. White simply puts a pawn in the center and wants to develop further. We'll go for our usual idea here and castle queenside.} 5...O-O-O 6.Be3 {6.Be3 defends the center but allows our main idea which we've seen in the previous chapters of the course.} 6...e5 7.c4 {In this variation, White can throw in the move c4 to attack our queen while it's on d5. The queen will go to a familiar square with a tempo.} 7...Qa5+ 8.Bd2 {Once the bishop blocks, I want to show you something revolutionary. We can go for ...Bb4, but instead we have a move which will undermine White's hanging central pawns. Qa6 leaves White with questions regarding the pressure on both central pawns.} 8...Qa6 9.c5 {The most logical move for White is probably c5, attacking the queen. We don't have to move the queen right now, since we can capture on f3, and if White goes for Bxa6, we can capture their queen.} 9...Bxf3 10.gxf3 {They'll capture on f3 instead and preserve the attack on our queen. Our queen seems to be trapped, but we have a strong defensive move with b5!} 10...b5 11.d5 {Pushing the pawn might look like a blunder, but it's not! We mustn't capture on d5 because of Nc3.} 11...Nd4 {But we can jump to the center instead. We'll aim to capture on c5 on the next move, as well as activate our knight to e7. All in all, an unclear position.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Move 3 Sidelines / Study 2: White Plays 4.Be2 and 5.d4 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/ab6b9c7c-f20c-455f-9511-458c625164d7/studies/752f4bca-51e5-4c49-a707-91e3cf0cdc37/lines"]

1.e4 {I want us to look at more 3.Nf3 lines. There's a less common variation, and that includes White playing 4.Be2 like in the previous study but also 5.d4, the direct central pawn break. We have to know how to fight such a simple and straightforward assault. Let's get things underway with 1.e4 d5.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White takes on d5. We'll recapture with the queen.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nf3 {White wants to develop the kingside first. I suggest we pin the knight right away.} 3...Bg4 4.Be2 {White continues with their plan, unpins the knight and has already developed their kingside pieces. It doesn't pose us any direct problems, though. We'll continue to develop our queenside.} 4...Nc6 5.d4 {5.d4 is played only 10% of the time here, but it might be the most instructive move. White simply puts a pawn in the center and wants to develop further. We'll go for our usual idea here and castle queenside.} 5...O-O-O 6.c3 {6.c3 defends the center, but is slow and gives exactly what we want. With ...e5, we strike directly at the white center.} 6...e5 7.Be3 {The bishop is required to come and help out with the defense of the center...} 7...Nge7 {But White will need to be careful after we develop the knight to e7. If our knight reaches the f5-square on the next move, White might get into trouble!} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Move 3 Sidelines / Study 2: White Plays 4.Be2 and 5.d4 / Variation 11"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/ab6b9c7c-f20c-455f-9511-458c625164d7/studies/752f4bca-51e5-4c49-a707-91e3cf0cdc37/lines"]

1.e4 {I want us to look at more 3.Nf3 lines. There's a less common variation, and that includes White playing 4.Be2 like in the previous study but also 5.d4, the direct central pawn break. We have to know how to fight such a simple and straightforward assault. Let's get things underway with 1.e4 d5.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White takes on d5. We'll recapture with the queen.} 2...Qxd5 3.Nf3 {White wants to develop the kingside first. I suggest we pin the knight right away.} 3...Bg4 4.Be2 {White continues with their plan, unpins the knight and has already developed their kingside pieces. It doesn't pose us any direct problems, though. We'll continue to develop our queenside.} 4...Nc6 5.d4 {5.d4 is played only 10% of the time here, but it might be the most instructive move. White simply puts a pawn in the center and wants to develop further. We'll go for our usual idea here and castle queenside.} 5...O-O-O 6.O-O {6.O-O might be blitzed out by some players. You can look at it either as a blunder or as a sacrifice of the d4-pawn! We take on f3.} 6...Bxf3 7.Bxf3 {White recaptures. And now we've got ...Qxd4 to win the central pawn.} 7...Qxd4 8.Qe2 {If they avoid the queen trade and slide to e2 instead...} 8...Qe5 {Black is better after ...Qe5, offering a trade again. We want to get rid of that bishop on f3 and have ...Nd4 in mind if White doesn't take on e5.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Move 3 Sidelines / Study 3: Other Move 3 Sidelines / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/ab6b9c7c-f20c-455f-9511-458c625164d7/studies/42ee52c3-7b70-4fc6-8412-b1a2b7e6faa9/lines"]

1.e4 {In the second study of Chapter 6, we'll see what White can play apart from Nf3 on move 3. You won't come across these moves as often as Nf3, but it's important to know how to deal with them, as our approach will change depending on what White plays sometimes. It's never good to just blitz out the same moves you learned independent of White's responses! Let's begin with the customary 1.e4 d5.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White takes the pawn. We'll recapture.} 2...Qxd5 3.d4 {3.d4 is played 8% of the time in this position. White doesn't activate either knight but rather places a pawn in the center. This allows for an interesting idea I want you to remember. We're going to directly strike at the white center with our e-pawn!} 3...e5 4.dxe5 {White will capture the pawn here 23% of the time, thinking we'll just recapture and they'll get some tempi. But this is an inaccuracy. We can exchange queens and misplace the white king.} 4...Qxd1+ 5.Kxd1 {White has to recapture. And our idea is to develop quickly with a serious initiative, not being in a rush to regain our pawn.} 5...Nc6 6.Bb5 {An active defense is White pinning our knight on c6 and threatening to disrupt our pawn structure. Now, defending with something like ...Bd7 is too passive, I'd say. Let's win some tempi instead, developing with check.} 6...Bg4+ 7.f3 {White can block with f3 and attack the bishop, but this doesn't mean we'll retreat. We can continue in the same vein and castle queenside, giving check.} 7...O-O-O+ 8.Bd2 {White has to react again. Now we'll bring our bishop back and guard the c6-knight.} 8...Bd7 9.Bxc6 {White will probably have to capture on c6 to get rid of the pressure on the e5-pawn.} 9...Bxc6 {We have the bishop pair and a lead in development, while the white king is still stuck in the center. We have a clear advantage, despite the pawn deficit.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Move 3 Sidelines / Study 3: Other Move 3 Sidelines / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/ab6b9c7c-f20c-455f-9511-458c625164d7/studies/42ee52c3-7b70-4fc6-8412-b1a2b7e6faa9/lines"]

1.e4 {In the second study of Chapter 6, we'll see what White can play apart from Nf3 on move 3. You won't come across these moves as often as Nf3, but it's important to know how to deal with them, as our approach will change depending on what White plays sometimes. It's never good to just blitz out the same moves you learned independent of White's responses! Let's begin with the customary 1.e4 d5.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White takes the pawn. We'll recapture.} 2...Qxd5 3.d4 {3.d4 is played 8% of the time in this position. White doesn't activate either knight but rather places a pawn in the center. This allows for an interesting idea I want you to remember. We're going to directly strike at the white center with our e-pawn!} 3...e5 4.dxe5 {White will capture the pawn here 23% of the time, thinking we'll just recapture and they'll get some tempi. But this is an inaccuracy. We can exchange queens and misplace the white king.} 4...Qxd1+ 5.Kxd1 {White has to recapture. And our idea is to develop quickly with a serious initiative, not being in a rush to regain our pawn.} 5...Nc6 6.Nf3 {White might blitz out Nf3 here, developing and defending the center. But that already plays into our hands. We'll develop and pin the defender of the e5-pawn. This threatens the pawn, threatens to capture on f3 and allows us to castle long on the next move!} 6...Bg4 7.Bf4 {White deals with the biggest problem, which is the loose pawn on e5. We'll castle long now, with check!} 7...O-O-O+ 8.Nd2 {The knight blocks, but we keep calling the shots. Let's develop the bishop with an attack on f2 now! The position plays itself - we develop and create threats, move after move.} 8...Bc5 9.Bg3 {It's probably best for White to retreat to g3...} 9...Nge7 {And we'll develop the knight next, with the idea of ...Nf5, attacking that bishop that holds everything together for White. It's extremely easy to play this position.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Move 3 Sidelines / Study 3: Other Move 3 Sidelines / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/ab6b9c7c-f20c-455f-9511-458c625164d7/studies/42ee52c3-7b70-4fc6-8412-b1a2b7e6faa9/lines"]

1.e4 {In the second study of Chapter 6, we'll see what White can play apart from Nf3 on move 3. You won't come across these moves as often as Nf3, but it's important to know how to deal with them, as our approach will change depending on what White plays sometimes. It's never good to just blitz out the same moves you learned independent of White's responses! Let's begin with the customary 1.e4 d5.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White takes the pawn. We'll recapture.} 2...Qxd5 3.d4 {3.d4 is played 8% of the time in this position. White doesn't activate either knight but rather places a pawn in the center. This allows for an interesting idea I want you to remember. We're going to directly strike at the white center with our e-pawn!} 3...e5 4.dxe5 {White will capture the pawn here 23% of the time, thinking we'll just recapture and they'll get some tempi. But this is an inaccuracy. We can exchange queens and misplace the white king.} 4...Qxd1+ 5.Kxd1 {White has to recapture. And our idea is to develop quickly with a serious initiative, not being in a rush to regain our pawn.} 5...Nc6 6.f4 {6.f4 might look scary, as White gets some space and protects the center with the pawn. But them not having any pieces developed yet might backfire. We can challenge that structure right away with ...f6.} 6...f6 7.exf6 {White takes.} 7...Nxf6 {And we'll recapture with the knight, having activated two pieces against White's none. The white king is stranded in the center of the board. Next, our bishops are coming to c5 and either f5 or g4, while our f6-knight can easily jump forward and threaten a fork on f2. Castling long and getting the rooks to the center is natural. Black is better.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Move 3 Sidelines / Study 3: Other Move 3 Sidelines / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/ab6b9c7c-f20c-455f-9511-458c625164d7/studies/42ee52c3-7b70-4fc6-8412-b1a2b7e6faa9/lines"]

1.e4 {In the second study of Chapter 6, we'll see what White can play apart from Nf3 on move 3. You won't come across these moves as often as Nf3, but it's important to know how to deal with them, as our approach will change depending on what White plays sometimes. It's never good to just blitz out the same moves you learned independent of White's responses! Let's begin with the customary 1.e4 d5.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White takes the pawn. We'll recapture.} 2...Qxd5 3.d4 {3.d4 is played 8% of the time in this position. White doesn't activate either knight but rather places a pawn in the center. This allows for an interesting idea I want you to remember. We're going to directly strike at the white center with our e-pawn!} 3...e5 4.Nf3 {4.Nf3 is the most popular move at this point, defending the center and attacking e5. I suggest we relieve the tension now and take on d4.} 4...exd4 5.Qxd4 {If White recaptures with the queen, it means they would really like to trade and play an endgame they can't be worse in. Let's try to keep the queens on.} 5...Qe6+ 6.Qe3 {If White blocks with the queen, we can't avoid the trade anymore. But we've got a small trick to accelerate our development.} 6...Bc5 7.Qxe6+ {White will most likely capture here.} 7...Bxe6 {We'll take back with the bishop. It might be boring for you to play a queenless middlegame, but if that's what White wants, this is the most we can draw out of it. A flexible position where we can castle either way. If anyone is better here, it's Black.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Move 3 Sidelines / Study 3: Other Move 3 Sidelines / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/ab6b9c7c-f20c-455f-9511-458c625164d7/studies/42ee52c3-7b70-4fc6-8412-b1a2b7e6faa9/lines"]

1.e4 {In the second study of Chapter 6, we'll see what White can play apart from Nf3 on move 3. You won't come across these moves as often as Nf3, but it's important to know how to deal with them, as our approach will change depending on what White plays sometimes. It's never good to just blitz out the same moves you learned independent of White's responses! Let's begin with the customary 1.e4 d5.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White takes the pawn. We'll recapture.} 2...Qxd5 3.d4 {3.d4 is played 8% of the time in this position. White doesn't activate either knight but rather places a pawn in the center. This allows for an interesting idea I want you to remember. We're going to directly strike at the white center with our e-pawn!} 3...e5 4.Nc3 {White could go for the peculiar-looking 4.Nc3, attacking our queen at this "inconvenient" moment. On the first glance, White simply gives up the d4-pawn, but it's not so easy. White will trade queens and then use the knight to get some compensation. This move is played 21% of the time.} 4...Qxd4 5.Qxd4 {White trades, and we recapture.} 5...exd4 6.Nb5 {They play Nb5, forking the pawn on d4 and the pawn on c7 and threatening a fork! If we just play a move like ...Kd8, there is Bf4 and we are in trouble. That's why trading the dark-squared bishops is key here. We insert a check on b4 before continuing.} 6...Bb4+ 7.Bd2 {Blocking is more confrontational, but we are happy to exchange bishops.} 7...Bxd2+ 8.Kxd2 {Once White recaptures...} 8...Kd8 {Now it's safe to go for ...Kd8 and defend against the fork. Next, we'll play ...c6 to kick the knight away if it doesn't take our central pawn, and activate our own knight to f6. From there, it can check the king on e4 and then reroute to the better d6-square. All in all, our king will be safe on the dark squares like c7 or e7 since the dark-squared bishops have been traded.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Move 3 Sidelines / Study 3: Other Move 3 Sidelines / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/ab6b9c7c-f20c-455f-9511-458c625164d7/studies/42ee52c3-7b70-4fc6-8412-b1a2b7e6faa9/lines"]

1.e4 {In the second study of Chapter 6, we'll see what White can play apart from Nf3 on move 3. You won't come across these moves as often as Nf3, but it's important to know how to deal with them, as our approach will change depending on what White plays sometimes. It's never good to just blitz out the same moves you learned independent of White's responses! Let's begin with the customary 1.e4 d5.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White takes the pawn. We'll recapture.} 2...Qxd5 3.d4 {3.d4 is played 8% of the time in this position. White doesn't activate either knight but rather places a pawn in the center. This allows for an interesting idea I want you to remember. We're going to directly strike at the white center with our e-pawn!} 3...e5 4.Nf3 {4.Nf3 is the most popular move at this point, defending the center and attacking e5. I suggest we relieve the tension now and take on d4.} 4...exd4 5.Nxd4 {If White recaptures with the knight, there's an interesting developing idea. We don't want to activate the knight to c6 immediately and offer a trade. Instead, we'll give a check on b4.} 5...Bb4+ 6.Nc3 {If White blocks with the knight, we've got nothing to worry about.} 6...Ne7 {We'll just get our knight to e7 to support ...Nbc6 next. Our queen and bishop are doing a great job on the diagonals, making it hard for White to develop.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Move 3 Sidelines / Study 3: Other Move 3 Sidelines / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/ab6b9c7c-f20c-455f-9511-458c625164d7/studies/42ee52c3-7b70-4fc6-8412-b1a2b7e6faa9/lines"]

1.e4 {In the second study of Chapter 6, we'll see what White can play apart from Nf3 on move 3. You won't come across these moves as often as Nf3, but it's important to know how to deal with them, as our approach will change depending on what White plays sometimes. It's never good to just blitz out the same moves you learned independent of White's responses! Let's begin with the customary 1.e4 d5.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White takes the pawn. We'll recapture.} 2...Qxd5 3.d4 {3.d4 is played 8% of the time in this position. White doesn't activate either knight but rather places a pawn in the center. This allows for an interesting idea I want you to remember. We're going to directly strike at the white center with our e-pawn!} 3...e5 4.Nf3 {4.Nf3 is the most popular move at this point, defending the center and attacking e5. I suggest we relieve the tension now and take on d4.} 4...exd4 5.Nxd4 {If White recaptures with the knight, there's an interesting developing idea. We don't want to activate the knight to c6 immediately and offer a trade. Instead, we'll give a check on b4.} 5...Bb4+ 6.c3 {It's logical to block with the pawn and attack the bishop.} 6...Bc5 {Let's retreat to c5. We attack the knight and would like to capture on d4 with the bishop if allowed. Next, we'll develop the knight to e7 and castle short.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Move 3 Sidelines / Study 3: Other Move 3 Sidelines / Variation 10"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/ab6b9c7c-f20c-455f-9511-458c625164d7/studies/42ee52c3-7b70-4fc6-8412-b1a2b7e6faa9/lines"]

1.e4 {In the second study of Chapter 6, we'll see what White can play apart from Nf3 on move 3. You won't come across these moves as often as Nf3, but it's important to know how to deal with them, as our approach will change depending on what White plays sometimes. It's never good to just blitz out the same moves you learned independent of White's responses! Let's begin with the customary 1.e4 d5.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White takes the pawn. We'll recapture.} 2...Qxd5 3.Qf3 {3.Qf3 is played only 1% of the time, but that is still almost 2 million games in the database! White would like to trade our queen off from the center and recapture with the knight. We'll keep the queens on.} 3...Qd6 4.Nc3 {White activates the queenside knight. We'll respond by developing as well.} 4...Nf6 5.Bc4 {Once the bishop gets out...} 5...Nc6 {It's important to develop the other knight to c6. With White's queen being in the center, this knight can often jump to d4 or e5 to create trouble. Also, White can't really play d4, so we'll go ...e5 at some point. All in all, 3.Qf3 is nothing to worry about.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Move 3 Sidelines / Study 3: Other Move 3 Sidelines / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/ab6b9c7c-f20c-455f-9511-458c625164d7/studies/42ee52c3-7b70-4fc6-8412-b1a2b7e6faa9/lines"]

1.e4 {In the second study of Chapter 6, we'll see what White can play apart from Nf3 on move 3. You won't come across these moves as often as Nf3, but it's important to know how to deal with them, as our approach will change depending on what White plays sometimes. It's never good to just blitz out the same moves you learned independent of White's responses! Let's begin with the customary 1.e4 d5.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White takes the pawn. We'll recapture.} 2...Qxd5 3.c4 {3.c4 is played 3% of the time, and the idea is to grab some central space while attacking our queen. Some players prefer this to Nc3. But it has its downsides, of course.} 3...Qe4+ {For example, we've got the e4-square now to give a check on, and White has to react already. This wouldn't have been possible after 3.Nc3. Now: 1. If 4.Be2, we take g2. 2. If 4.Ne2, we take c4. 3. If 4.Qe2, trade, and play a normal game, castling either way.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Move 3 Sidelines / Study 3: Other Move 3 Sidelines / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/ab6b9c7c-f20c-455f-9511-458c625164d7/studies/42ee52c3-7b70-4fc6-8412-b1a2b7e6faa9/lines"]

1.e4 {In the second study of Chapter 6, we'll see what White can play apart from Nf3 on move 3. You won't come across these moves as often as Nf3, but it's important to know how to deal with them, as our approach will change depending on what White plays sometimes. It's never good to just blitz out the same moves you learned independent of White's responses! Let's begin with the customary 1.e4 d5.} 1...d5 2.exd5 {White takes the pawn. We'll recapture.} 2...Qxd5 3.d4 {3.d4 is played 8% of the time in this position. White doesn't activate either knight but rather places a pawn in the center. This allows for an interesting idea I want you to remember. We're going to directly strike at the white center with our e-pawn!} 3...e5 4.Nf3 {4.Nf3 is the most popular move at this point, defending the center and attacking e5. I suggest we relieve the tension now and take on d4.} 4...exd4 5.Qxd4 {If White recaptures with the queen, it means they would really like to trade and play an endgame they can't be worse in. Let's try to keep the queens on.} 5...Qe6+ 6.Be3 {If White blocks with the bishop, that makes it easy for us to continue.} 6...Nc6 {We'll be the ones developing the knight and attacking the queen now. Our pieces will come to life on their natural squares soon after, and we'll castle long. A comfortable position for Black.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: Move 2 Sidelines / Study 1: Move 2 sidelines / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/6fc288f4-02b5-42c6-94d4-4f513c81b24a/studies/8d00ecb1-8843-4fd5-ad7f-688bcf997238/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the seventh chapter! We'll take a look at a few of the second moves that White might opt for instead of immediately capturing after the normal opening moves of 1.e4 d5.} 1...d5 2.d4 {White tries to grab central space and gambit a pawn with 2. d4, which is a bit more popular than 2. d3, having a 3% occurrence.} 2...dxe4 3.Nc3 {White develops. We can develop and fight for the e4-pawn for now.} 3...Nf6 4.f3 {White offers another pawn, so they can speed up their development. We won't help White. We'll develop and support the e-pawn.} 4...Bf5 5.fxe4 {White captures on e4. We take back with the knight.} 5...Nxe4 6.Qf3 {White brings their queen to f3. Both minor pieces are now attacked. We have to trade on c3.} 6...Nxc3 7.bxc3 {White recaptures, keeping the bishop and the b7-pawn under pressure. With ...e6, Black supports the bishop on f5 and opens up the other bishop.} 7...e6 8.Qxb7 {White grabs the queenside pawn. ...Nd7 is mandatory now.} 8...Nd7 9.Bb5 {White pins our d7-knight, intending to capture it and force us to take back with the king. Let's kick away the queen.} 9...Rb8 10.Bxd7+ {Looks like we have to move our king out, since the rook would be left hanging if we recaptured with the queen.} 10...Kxd7 11.Qf3 {The queen retreats. We can force a queen trade, which is definitely beneficial for us.} 11...Qh4+ 12.g3 {White blocks.} 12...Qe4+ {The queen has some scope on e4!} *

[Event "Chapter 7: Move 2 Sidelines / Study 1: Move 2 sidelines / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/6fc288f4-02b5-42c6-94d4-4f513c81b24a/studies/8d00ecb1-8843-4fd5-ad7f-688bcf997238/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the seventh chapter! We'll take a look at a few of the second moves that White might opt for instead of immediately capturing after the normal opening moves of 1.e4 d5.} 1...d5 2.d4 {White tries to grab central space and gambit a pawn with 2. d4, which is a bit more popular than 2. d3, having a 3% occurrence.} 2...dxe4 3.Nc3 {White develops. We can develop and fight for the e4-pawn for now.} 3...Nf6 4.f3 {White offers another pawn, so they can speed up their development. We won't help White. We'll develop and support the e-pawn.} 4...Bf5 5.fxe4 {White captures on e4. We take back with the knight.} 5...Nxe4 6.Qf3 {White brings their queen to f3. Both minor pieces are now attacked. We have to trade on c3.} 6...Nxc3 7.bxc3 {White recaptures, keeping the bishop and the b7-pawn under pressure. With ...e6, Black supports the bishop on f5 and opens up the other bishop.} 7...e6 8.Qxb7 {White grabs the queenside pawn. ...Nd7 is mandatory now.} 8...Nd7 9.Nf3 {White develops. Let's ask the queen on b7 a question.} 9...Rb8 10.Qxa7 {White grabs another pawn.} 10...Bxc2 {We do as well. Black is once again doing very well. We've got the open b-file and easy development for our kingside.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: Move 2 Sidelines / Study 1: Move 2 sidelines / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/6fc288f4-02b5-42c6-94d4-4f513c81b24a/studies/8d00ecb1-8843-4fd5-ad7f-688bcf997238/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the seventh chapter! We'll take a look at a few of the second moves that White might opt for instead of immediately capturing after the normal opening moves of 1.e4 d5.} 1...d5 2.d3 {White instead supports the e4-pawn with d3, which isn't a very popular move (2% of games), but it isn't illogical either. We can enter a safe endgame with 2...dxe4, but I want to recommend a more fighting approach as well.} 2...e5 3.Nf3 {White attacks the e5-pawn insead of trading. Let's defend it.} 3...Nc6 4.Nbd2 {White is simply developing. We'll later want to push the pawn to f6 to solidify the center, so ...Ne7 is logical.} 4...Nge7 5.g3 {White prepares to fianchetto.} 5...f6 6.Bg2 {White fianchettoes.} 6...Bg4 {And we prepare to castle queenside. Our center looks very good!} *

[Event "Chapter 7: Move 2 Sidelines / Study 1: Move 2 sidelines / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/6fc288f4-02b5-42c6-94d4-4f513c81b24a/studies/8d00ecb1-8843-4fd5-ad7f-688bcf997238/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the seventh chapter! We'll take a look at a few of the second moves that White might opt for instead of immediately capturing after the normal opening moves of 1.e4 d5.} 1...d5 2.d4 {White tries to grab central space and gambit a pawn with 2. d4, which is a bit more popular than 2. d3, having a 3% occurrence.} 2...dxe4 3.Nc3 {White develops. We can develop and fight for the e4-pawn for now.} 3...Nf6 4.f3 {White offers another pawn, so they can speed up their development. We won't help White. We'll develop and support the e-pawn.} 4...Bf5 5.fxe4 {White captures on e4. We take back with the knight.} 5...Nxe4 6.Qf3 {White brings their queen to f3. Both minor pieces are now attacked. We have to trade on c3.} 6...Nxc3 7.Qxf5 {If White grabs the bishop...} 7...e6 {...e6 with ...Nd5 next gives Black a comfortable position. White has some practical compensation in the form of open files but we have an extra pawn and aren't even behind in development.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: Move 2 Sidelines / Study 1: Move 2 sidelines / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/6fc288f4-02b5-42c6-94d4-4f513c81b24a/studies/8d00ecb1-8843-4fd5-ad7f-688bcf997238/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the seventh chapter! We'll take a look at a few of the second moves that White might opt for instead of immediately capturing after the normal opening moves of 1.e4 d5.} 1...d5 2.d4 {White tries to grab central space and gambit a pawn with 2. d4, which is a bit more popular than 2. d3, having a 3% occurrence.} 2...dxe4 3.Nc3 {White develops. We can develop and fight for the e4-pawn for now.} 3...Nf6 4.f3 {White offers another pawn, so they can speed up their development. We won't help White. We'll develop and support the e-pawn.} 4...Bf5 5.g4 {White could attack our bishop instead of capturing on e4. We'll retreat to g6, maintaining the connection to the e4-pawn.} 5...Bg6 6.g5 {White just keeps on expanding on the queenside. Now our knight is driven away from the defense of e4. We'll bring it into the center of the board.} 6...Nd5 7.Nxe4 {White captures on e4.} 7...Nc6 {And we answer with development. We've given up our pawn back, but White has weakened their position with pawn pushes. Next up we can explode the center with the aggressive ...e5 or continue with the prosaic ...e6.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: Move 2 Sidelines / Study 1: Move 2 sidelines / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/6fc288f4-02b5-42c6-94d4-4f513c81b24a/studies/8d00ecb1-8843-4fd5-ad7f-688bcf997238/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the seventh chapter! We'll take a look at a few of the second moves that White might opt for instead of immediately capturing after the normal opening moves of 1.e4 d5.} 1...d5 2.Nf3 {White goes for this pawn sacrifice, intending to cause some trouble with the knight. Doesn't look very threatening, but it happens in 8% of the games!} 2...dxe4 3.Ng5 {White wants to grab the pawn back, so let's protect it!} 3...Bf5 4.Nc3 {White attacks it yet again. Let's keep defending and developing.} 4...Nf6 5.Bc4 {White attacks the f7-pawn. We are content to block the attack and play e6, preparing further development.} 5...e6 6.Qe2 {White attacks e4. We can keep protecting the d4-pawn by centralizing our queen.} 6...Qd4 7.Nb5 {White attacks the queen.} 7...Qb6 {The queen just retreats. We keep the diagonal and White's knights aren't doing anything special. We can kick them back with our pawns if we feel the need.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: Move 2 Sidelines / Study 1: Move 2 sidelines / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/6fc288f4-02b5-42c6-94d4-4f513c81b24a/studies/8d00ecb1-8843-4fd5-ad7f-688bcf997238/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the seventh chapter! We'll take a look at a few of the second moves that White might opt for instead of immediately capturing after the normal opening moves of 1.e4 d5.} 1...d5 2.Nf3 {White goes for this pawn sacrifice, intending to cause some trouble with the knight. Doesn't look very threatening, but it happens in 8% of the games!} 2...dxe4 3.Ng5 {White wants to grab the pawn back, so let's protect it!} 3...Bf5 4.Nc3 {White attacks it yet again. Let's keep defending and developing.} 4...Nf6 5.d3 {White offers the d-pawn. Giving it up on e4 wouldn't be the best idea here, so we'll just trade.} 5...exd3 6.Qf3 {White once again uses the Qf3 idea. With ...Qc8, we protect both the b7-pawn and the bishop.} 6...Qc8 7.Bxd3 {White recaptures on d3. We may as well trade bishops.} 7...Bxd3 8.Qxd3 {White recaptures.} 8...h6 {We'll attack the knight. Black is doing great here. White is down a pawn for nothing.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: Move 2 Sidelines / Study 1: Move 2 sidelines / Variation 10"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/6fc288f4-02b5-42c6-94d4-4f513c81b24a/studies/8d00ecb1-8843-4fd5-ad7f-688bcf997238/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the seventh chapter! We'll take a look at a few of the second moves that White might opt for instead of immediately capturing after the normal opening moves of 1.e4 d5.} 1...d5 2.Nf3 {White goes for this pawn sacrifice, intending to cause some trouble with the knight. Doesn't look very threatening, but it happens in 8% of the games!} 2...dxe4 3.Ng5 {White wants to grab the pawn back, so let's protect it!} 3...Bf5 4.f3 {White pushes f3 to attack e4. We can safely grab on f3 now.} 4...exf3 5.Qxf3 {The queen recaptures, hitting f5 and b7. We'll once again carry out this idea from previous moves — sacrificing the b7-pawn for development.} 5...e6 6.Qxb7 {White captures on b7.} 6...Nd7 {With ...Rd8 Black is once again doing very well here with a lot of developing options.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: Move 2 Sidelines / Study 1: Move 2 sidelines / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/6fc288f4-02b5-42c6-94d4-4f513c81b24a/studies/8d00ecb1-8843-4fd5-ad7f-688bcf997238/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the seventh chapter! We'll take a look at a few of the second moves that White might opt for instead of immediately capturing after the normal opening moves of 1.e4 d5.} 1...d5 2.Nf3 {White goes for this pawn sacrifice, intending to cause some trouble with the knight. Doesn't look very threatening, but it happens in 8% of the games!} 2...dxe4 3.Ng5 {White wants to grab the pawn back, so let's protect it!} 3...Bf5 4.Bc4 {White goes after the f7-pawn. Blocking the diagonal with e6 is natural and good. The g5-knight is now en prise.} 4...e6 5.d3 {White protects it.} 5...exd3 {We can take on d3 and simply continue developing. White's pieces aren't doing anything to our position, and we can get our pieces out quickly, with ...Nc6 coming.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: Move 2 Sidelines / Study 1: Move 2 sidelines / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/6fc288f4-02b5-42c6-94d4-4f513c81b24a/studies/8d00ecb1-8843-4fd5-ad7f-688bcf997238/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the seventh chapter! We'll take a look at a few of the second moves that White might opt for instead of immediately capturing after the normal opening moves of 1.e4 d5.} 1...d5 2.d3 {White instead supports the e4-pawn with d3, which isn't a very popular move (2% of games), but it isn't illogical either. We can enter a safe endgame with 2...dxe4, but I want to recommend a more fighting approach as well.} 2...e5 3.exd5 {White trades. We'll recapture and get the queen out.} 3...Qxd5 4.Nc3 {The knight can attack our queen, but we can pin it by developing the bishop.} 4...Bb4 5.Bd2 {White breaks the pin. Let's get rid of the knight to keep our queen in the center.} 5...Bxc3 6.Bxc3 {White recaptures.} 6...Nc6 {And with ...Nc6 we start to develop our minor pieces. We can decide if we castle kingside or queenside.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: Move 2 Sidelines / Study 2: White Plays 2.Nc3 and 2.e5 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/6fc288f4-02b5-42c6-94d4-4f513c81b24a/studies/2794c859-dbcb-4b24-a31d-10da98300c46/lines"]

1.e4 {Finally, I want to conclude with two moves that aren't gambits. 2.Nc3 and 2.e5 give rise to normal positions, but against both of them we can get good development.} 1...d5 2.e5 {White chooses to expand in the center with e5 and intends to go d4. We can see this in 8% of games. Our goal is to make d4 harder for White to play.} 2...c5 3.d4 {This move isn't the best, but it's the most popular here, being played nearly 27% of the time. We'll capture because we can gain a tempo on the queen.} 3...cxd4 4.Qxd4 {White recaptures. We can now develop with a tempo.} 4...Nc6 5.Bb5 {White will usually pin us. In this case, I suggest we merely continue with development, starting with the light-squared bishop as we want to push ...e6 later.} 5...Bf5 6.Bxc6+ {The most popular continuation involves White taking on c6.} 6...bxc6 7.Nf3 {White develops their knight. And now it's time to complete the strong pawn structure.} 7...e6 8.O-O {Even though they can castle, we can make use of our center pawns.} 8...c5 9.Qa4+ {And since there isn't a light-squared bishop, we will block with the queen.} 9...Qd7 10.Qxd7+ {White exchanges queens on d7, and we recapture.} 10...Kxd7 11.Nc3 {Many people may just forget about c2. This is a common blunder.} 11...Bxc2 {Since it's safe and there are no queens on the board, we can take. Black should win this.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: Move 2 Sidelines / Study 2: White Plays 2.Nc3 and 2.e5 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/6fc288f4-02b5-42c6-94d4-4f513c81b24a/studies/2794c859-dbcb-4b24-a31d-10da98300c46/lines"]

1.e4 {Finally, I want to conclude with two moves that aren't gambits. 2.Nc3 and 2.e5 give rise to normal positions, but against both of them we can get good development.} 1...d5 2.e5 {White chooses to expand in the center with e5 and intends to go d4. We can see this in 8% of games. Our goal is to make d4 harder for White to play.} 2...c5 3.d4 {This move isn't the best, but it's the most popular here, being played nearly 27% of the time. We'll capture because we can gain a tempo on the queen.} 3...cxd4 4.Qxd4 {White recaptures. We can now develop with a tempo.} 4...Nc6 5.Bb5 {White will usually pin us. In this case, I suggest we merely continue with development, starting with the light-squared bishop as we want to push ...e6 later.} 5...Bf5 6.Nc3 {White could simply develop and attack d5. We'll support d5 with the e-pawn.} 6...e6 7.Nf3 {White develops. Even though c2 hangs, we want to develop, too.} 7...Ne7 {That's why we'll support our knight. Black's advantage is starting to become palpable. ...a6 will come with a tempo on the queen.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: Move 2 Sidelines / Study 2: White Plays 2.Nc3 and 2.e5 / Variation 11"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/6fc288f4-02b5-42c6-94d4-4f513c81b24a/studies/2794c859-dbcb-4b24-a31d-10da98300c46/lines"]

1.e4 {Finally, I want to conclude with two moves that aren't gambits. 2.Nc3 and 2.e5 give rise to normal positions, but against both of them we can get good development.} 1...d5 2.e5 {White chooses to expand in the center with e5 and intends to go d4. We can see this in 8% of games. Our goal is to make d4 harder for White to play.} 2...c5 3.c3 {This move has happened the most in the games of masters, but amateurs play it less often (around 17% of times). We'll get the knight out. It adds control over the d4-square.} 3...Nc6 4.d4 {White expands in the center. Before ...e6, the bishop should come out.} 4...Bf5 5.Nf3 {White develops. And now we can go ahead and play ...e6.} 5...e6 6.Be2 {White prepares to castle. By trading on d4, we make a route for the dark-squared bishop.} 6...cxd4 7.cxd4 {White recaptures.} 7...Bb4+ {With ...Bb4+ we develop the bishop. We plan to bring the knight to e7, and the rook to c8 to occupy the c-file. The d4-pawn could also potentially become a target.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: Move 2 Sidelines / Study 2: White Plays 2.Nc3 and 2.e5 / Variation 10"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/6fc288f4-02b5-42c6-94d4-4f513c81b24a/studies/2794c859-dbcb-4b24-a31d-10da98300c46/lines"]

1.e4 {Finally, I want to conclude with two moves that aren't gambits. 2.Nc3 and 2.e5 give rise to normal positions, but against both of them we can get good development.} 1...d5 2.e5 {White chooses to expand in the center with e5 and intends to go d4. We can see this in 8% of games. Our goal is to make d4 harder for White to play.} 2...c5 3.f4 {3.f4 aims to defend the e5-pawn. We'll simply develop our knight, since playing ...c5 has given us the c6-square to work with.} 3...Nc6 4.Nf3 {White brings their knight out. The f5-square is waiting for our bishop.} 4...Bf5 5.Bb5 {White pins our knight. But even though this pin is a popular idea, many times it'll simply give us the bishop pair after we attack the bishop.} 5...Qb6 6.Bxc6+ {White takes. We'll recapture with the queen.} 6...Qxc6 7.O-O {White castles. Let's complete the central pawn structure.} 7...e6 8.d3 {The pawn goes to d3, controlling e4 and c4 and blunting our bishop. It's White's best attempt to develop normally. We'll develop to e7, intending ...Ng6.} 8...Ne7 9.Nc3 {White brings their knight out.} 9...Ng6 {We have the bishop pair and we'll castle kingside. Do keep an eye out for h3 ideas to trap your bishop: in that case, ...h5 is a good move to prevent this plan. Otherwise, you should feel content about the opening.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: Move 2 Sidelines / Study 2: White Plays 2.Nc3 and 2.e5 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/6fc288f4-02b5-42c6-94d4-4f513c81b24a/studies/2794c859-dbcb-4b24-a31d-10da98300c46/lines"]

1.e4 {Finally, I want to conclude with two moves that aren't gambits. 2.Nc3 and 2.e5 give rise to normal positions, but against both of them we can get good development.} 1...d5 2.Nc3 {White immediately brings out the queenside knight and protects e4. Since 2. exd5 is the most popular, we only see 2. Nc3 6% of the time. We'll lure out the knight by trading on e4, though pushing the d-pawn forward is a legitimate alternative.} 2...dxe4 3.Nxe4 {White recaptures. Let's start developing.} 3...Nf6 4.Nxf6+ {White trades. We'll recapture with the e-pawn.} 4...exf6 5.d4 {White grabs space in the center. We can now bring the bishop out to the h2-b8 diagonal and prepare to castle.} 5...Bd6 6.Nf3 {White goes for standard development. We'll castle.} 6...O-O 7.Be2 {White wants to castle as well. Now it's time to occupy the e-file.} 7...Re8 8.O-O {The king gets to safety. The knight can be rerouted via d7 and f8.} 8...Nd7 9.c4 {White expands.} 9...b6 {We prepare to fianchetto and fight for the c5-square. We'll continue with ...Nf8 to g6 to improve the knight.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: Move 2 Sidelines / Study 2: White Plays 2.Nc3 and 2.e5 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/6fc288f4-02b5-42c6-94d4-4f513c81b24a/studies/2794c859-dbcb-4b24-a31d-10da98300c46/lines"]

1.e4 {Finally, I want to conclude with two moves that aren't gambits. 2.Nc3 and 2.e5 give rise to normal positions, but against both of them we can get good development.} 1...d5 2.Nc3 {White immediately brings out the queenside knight and protects e4. Since 2. exd5 is the most popular, we only see 2. Nc3 6% of the time. We'll lure out the knight by trading on e4, though pushing the d-pawn forward is a legitimate alternative.} 2...dxe4 3.Nxe4 {White recaptures. Let's start developing.} 3...Nf6 4.Ng3 {White retreats instead of trading. A common response whenever a knight appears on g3 (or g6) is to push the h-pawn.} 4...h5 5.h4 {White stops our advance. Let's develop a piece with tempo.} 5...Bg4 6.Be2 {White offers a trade.} 6...c5 {With ...c5 we fight for the d4-square. With ...Nc6 and ...Qc7 we can plan to castle queenside!} *

[Event "Chapter 7: Move 2 Sidelines / Study 2: White Plays 2.Nc3 and 2.e5 / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/6fc288f4-02b5-42c6-94d4-4f513c81b24a/studies/2794c859-dbcb-4b24-a31d-10da98300c46/lines"]

1.e4 {Finally, I want to conclude with two moves that aren't gambits. 2.Nc3 and 2.e5 give rise to normal positions, but against both of them we can get good development.} 1...d5 2.e5 {White chooses to expand in the center with e5 and intends to go d4. We can see this in 8% of games. Our goal is to make d4 harder for White to play.} 2...c5 3.Nf3 {With the second most popular move (played nearly 22% in the database), White adds control to the d4-square by developing the knight to f3, also supporting the e5-pawn. By pinning the knight we fight for the d4-square.} 3...Bg4 4.h3 {White attacks our bishop. We can remove the knight which fights for the center.} 4...Bxf3 5.Qxf3 {White recaptures.} 5...a6 {We prevent Bb5, and prepare to play ...Nc6 and ...e6. The d4-square is ours!} *

[Event "Chapter 7: Move 2 Sidelines / Study 2: White Plays 2.Nc3 and 2.e5 / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/6fc288f4-02b5-42c6-94d4-4f513c81b24a/studies/2794c859-dbcb-4b24-a31d-10da98300c46/lines"]

1.e4 {Finally, I want to conclude with two moves that aren't gambits. 2.Nc3 and 2.e5 give rise to normal positions, but against both of them we can get good development.} 1...d5 2.e5 {White chooses to expand in the center with e5 and intends to go d4. We can see this in 8% of games. Our goal is to make d4 harder for White to play.} 2...c5 3.Nf3 {With the second most popular move (played nearly 22% in the database), White adds control to the d4-square by developing the knight to f3, also supporting the e5-pawn. By pinning the knight we fight for the d4-square.} 3...Bg4 4.Be2 {If White breaks the pin...} 4...Nc6 {...both the e5-pawn and the d4-square become weak after ...Nc6.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: Move 2 Sidelines / Study 2: White Plays 2.Nc3 and 2.e5 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/6fc288f4-02b5-42c6-94d4-4f513c81b24a/studies/2794c859-dbcb-4b24-a31d-10da98300c46/lines"]

1.e4 {Finally, I want to conclude with two moves that aren't gambits. 2.Nc3 and 2.e5 give rise to normal positions, but against both of them we can get good development.} 1...d5 2.Nc3 {White immediately brings out the queenside knight and protects e4. Since 2. exd5 is the most popular, we only see 2. Nc3 6% of the time. We'll lure out the knight by trading on e4, though pushing the d-pawn forward is a legitimate alternative.} 2...d4 3.Nce2 {The knight retreats. Let's expand in the center now.} 3...e5 4.Ng3 {White is trying to develop. By bringing the bishop to e6 we'll also prevent Bc4.} 4...Be6 5.Nf3 {The knight jumps to f3. We've got a nice way of creating a strong pawn chain.} 5...f6 6.Bb5+ {White develops the bishop. We'll block and attack it with c6.} 6...c6 7.Ba4 {The bishop retreats.} 7...h5 {The center is closed, so we can expand on the kingside now!} *

[Event "Chapter 7: Move 2 Sidelines / Study 2: White Plays 2.Nc3 and 2.e5 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/6fc288f4-02b5-42c6-94d4-4f513c81b24a/studies/2794c859-dbcb-4b24-a31d-10da98300c46/lines"]

1.e4 {Finally, I want to conclude with two moves that aren't gambits. 2.Nc3 and 2.e5 give rise to normal positions, but against both of them we can get good development.} 1...d5 2.Nc3 {White immediately brings out the queenside knight and protects e4. Since 2. exd5 is the most popular, we only see 2. Nc3 6% of the time. We'll lure out the knight by trading on e4, though pushing the d-pawn forward is a legitimate alternative.} 2...d4 3.Nce2 {The knight retreats. Let's expand in the center now.} 3...e5 4.Ng3 {White is trying to develop. By bringing the bishop to e6 we'll also prevent Bc4.} 4...Be6 5.Nf3 {The knight jumps to f3. We've got a nice way of creating a strong pawn chain.} 5...f6 6.d3 {White might try to open up the dark-squared bishop, but the f1-bishop suffers. Let's further support our central structure.} 6...c5 7.Be2 {White prepares to castle. Our next move ...g6 will nicely restrict White's knights.} 7...g6 8.O-O {The king hides.} 8...Nc6 {We continue development. Our center is perfect and we have more space for our pieces.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: Move 2 Sidelines / Study 2: White Plays 2.Nc3 and 2.e5 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/d37755a0-252e-4925-8c5f-2d2f62d6a2ab/chapters/6fc288f4-02b5-42c6-94d4-4f513c81b24a/studies/2794c859-dbcb-4b24-a31d-10da98300c46/lines"]

1.e4 {Finally, I want to conclude with two moves that aren't gambits. 2.Nc3 and 2.e5 give rise to normal positions, but against both of them we can get good development.} 1...d5 2.Nc3 {White immediately brings out the queenside knight and protects e4. Since 2. exd5 is the most popular, we only see 2. Nc3 6% of the time. We'll lure out the knight by trading on e4, though pushing the d-pawn forward is a legitimate alternative.} 2...dxe4 3.Nxe4 {White recaptures. Let's start developing.} 3...Nf6 4.Nxf6+ {White trades. We'll recapture with the e-pawn.} 4...exf6 5.d4 {White grabs space in the center. We can now bring the bishop out to the h2-b8 diagonal and prepare to castle.} 5...Bd6 6.Bd3 {White develops as well. We castle.} 6...O-O 7.Ne2 {White wants to castle. Let's occupy the e-file.} 7...Re8 8.O-O {White gets their king out of the center. We'll develop the bishop and put pressure on the knight.} 8...Bg4 9.f3 {White attacks the bishop. We'll retreat.} 9...Bh5 10.c3 {White supports the d4-pawn. We can now try to chip away at White's center.} 10...c5 11.dxc5 {White trades.} 11...Bxc5+ 12.Kh1 {The king steps away.} 12...Nc6 {We'll develop the final piece. Black is doing very well here. We plan to activate the queen and connect the rooks. Our pieces are much more active than White's.} *